1 OCCU Colonel, Surveyor AFT 1730 Charlotte Co., VA
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for Virginia.  [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A081658  DAR  Ancestor #: A081839] 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/210217742/joseph-morton
2 CONT
2 CONT Birth 	27 Dec 1709 Henrico County, Virginia, USA
2 CONT Death 	28 Jun 1772 (aged 62) Charlotte County, Virginia, USA
2 CONT Burial 	Cool Spring Cemetery Lunenburg County, Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT According to Bradshaw, Joseph Morton was born in Henrico County on December 27, 1709. He came at an early age to Amelia County as a marksman for the Randolph's. As a marksman, he sought out desirable lands and recommended them for
2 CONC  patenting. He also selected lands for himself and acquired 5553 acres in the wilderness for his employers. He requested that a road be built from Bush River to Buffalo in 1738 and was made surveyor of this job when the request wa
2 CONC s granted. On March 26, 1739 he acquired a patent on 1200 acres on the Briery River, a few miles from the present location of Hampden-Sydney College. This's land was originally in Amelia County but is now Prince Edward County. Jos
2 CONC eph and Agnes lived at this plantation for about 16 years. In 1754, when Prince Edward County was formed, Joseph was one of the first Justices. He and his family lived at Slate Hill at the time. He was defeated for the House of Bu
2 CONC rgesses and was also a vestryman at Nottoway Parish at the time.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph moved is family to Little Roanoke Bridge in Lunenburg County about 1755. This was frontier country and the nearest neighbor was thirty miles away. In 1765, this plantation fell into Charlotte County as it was formed out of 
2 CONC Lunenburg County. He was named one of the first Justices for Charlotte County. He lived out is life at this home.
2 CONT
2 CONT His former home, Slate Hill, was now occupied by his daughter and her husband, Nathaniel Venable. They lived there when the Presbytery met in the office which stood in the yard of Slate Hill and made plans which resulted in the bu
2 CONC ilding of Hampden-Sydney College. Joseph was active in the founding of this college and in helping to raise funds for it. While the original house is gone, the office now sits on the campus of Hampden-Sydney College.
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph was Justice of the Peace in Charlotte County in 1769. He was a member of the Committee of Safety for the county during the Revolutionary War. He and Agnes furnished supplies to the American Army during the war. All of their
2 CONC  son's served as officers in the American Army during the war. DAR Ancestor #: A081839
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/221141259/mary-morton
2 CONT
2 CONT JOSEPH MORTON OF LITTLE ROANOKE BRIDGE. Joseph Morton, b. 1709, d. June 28, 1772; m. (I) Miss-- Goode, m. (2) Agnes Woodson, b. February 27, I711, d. March 10, 1802, dau. Richard Woodson and wife Agnes Micheaux, grand-daughter Abr
2 CONC aham Micheaux and wife Susannah Rochette. Joseph Morton settled at Little Roanoke Bridge. By profession he was a surveyor. At first a staunch Episcopalian, subsequently became a staunch Presbyterian and remained so the rest of his
2 CONC  life. He became an Elder in the Briery Church. Was gentleman Justice of Prince Edward county, Va., when county was organized January 8, I754. Said to have come from Orange county, Va., and to have been employed by the Randolph fa
2 CONC mily to survey their lands. A man named John Morton is said to have been asso- ciated with Joseph Morton in surveying. What relation existed between the two men is not known. John Morton m. Elizabeth Woodson, dau. Sanborne Woodson
2 CONC . Children Ist marriage: Morton: i i. Mary, b. November, I 733. Children 2nd marriage: Morton: 12. Josiah, b. November 14, 1737, d. November 27, 1785. 13. Judith, b. December 27, I739. 14. William, b. November 27, 1743, d. Novembe
2 CONC r 29, 1820, m. October 29, 1764, Susannah Watkins. I5. Jane, b. February 14, 1745. I6. Agnes, b. February 26, 1747. p. 315
2 CONT
2 CONT 17. Little Joe, b. November 15, 1749. 18. Jacob, b. January 29, 1751, d. May 22, 1829, m. September 3, 1774, Jane D. Booker. I9. Elizabeth, b. May 14, 1754. NOTE.-This lineage I have not verified by documentary proof. D. M. p. 316
2 CONT
2 CONT The Morton Patriarchs of Virginia by Daniel Morton, The Virginia Magazine of History and Biography, Vol. 17, No. 3 (Jul., 1909), pp. 311-316, Virginia Historical Society
