1 OCCU US and Confederate politician
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Watkins_Venable : ''Confederate Congressman''
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham Watkins Venable (October 17, 1799 – February 24, 1876) was a nineteenth century US politician and lawyer from North Carolina. He was the nephew of congressman and senator Abraham Bedford Venable.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Springfield, Virginia, he graduated from Hampden-Sydney College. He studied medicine for two years before turning to law. He later graduated from Princeton University in 1819 and was admitted to the bar in 1821. He practic
2 CONC ed law in Virginia in both Prince Edward and Mecklenburg counties until 1829 when he moved to North Carolina. He later got involved politics and was elected to the thirtieth congress as a Democrat, serving from 1847 to 1853, unsuc
2 CONC cessful for reeletion in 1852. He was a presidential elector in the 1860 presidential election on the Democratic ticket for John C. Breckinridge and Joseph Lane. When his state succeeded from the Union, he went with it to the Conf
2 CONC ederacy and was elected to the Provisional Confederate Congress. He was later elected to the '''first Confederate congress, serving from 1862 to 1864'''. He died in Oxford, North Carolina in 1876 and was interred at Shiloh Presbyt
2 CONC erian Churchyard in Granville County, North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1860,  A. W. Venable owned 43 Slaves
2 CONT RESEARCH NOTES:	
2 CONT [1] Possibly close to 1,000 Acres
2 CONT
2 CONT [2] The Census returns for Granville Co. 1830-1850
2 CONT
2 CONT [3] Post Office Opened July 1 1806 - Closed March 1 1815 - Thomas Brown Post Master - Reopened June 26 1818 - Closed Dec 6, 1866 - Mavis Fuller P.M. - Reopened Dec 18 – 1867 - Closed March 15, 1905 Caroline H. Clark P.M. [From Pos
2 CONC tal Records]
2 CONT
2 CONT [4]  'ENSLAVED ANCESTORS ABSTRACTED FROM DEED BOOKS, GRANVILLE COUNTY, NORTH CAROLINA DEED BOOKS A-Z & 1-21 1746-1864'
2 CONT
2 CONT [5] Shilo Cemetery is 100 feet from the road. There are some marked graves there although the local people say its been a hundred years since anyone has been buried there. It is fenced in and covers an area about 100 Ft. Sq. The c
2 CONC emetery is somewhat over grown now but some one has made some effort to keep it from growing up completely. It is located 1/2 mile from Grassy Creek Church 4 miles west of Bullock NC.
2 CONT
2 CONT MISCELLANEOUS:	 A. W. Venable Papers 1847-1872, Wilson Library, UNC CH;  Abraham Watkins Venable Biography (1799-1876); Brownsville, NC Gazetteer
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Close friend with John C. Calhoun.
