1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''John Benjamin Watkins'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_B._Watkins
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/9340630/john_benjamin_watkins
2 CONT * https://books.google.com/books/about/The_Family_of_John_Benjamin_Watkins_and.html?id=egLQzgEACAAJ
2 CONT
2 CONT Watkins was a Democratic politician who served as a member of the Virginia Senate twice, first representing the state's 16th district, and a second time representing the 35th district, comprising Henrico, Chesterfield, New Kent, C
2 CONC harles City, and James City Counties and also the City of Williamsburg ''(which he held at the time of his death).'' A businessman, Senator Watkins started a nursery with his brother in Midlothian, Virginia. John B. Watkins is the
2 CONC  great-grandfather of Virginia State Senator John Watkins, who represents some of the same localities in the current Virginia Senate as well as continuing to operate the family nursery business.
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT J. B. Watkins was born on his father's farm in the lower end of Powhatan County, June 14th, 1855. After studying in the private schools near his home, he became in 1873 a student at Richmond College. Later he returned to his fathe
2 CONC r's farm and with his father carried on agricultural work. Thirty or more years ago, he began the nursery business, growing at first only fruit trees; later, however, as the business developed, shade trees, ornamental shrubs and e
2 CONC vergreens have been grown. The nursery has proved a very profitable enterprise and the business has greatly increased in the last few years.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1879, Mr. Watkins was married to Miss Lelia N. Michaux, of Michaux, Powhatan County, Virginia. Of the ten children born to them, eight are still living. Two of the sons are graduates of the Virginia Polytechnic Institute.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the nineties Mr. Watkins was elected for a number of years president of the Virginia State Fair Association, of Richmond. He was elected also, in 1907, to the Senate of Virginia from the Sixteenth Senatorial District, served fo
2 CONC ur years and was reelected in 1911. He was appointed by Governor Montague a member of the board of visitors of the Virginia Polytechnic Institute and served six or seven years prior to 1908. He was then appointed by Governor Swans
2 CONC on on the board of the State Normal School at Farmville. In June, 1912, he was reappointed to the Virginia Polytechnic Institute board of visitors by Governor Mann. He is also president of the Chesterfield County Agricultural Asso
2 CONC ciation.
