1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Born at at "Summer Grove," Truro Parish, Fairfax County, Virginia, which he, his children, grandchildren and great-grandchildren often returned to visit well into the late 19th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Francis and Jane Watkins Summers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary: Kanawa ENQUIRER, 31 Jan 1818, page 3.
2 CONT
2 CONT George W. Summers acquired Walnut Grove Plantation about 1811.  Walnut Grove was located near present-day Winfield, West Virginia, in Putnam County.  The original family home was a log structure built around 1812, later sided with
2 CONC  clapboard, and remained in the family until 1980.  
2 CONT His son, Col. George W. Summers II inherited Walnut Grove and lived there when he was elected to the Virginia Legislature in 1830 and to Congress in 1841. 
2 CONT Family tradition states that Abraham Lincoln asked him to be his running mate when he ran for his second term as President, but George Summers declined because of his wife's illness.
2 CONT The last Summers to reside at Walnut Grove was Fred Summers who left the property to his niece, Lucy Quarrier. 
2 CONT  The John Amos Power Plant purchased the farm with plans to move the historic house to Charleston, where an entire city block was set aside for it.  It was intended to be listed on the National Register of Historic Places, but tho
2 CONC se plans fell through.  Lucy Quarrier then donated the home to West Virginia Farm Museum, so the house was moved to Point Pleasant where it now stands preserved as a state-operated museum.  Address:  W.Va. State Farm Museum, 1458 
2 CONC Fairground Road, Point Pleasant, WV 25550 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family cemetery was moved from Walnut Grove plantation to Charleston's Spring Hill in 1978.  
2 CONT From Theresa's Haunted History of the Tri-State, Jan. 6, 2017: "The family cemetery contained not only the Summers patriarch, Colonel George, Sr., but also George Summers (Jr.), his brother Lewis, and other family.  Interestingly,
2 CONC  a favorite family slave, an alleged African prince named Scipio, was buried with the family.  When the cemetery was relocated, Scipio retained his place of honor, along side his master, Colonel George Summers, Sr."
2 CONT
2 CONT U.S. Genweb Archives contains this description:
2 CONT OLD SUMMERS CEMETERY: Located at Walnut Grove above Winfield (1/2 mile below Shawnee Estates). This cemetery was moved into the Spring Hill Cemetery in Charleston on Dec. 19, 1978.  
2 CONT Each grave was located in the same proximity to the grave next to as it had been in the old cemetery.
2 CONT
