1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Pendleton_Barbour
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip Pendleton Barbour (May 25, 1783 – February 25, 1841) was a U.S. Congressman from Virginia and an Associate Justice of the United States Supreme Court. He was also the brother of Virginia governor and U.S. Secretary of War J
2 CONC ames Barbour as well as the first cousin of John S. Barbour and first cousin, once removed of John S. Barbour, Jr..
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbour was born near Gordonsville, Orange County, Virginia. He attended common and private schools and graduated from the College of William and Mary, Williamsburg, Virginia, in 1799. A year later he was admitted to the bar, and 
2 CONC began practicing law in Bardstown, Kentucky. In 1801, he moved his law practice to Gordonsville in Orange County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbour started his public life as a member of the Virginia House of Delegates from 1812 to 1814. When U.S. Representative John Dawson died, Barbour won the special election to fill the seat, and served as a Democratic-Republican 
2 CONC in the U.S. House of Representatives from September 19, 1814 to March 4, 1825, reaching the office of Speaker from 1821 to 1823.
2 CONT
2 CONT He declined to run for re-election in 1824 and turned down an offer to become the professor of law in the University of Virginia in 1825. Instead, he was appointed a judge of the general court of Virginia and served for two years,
2 CONC  resigning in 1827 to return to his seat in the House of Representatives as a Jacksonian. For the first two years of his second stint in the House, he was chair of the U.S. House Committee on the Judiciary. In 1829 he was presiden
2 CONC t of the Virginia constitutional convention while remaining a Representative.
2 CONT
2 CONT Federal judicial service
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbour turned down offers of a chancellorship and the post of U.S. Attorney General before finally resigning October 15, 1830 to accept President Jackson's appointment to be judge of the United States District Court for the Easte
2 CONC rn District of Virginia. Jackson appointed Barbour by recess appointment on October 8, 1830, to a seat on that court vacated by George Hay. Barbour was formally nominated on December 14, 1830, and two days later he was confirmed b
2 CONC y the Senate, and received his commission., where he served until March 17, 1836. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbour continued to receive offers: he refused nominations for judge of the court of appeals, for Governor, and for United States Senator. Finally, he was offered appointment to the U.S. Supreme Court. Nominated by Jackson on Dec
2 CONC ember 28, 1835, to a seat vacated by Gabriel Duvall, Barbour was confirmed, and received his commission, on March 15, 1836. Barbour served until his death in Washington, D.C., on February 25, 1841.
2 CONT
2 CONT He built a large and graceful brick home in Orange County, Virginia called "Frascati". Historians often say that he was born at "Frascati", but it did not exist at his birth. His brother, James Barbour, also build a beautiful plan
2 CONC tation house. His was designed by Thomas Jefferson. One can see the Jeffersonian influence in Frascati as it is very much like Barboursville and was erected by the same workmen who built the University of Virginia and Barboursvill
2 CONC e. Unfortunately, the homes of both Barbours were destroyed by fire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip Pendleton Barbour was buried in the Congressional Cemetery, Washington, D.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT Places named for Philip Barbour
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbour County, West Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippi, West Virginia
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip Barbour High School in Philippi, West Virginia
2 CONT
