3 STAE TN
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Farmer
1 TITL PVT.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Samuel James Leake was born on March 15, 1839 in Albemarle County, Virginia. He was born as the eldest son to Samuel Austin Leake and Martha A. Boyd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam was baptized on August 2, 1840 at Cove Presbyterian Church in Covesville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam served in the Confederate army as a private for the 19th Virginia Regiment under company K. He enlisted on April 15, 1862 and served until June 30, 1864.
2 CONT
2 CONT A few years after his time in the Civil War, he married his first cousin. She was a woman by the name of Lucy “Helen” Helen Boyd and the daughter of his maternal uncle (James R. Boyd).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam and Helen married on January 21, 1867 in Albemarle County, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together, Sam and Helen had five known children:
2 CONT Mary Carter Leake (1868-1975)
2 CONT Lula Morris Leake (1870-1938)
2 CONT Jennie Austin Leake (C. 1874-1874)
2 CONT Percival “Percy” Earlwood Leake (1875-1947)
2 CONT Claude Montelle Leake (1877-1924)
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1870, he and Helen lived in the Samuel Miller district of Albemarle County, Virginia. He worked as a farmer, while Helen worked as a housekeeper. The members of the household went as follows: Saml J Leak (age 30), Helen Leak (a
2 CONC ge 25), Mary C Leak (age 2), Lula M Leak (age 2/12 months).
2 CONT
2 CONT The family moved to Shelby County, Tennessee around the early to mid 1870’s. Their first stop was the city of Memphis. When the yellow fever epidemic started spreading in the Mississippi Valley in 1877, the family moved to Collier
2 CONC ville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sam and Helen both succumbed to yellow fever. Helen was the first to pass away on September 30, 1878, and Sam was the second, on October 19, 1878. Dr. Elgin Kossuth Leake became the guardian of their children on November 12, 1878 
2 CONC and the estate on the 15th. Not too long after, he sold the estate to pay for Sam’s funeral expenses and sent the children to live with their maternal grandfather, James R. Boyd in North Garden.
