1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Because Richard Morton was not listed in 1737 tithables for Amelia County, but his brother Thomas was, I feel he was younger that Thomas.  Thomas would have had to have been born in 1721 or earlier to be tithable. 
2 CONC  So about 1724 would be part way between ThomasÕs supposed birth in 1721 and JudithÕs approximate birth in 1727.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1731 John Morton conveyed to Richard Morton, son of Thomas Morton late of Henrico Co for 2500 pounds of tobacco already paid by Thomas Morton deceased, a tract of land granted by patent to John Woodson Sr on Thomas MortonÕs for
2 CONC mer line on Chickahominy Swamp and along James Cocke's line to line of Thomas Morton deceased (witnesses John Povall, Joseph Childers and Joseph Pleasants).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1740 Elizabeth Morton gave her children land in Amelia County.  Richard sold the land his mother gave him in 1740 to Thomas Brackett 19 April 1746.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 Jul 1745, Richard Morton was granted 400 acres of land in Amelia County on lower side of Buffalo River, known by the name of Barron Licks, bounded by a faced corner by the path.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 2 Jun 1758, Richard Morton was granted 800 acres of land in Amelia County on the lower side of Buffalo River by the path for 40 shillings; 400 acres part thereof being formerly granted to said Richard Morton by patent 20 Jul 17
2 CONC 45; 400 acres the residue never before Granted.
2 CONT
2 CONT Richard was listed in the Prince Edward tax list for 1767 with 2 sons over 15 living at home, Samuel and Quinn, and 5 tithable slaves: Frank, Simpson, Jack, Jean, Phoebe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Head of household in Prince Edward County in 1785 census listed with 7 whites, 1 dwelling, & 3 other buildings.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1786, John Morton and James Allen were appointed to take the acknowledgement of Judith wife of Richard Morton to deed for sale of 400 acres of land sold to James Morton (Judith being unable to travel).  George Quinn of Dinwiddi
2 CONC e County, VA, brother of Judith, gave the heirs of sister certain property in Henrico County in 1779 Ð namely Quinn, Samuel, John, and Sarah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Was merchant in Prince Edward Co. but in middle and late 1780's he and his son John took up large land grants in Kentucky and in 1790 he moved there with son John.
2 CONT
2 CONT Head of household over age 45 in 1810 census in Clark County, KY with 1 female over age 45 (Sarah Tanner), 1 female age 26-45 (Nancy Tanner).
2 CONT
2 CONT Will extract of Richard Morton
2 CONT
2 CONT My sons [should be grandsons Ð their father Samuel is his son] Archibald and Richard, My son Quinn, My son John, My son David, My son Jonathan, My daughter Sarah Tanner, My daughter Susanna Tinsley, My wife Judith. Written: 4 Dece
2 CONC mber 1804. Witnesses: James Jenkins, William Canaday, and Graham Hazelrigg. Probated: 27 February 1815 in Clark County, KY. [What happened to Lucy McCargo?  Was 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
