1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/KVP1-THC
2 CONT Thomas BROWN (16jun1776-27apr1856 Auchlochan) (Dennis Brown, rootsweb). Mundia gives birth 16jun1775. Thomas marr Martha DANIEL (1789 Virginia) (rootsweb). Thomas (16jun1776 Lesmahagow-27apr1856 Lesmahagow) marr Martha Morton Dani
2 CONC el 22may1804 at Charlotte, Virginia (LDS submission). Martha (1786 Charlotte-1851 Scotland) (LDS). Martha’s mother was a Morton (Hamiltons of Burnside, N. Carolina, book).Thomas Brown of Auchlochan settled in Southland, New Zealan
2 CONC d (NZ newspaper, 1882).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1841 the Misses Browns, A. (1773), E. (1775), M. (1778), J. (1791), were at Craighead House, Blantyre, Lanarks., as were Thomas Brown (1776), John G. Brown (1821), George Alston (1775) and Ann C. Alston (1816); all were indepen
2 CONC dent, b. Lanarks. (census). In 1841 Martha Brown (1784, independent), James (1811), Elisabeth (1821), b. outside county, were at Auchlochan, Lesmahagow, Lanarkshire (census). In 1851 Thomas Brown (1776 Lesmahagow, wid, landed prop
2 CONC rieter, JP), children (b. America, unm), James J. (1811, Justice of Peace), Elisabeth M. (1821), were at Auchlochan House, Lesmahagow (census).
2 CONT
2 CONT A modern mansion of Auchlochan supplanted the ancient one in abt 1814; Thomas Brown of Auchlochan d. 1856 and was succeeded by his son Maj. James Thomas Brown (“Annals of the parish of Lesmahagow”). Picture of Auchlochan, from “An
2 CONC nals of the parish of Lesmahagow” (1864): http://www.lesmahagow.com/pictgall/auchlochan.asp - NB web address is not secure (https:) - and alternative links
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lesmahagow  or  https://www.visitlanarkshire.com/explore/lesmahagow/
2 CONT
2 CONT from Find A Grave - see link under 'Sources' tab
2 CONT Dr Thomas Alston Brown
2 CONT BIRTH  1776 Lesmahagow, South Lanarkshire, Scotland
2 CONT DEATH  27 Apr 1856 (aged 79–80),  Lesmahagow, South Lanarkshire, Scotland
2 CONT BURIAL  Lesmahagow Old Parish Churchyard Cemetery, Lesmahagow, South Lanarkshire, Scotland
2 CONT
2 CONT BROWNSVILLE PLANTATION
2 CONT
2 CONT Granville County, North Carolina
2 CONT
2 CONT PLANTATION NAME:	BROWNSVILLE PLANTATION
2 CONT ASSOCIATED LINK(s):	 
2 CONT OWNER:	Thomas Brown of Scotland (1776-1856)
2 CONT BUILT:	Ca 1800
2 CONT ASSOCIATED SURNAMES:	BROWN, VENABLE
2 CONT HISTORY:	
2 CONT BROWNSVILLE PLANTATION (c1800-1876)
2 CONT
2 CONT c1800 Thomas BROWN (1776-1856) arrived in America, from his home in Lesmahagow , LKS, SCOTLAND. He joined up with his older brothers, John (1762-1832) and George BROWN (1773-1824), in Granville Co. They had arrived earlier, and we
2 CONC re in Partnership with Thomas MUTTER, in what would appear to be a Finance Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Brown bought a large tract of land [1] ¼ mile SE of Grassy Creek Baptist Church , which had been in existence for some 40 years before their arrival. [2] The land was on both sides of Grassy Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brownsville NC had a Post Office [3] , store and a school of some kind. The PO store and school were housed in a 2 story building that the local people, of later generations, called the High House.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1829/1830 Thomas started selling up his assets in ‘ Brownsville ' to his Son-in-Law Abraham Watkins VENABLE, as the following extract shows:-
2 CONT
2 CONT · Brown, Thomas to Abraham W. Venable.."...the following Negro slaves to wit, Joshua and his wife Pink her infant child Joshua, also her children Maria, Alfred, William, Doctor, Julia, Alexander also Jacob & his wife Eliza and the
2 CONC ir children Polly, Washington, Jane. Mingo and his wife Nancy and their children, Daniel & Booker; Ben and his wife Mary; Molly, Selly, Mary, George, Shadrack, Jane and old Jim, Frank and Micy. To have and to hold the said Negro s
2 CONC laves to him the said Abraham W. Venable his heirs & assigns forever..."( BOS-1829, p.171, book 12) [4]
2 CONT
2 CONT [Hon] Abraham W. Venable, born Oct. 16, 1779, died at Brownsville Feb.19, 1876. His loving wife Isobella Alston n é e BROWN died April 14, 1871, at Brownsville. They are buried in the near by Shilo [5] Cemetery, along with their d
2 CONC aughter Martha.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas BROWN with his wife and 3 younger children, returned to take up his inheritance of the Auchlochan Estates in Lesmahagow, LKS, SCOTLAND , and both died there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dennis BROWN 2002
2 CONT
2 CONT <denbrown@clear.net.nz>
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT SLAVE POPULATION:	In 1860,  A. W. Venable owned 43 Slaves
2 CONT RESEARCH NOTES:	
2 CONT [1] Possibly close to 1,000 Acres
2 CONT
2 CONT [2] The Census returns for Granville Co. 1830-1850
2 CONT
2 CONT [3] Post Office Opened July 1 1806 - Closed March 1 1815 - Thomas Brown Post Master - Reopened June 26 1818 - Closed Dec 6, 1866 - Mavis Fuller P.M. - Reopened Dec 18 – 1867 - Closed March 15, 1905 Caroline H. Clark P.M. [From Pos
2 CONC tal Records]
2 CONT
2 CONT [4]  'ENSLAVED ANCESTORS ABSTRACTED FROM DEED BOOKS, GRANVILLE COUNTY, NORTH CAROLINA DEED BOOKS A-Z & 1-21 1746-1864'
2 CONT
2 CONT [5] Shilo Cemetery is 100 feet from the road. There are some marked graves there although the local people say its been a hundred years since anyone has been buried there. It is fenced in and covers an area about 100 Ft. Sq. The c
2 CONC emetery is somewhat over grown now but some one has made some effort to keep it from growing up completely. It is located 1/2 mile from Grassy Creek Church 4 miles west of Bullock NC.
2 CONT
2 CONT Auchlochan House near Lesmahagow was the home to Covenanter Thomas Brown.
2 CONT Thomas Brown of Auchlochan fought at the Battles of Drumclog and Bothwell Bridge and afterwards was a wanted man.
2 CONT One day, two of Claverhouse's dragoons spotted him at the side of the River Nethan near to his home and attacked him. Thomas, with his sword drawn, defended himself ably for a while against the two dragoons before eventually being
2 CONC  overpowered and left for dead. The appearance of another wanted Covenanter on the opposite side of the Nethan attracted the dragoons attention and they left Thomas to go in pursuit of him. We don't know the name of this other Cov
2 CONC enanter but we do know he was sadly caught and killed on the spot. Thomas however was not dead as the dragoons thought, but he was unconscious, bleeding badly and severely injured. With the love and care of his friend he was looke
2 CONC d after and eventually made a full recovery, living to a ripe old age.
2 CONT The photo shows the newer house which was built on the site of the earlier one by Thomas's descendants around 1815, sadly for some reason it was not a listed building and was demolished in 2014.
