1 NOTE {geni:about_me} James Thomas BROWN of Auchlochan (Hamiltons of Burnside, N. Carolina, book) was b. abt 1811 at N. Carolina (D. Brown, rootsweb). Thomas Brown of Auchlochan d. 1856 and was succeeded by his son Maj. James Thomas Bro
2 CONC wn of the Lanarks. Rifle Volunteers (“Annals of the parish of Lesmahagow”). In 1871 James T. Brown (1807 America, deputy I.P. of Lanarkshire) was at “Anchlochan” Hse, Lesmahagow (census findmypast, in census at ancestry.com birth 
2 CONC was 1810).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT James Thomas Brown 
2 CONT Glasgow merchant, partner in Gray Roxburgh (q.v.), and awarded with co-partners the compensation for the Friendship estate in Trinidad and another smaller award there..James Thomas Brown[e] was not shown as a partner by the time G
2 CONC ray Roxburgh was subject to sequestration in 1848. There is only one death for a James Thomas Brown in Scotland between 1835 and 1880: that of James Thomas Brown of Auchlochan who died 28/10/1880 age 75 in Logie, Perthshire, singl
2 CONC e, age 75, the son of Thomas Brown of Auchlochan and his wife Martha Merton Brown née Daniel.
2 CONT
2 CONT 27/04/1863 – Glasgow Herald – Minerals to let in Auchlochan Estate including clay band and fire clay … Proprietor James T Brown, Auchlochan House by Lesmahagow
2 CONT
2 CONT James Thomas Brown, sometime residing at Quarter House near Denny (Stirlingshire), thereafter at Row House, Doune (Perthshire), and latterly at Bandora Hall, Bridge of Allan, died at Bandora Hall on 28 October 1880. His personal e
2 CONC state in Scotland was valued at £13,126 0s 1d.—From the James Thomas Brown 1880 Testament.
2 CONT
2 CONT 'James Thomas Brown sold Auchlochan to William Cathcart Smith Cuninghame'. Auchlochan had been in the Brown family for a long time. 'Before the Reformation the parish of Lesmahagow belonged to the Abbey of Kelso, and Mr. Greenshie
2 CONC lds, in his Annals of the Parish of Lesinahagow, Edin., 1864, p. 98, says that the Browns of Auchlochan were vassals under the Church. There is a Precept of Sasine in 1575 by Francis Stewart, Earl of Bothwell, Commendator of Kelso
2 CONC , to John Brown in Auchlochan, to infeft Alexander Brown in the lands of Fulfurde'. -- From the ‘MINUTE BOOK OF THE BOARD OF GREEN CLOTH 1809—1820’ Page 148 to 149 
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas Brown, by will, devised that mansion and its broad acres to Col. Venable. But a few years before the death of Mr. Brown, the Bank of Glasgow, of which he was a director and large stockholder broke, greatly shattering the fo
2 CONC rtune of Mr. Brown. In consequence he purchased an annuity of the Scotch government and gave to it all his property.
2 CONT
