2 CONT
2 CONT Dinner to Major Brown of Auchlochan. 
2 CONT This gentleman was entertained to dinner in the Clydesdale Hotel on Tuesday, by a number of his friends, on the occasion of his leaving Auchlochan. Thirty gentlemen sat down to dinner, the chairman being Major J.S. Hamilton of Fai
2 CONC rholm, supported on his right by the guest of the evening on the left, by Mr J.P. Alston of Muirburn. Mr Andrew Smith of Castlemains, Douglas, was croupier. The band of the 2d Royal Lanark Militia was present, and discoursed some 
2 CONC excellent music during and after dinner. Letters of apology for absence were read from Col. Buchanan, Col. Gerard, Mr Mosman, and others. After a sumptuous repast, the usual preliminary toasts was given from the chair, and duly ho
2 CONC noured. The CHAIRMAN, in raising to propose the toast of the evening, was received with loud applause. He said – It is seldom that we have an opportunity of meeting to do honour to a gentleman whose family have been connected with
2 CONC  the County of Lanark for the last three centuries and a half. I have it on the very reliable authority of the historian of Lesmahagow parish, whom I am proud to see present among this company this evening, that the Browns of Auch
2 CONC lochan were tenants of the monks of Kelso on the Lesmahagow Estate long before the Reformation and ever since that period have possessed the lands. Our guest, in whose honour we are met this evening, though born in Virginia, was e
2 CONC ducated in this country, his father having the due appreciation of the lasting benefits to be derived from a good sound Scotch education. After a short visit to Virginia on the completion of his studies he entered into business in
2 CONC  Glasgow, but did not very long continue his mercantile pursuit, for, on his parents having come home to reside permanently at Auchlochan, he resigned his share in the business of the firm with which he was connected, and joined t
2 CONC hem there, when he immediately became one of the most esteemed and popular of country gentlemen and a most zealous officer. (Applause.) For 22 years, gentlemen, he continued to serve with the Upper Ward of Lanarkshire Yeomanry Cav
2 CONC alry, and had thought, as well he might, that after so long period spent in the voluntary service of his country, he might retire with a mind well satisfied that he had done his duty, yet, such was his indomitable energy, and love
2 CONC  for arms, that win at that critical period in our countries history, when the clouds of war lowered black and threatening, and seemed about to overwhelm us, our glorious army of volunteers sprang into existence, and dispelled by 
2 CONC the sheen of the bayonets, those encircling mists of danger; then our guest was foremost in that great movement, and most materially aided, both by his great generosity and loyal example, to being the volunteers of this Ward to th
2 CONC e excellence afterwards attained under his command. (Applause.) He became the leader of the first company of volunteers of the Upper Ward (the Lesmahagow), and when other districts followed the noble example set them, and their va
2 CONC rious companies were united in an Administrative Battalion, he was elected to its command, which he only resigned after nine more years of service, and not until the volunteer movement had been fully established, and the volunteer
2 CONC s recognised as a force in the country. (Applause.) The Browns of Auchlochan, we may say, gentlemen, are hereditary soldiers. An ancestor of our guest’s, the only son of the family, was cut down and left for dead on his own door a
2 CONC t Auchlochan on his return from the battle of Bothwell Bridge, in which he had taken apart, he happily survived however, and lived to transmit his trusty glaive to his descendants, who shall preserve it, as a cherished heirloom. (
2 CONC Applause.) In the late Civil War in America several members of the Auchlochan family fell bravely fighting for the land of their adoption. And now, gentlemen, I ask you to pledge a bumpy to one of the most loyal and true Scottish 
2 CONC gentlemen, to a generous host, to a kind benefactor, to a warm friend, and to one of the few of whom we may truly say in the words of the old ballot – “We shall ne’er see the like again.” (Loud applause.) The chairman concluded on
2 CONC  behalf of the company by wishing Major Brown long life and happiness in his new home. The toast was received with the greatest enthusiasm. Major BROWN rose with considerable emotion, and acknowledged the high compliment paid him 
2 CONC in very appropriate terms. Amongst the other toast were the following: – “The Lord Lieutenant of the County,” by Mr Greenshields of Kerse, replied to by Mr Alston, Deputy Lieutenant; “the Member of the South Lanarkshire,” by Capta
2 CONC in Bertram of Kersewell; “The Town and Trade of Lanark,” by the croupier, replied by Provost Brown; “Commercial Interests of the County,” by Mr Alston; “The Clergy,” by Mr Matthews, replied by the Rev. Mr Macready; “The Ladies,” b
2 CONC y Mr Greenshields, reply by Mr A. Alexander; “The Honorary Secretary,” by the chair, replied by Mr. John Annan; “Agricultural Interest,” replied by Mr Waugh, St. John’s 
