1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.familysearch.org/en/tree/person/details/LHWZ-F4C
2 CONT John George BROWN (1818 N. Carolina, USA-1882 Scotland) (Dennis Brown, rootsweb). John Brown was officer in British Army (Hamiltons of Burnside, N. Carolina, book). John George Brown d. 5 Sep1882, age 65, at Liberton, Midlothian, 
2 CONC son of Thomas Brown of Auchlochan and late of Southland, NZ (NZ newspaper). Will (1 page) of John George Brown, formerly Glasgow mercht, afterwards res Yewlands nr. Edinburgh, d. 5sep1882 at Yewlands (scotlandspeople). John George
2 CONC  Brown marr Rebecca Monteath GARDEN 25jul1843, Lesmahagow, Lanarks. (familysearch). Rebecca Monteith, only dau of Alexander Garden, esq., was marr by Rev. Francis Garden (Trinity Ch., Greenwich), to John George Brown, esq., 25jul1
2 CONC 843 (Glasgow Herald). Rebecca Monteith Garden was b. 17sep1821 at Glasgow, dau of Alexander Garden and Rebecca Monteith (familysearch).
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain Brown was a devout Scottish Presbyterian whose family had deep Covenanter roots. According to the Rev. Charles Stuart Ross, Brown:
2 CONT •	conducted worship in his home, 
2 CONT •	cared deeply for the spiritual welfare of his workers, 
2 CONT •	generously supported church ministry, 
2 CONT •	was known for both his hospitality and practical Christianity. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Will (9 pages) of Rebecca Monteith Brown or Garden, Yewlands, Liberton, widow of John George Brown, son of Thomas Brown of Auchenlochan, d. 8apr1890 at Yewlands (scotlandspeople).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1851 Rebecca Garden (1791 Glasgow, wid, house proprietrix), son in law John George Brown (1818 USA, muslin manufactr), granddau Sarah Fullarton Brown (1848 Carstairs, Lanarks.), sister Janet Monteith (1790 Glasgow, unm), were a
2 CONC t Croy Cottage, Row, Dunbartonshire (census).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1851 Alexander E. Monteith (1794 Glasgow, advocate, sheriff of Fife), wife Frances (1805 England), children, Mary Anna (1831 Edinburgh, unm), Anna Julia (1843 Pacaitland/Pencaitland, E. Lothian), niece Maria R. Brown (1832 Edin
2 CONC burgh, unm), visitor Rebecca M. Brown (1822 Glasgow, merchts wife), visitors (merchts children, b. Edinburgh), Thomas N.M. (findmypast)/Thomas U.M. (freecen) Brown (1845), Rebecca M. (1847), Alexander F. Brown (1851), were at Inve
2 CONC rleith House, Edinburgh (census). Alexander Earl Montieth marr Frances, dau of Lt Gen James Wallace-Dunlop (thepeerage).
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1861 John G. Brown (1818 USA, Capt, Royal Lanarks. Militia, mercht), wife Rebecca M. (1822 Glasgow), children, Thomas H.M. (1845 Edinburgh), Rebecca M.G. (1847 Edinburgh), Sarah F. (1848 Carstairs, Lanarks.), Alexander F.G. (18
2 CONC 51), John G. (1853 Musselburgh), James R. (1854 Musselburgh), mother in law Rebecca Garden (1791 Glasgow), were at Edinburgh (census). In 1871 John George Brown (1818 USA, mercht) and Rebecca Monteith Brown (1822 Carluke, Lanarks.
2 CONC ), both marr, were at Moulin, Perths. (census). In 1881 Mrs. Monti Alston (1847 Edinburgh, wid, private means), sister in law Jane Alston (1826 Glasgow, unm, private means), visitors John Brown (1818 Virginia, N. Carolina, marr, i
2 CONC nterest of money), Rebecca M. Brown (1822 Dalsorf, Lanarks., marr, interest of money), were at Edinburgh (census); the visitors were the parents of Monti.
2 CONT
2 CONT •BLACKMOUNT 
2 CONT What Mr Ligar did with Run No. 173 during 1857 the collector cannot say, but probably little or nothing, save probably ordering sheep to stock it. When Strathallan arrived at Otago Harbour on January 8, 1858, she carried the McKer
2 CONC char family who had left Perthshire to seek their fortunes in the south of New Zealand. A week after landing, the head of the family, Mir Donald McKerchar, secured an engagement as manager of a large run in western Southland, and 
2 CONC with his son John, a lad of 13 summers, set out to walk to his new job. The route was from Dunedin to Riverton and then upcountry and it took the pair nine days to reach their destination. This was run 173 and as Mr Ligar had retu
2 CONC rned to Victoria, Mr McKerchar had a free hand to go ahead, and dependable like most of his countrymen he faithfully laid the foundations for prosperous pastoralism. 
2 CONT In September 1859 he supplied the number of sheep on the run as 880 and next year as 1350. It is noteworthy that both years in the sheep returns his name is spelt "McKercher." It is said that he it was who named the run Blackmount
2 CONC , but whether from its natural appearance or from a place in Perthshire, the collector wondered until he read it was named because a grass fire swept the hill and left it a blackened landmark. The McKerchars let the run in 1861, a
2 CONC nd the collector has no particulars about the next three years of run history. 
2 CONT In 1864 the run came into the possession of John George Brown and William Stewart and the collector will sit back and let the Rev. Charles Stuart Ross take up the story for a little. 
2 CONT "Captain Brown," he wrote, "was the descendant of a long line of worthy ancestry, noted for their adherence to Reformation principles, and for the contribution which they made to the Martyrs' Roll in defence of Gospel truth. One o
2 CONC f the most cherished heirlooms in the possession of the family, we believe, is a trusty sword once wielded in defence of the cause so dear to every Covenanter's heart. Captain Brown came to the colony in 1863, and after spending s
2 CONC ome time in the provinces of Nelson and Canterbury, he finally determined in the following year, to settle in Southland, and invested largely in station property in the Waian district. 
2 CONT
2 CONT CAPTAIN J. G. BROWN 
2 CONT “He purchased Blackmount, jointly with Mr W. Stewart, and bought, shortly after, on his own account, an adjoining run to which he gave the name of 'Bellemont, after the name of his wife. In 1872, after some years spent in Scotland
2 CONC , Captain Brown returned to this country, intending permanently to settle here. He dissolved partnership with Mr Stewart, and joined interest with Mr Barnhill in the Blackmount run, and set about the erection of a house at Bellemo
2 CONC nt for the suitable accommodation of his wife and family. 
2 CONT • "To him we were indebted for our first acquaintance with the Waiau country. He pointed out to us short 'cuts' over mountain spur and tussock, and piloted us safely over blind creeks and quicksands which were far more numerous in
2 CONC  the country then than they are now. 
2 CONT "He was an ordained elder of the Free Church of Scotland, and gave the high influence of his personal Christian character to the furthering of the interests of evangelical religion in the land. He was an earnest tract distributor,
2 CONC  and concerned himself about the spiritual condition of his servants; he conducted divine service in his own house on the Sabbath day and was rarely absent from the quarterly central services which were held at Otahu. When he atte
2 CONC nded service at Riverton he put a pound note into the collection plate which always stood at the church door, and on top. of that he placed a shilling or half-a-crown, 'so that,' as he said, 'the wind shouldn't blow the paper away
2 CONC ! If a minister needed furlough his generous help was always sent unsolicited. 
2 CONT "He passed through some heavy trials in the colony which left their scars upon him. The passing away from the family of the ancestral estate in Lanarkshire, which had been for centuries in their possession, was a sore grief to him
2 CONC . With rare devotion he waited upon his elder son while a wasting fever preyed upon his life; and later on, he himself was smitten down by a serious illness, which he felt to be a solid spiritual gain. In 1877 he sold out all his 
2 CONC interests in the colony and left for Scotland, and we felt that we had lost a personal friend and our church one of her most loyal sons and generous benefactors. Some six years after that, the profoundest sorrow was awakened by th
2 CONC e tidings which reached us of his death." 
2 CONT
2 CONT RUNS 172 and 174 
2 CONT In common with other Waiau runs, the collector cannot give an exhaustive history of Blackmount, but he has a certain number of notes about it which should be incorporated here before passing on to the next run. In 1871 Brown and S
2 CONC tewart had 19,300 sheep on run 173, and the area of the run was only a little less than the 40,000 acres originally applied for, being 39,180 acres (162 Square Kilometres). Some time after this the run was apparently divided into 
2 CONC three portions known as 173A, 173B and 173C, the first of these being the northern or Redcliff run, the second the central portion: and the third the south-eastern portion. The collector is sorry he is unable to supply any details
2 CONC  of this division, or of the various subsequent owners of each portion, except that a Mr Hare is said to have been the owner of Blackmount in 1886. The only note the collector has about Blackmount: is that in the mid-seventies Joe
2 CONC  Bray had fencing contracts on this run, one of his assistants being John Driscoll, later a highly respected resident of Gore where he died some years ago. 
2 CONT (A lady correspondent has just sent in some. particulars regarding early Blackmount and these will be considered at a later stage.) 
2 CONT The next run on the list is No. 174 and here we run into another mystery for the Land Office at Invercargill has no particulars whatever about it, nor is it included in the list of runs transferred to the Southland area in 1871. I
2 CONC t is a run that has simply dropped out of existence, after being applied for in 1857 by C. W. Ligar and advertised at the same time as No. 173 which was also applied for by him. It has been confused with No. 176 in, the same distr
2 CONC ict and owned by McKay Bros and later by Gillow, 'and both these names are in my notes as reputed owners of No. 174 and both items are erroneous. The only clue possessed by the collector to its, whereabouts is on the 1857 map of O
2 CONC tago where it is marked as "between Little Creek and Redbank" near the Waiau. This placed it as. imine-diately above the first application for No. 173 and it seems to have been later incorporated in the area covered by the latter 
2 CONC number. At. the Waste Lands Board in July 1859; "a letter from /J. Macandrew and Co., relative to the cause of the non-stocking of Mr Ligar's run on the Walau was read and it was resolved that the run will not for the present be a
2 CONC bandoned; that every effort must be made to stock it within two months, and that the Chief Commissioner then bring the subject up again." Next year (1860) Run No. 173 was ex-tended, the northern line being placed further north, an
2 CONC d probably that explains the disappearance of the missing run.
