1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Henry Watkins, Sr'''. (1637–1714) was a prominent early American colonist, landowner, and devout Quaker who lived in Henrico County, Virginia. He is widely recognized by genealogists as the patriarch and immigrant ancestor of a
2 CONC  large and historically significant Southern family line.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Disputed origins 
2 CONT *Not the son of https://www.geni.com/people/Henry-Watkins/5589544176550107805?through=3999336755710034243. He had no spouse or children.
2 CONT * Not the son or wife  of  https://www.geni.com/people/Alice-Watkins/6000000226926981866 
2 CONT *While 20th-century compilations attached him to the 1623 Burgess Henry Watkins and "Alice Moslin," no contemporary primary records support this relationship, and modern genealogical consensus treats his origins as unknown.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Parentage
2 CONT
2 CONT There are no conclusively documented parents for Henry Watkins, Sr. in surviving 17th-century Virginia records. While he is the earliest "positively identified" member of this specific Watkins line, his exact parentage remains a s
2 CONC ubject of debate among genealogists.
2 CONT
2 CONT Religion and Legal Troubles
2 CONT
2 CONT Watkins was an active member of the Society of Friends (Quakers). Because his religious beliefs clashed with the laws of the Church of England and colonial authorities, he frequently appeared in court records:
2 CONT
2 CONT Fines for Quakerism: In 1684, he was heavily fined by colonial authorities for refusing to attend the Anglican church and continuing his Quaker practices.
2 CONT
2 CONT His Daughter's Imprisonment: His 15-year-old daughter, Elizabeth, was temporarily imprisoned when she refused to take a legal oath in court, adhering to the Quaker belief against swearing oaths.
2 CONT
2 CONT Support of the Faith: In 1699, he famously contributed 500 pounds of tobacco toward building the Curles Friends Meetinghouse in Henrico County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notable Landholdings
2 CONT
2 CONT Watkins accumulated significant real estate in colonial Virginia. He lived in the Malvern Hills and Turkey Island areas along the James River. Rather than keeping his wealth until death, he broke tradition in 1692 by dividing up h
2 CONC undreds of acres of land along the Chickahominy Swamp and gifting it directly to his five sons "with love and affection" while he was still alive.
2 CONT
2 CONT Family and Legacy
2 CONT Wife: He was married to Katherine (often identified as Katherine Pride), who was notably not a Quaker herself. She made headlines in colonial records in 1681 when she was involved in a high-profile rape accusation against a neighb
2 CONC or's slave; a case Henry initially resisted bringing to court due to his Quaker pacification beliefs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: They raised at least eight children, including sons Henry Jr., William, Edward, Thomas, and Joseph, and daughters Elizabeth, Rachel, and Mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT Famous Descendants: Through his sprawling family tree, Henry Watkins, Sr. is an ancestor to several prominent American figures, most notably the legendary 19th-century statesman and orator Henry Clay.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1692, Henry Watkins the Quaker executed deeds of gift in Henrico County transferring land to his verified sons:
2 CONT #  Henry Watkins Jr. (c. 1660–1715)
2 CONT #  Thomas Watkins (c. 1670–1760)
2 CONT #  Edward Watkins (c. 1676–1771)
2 CONT #  Joseph Watkins (c. 1680–1723)
2 CONT #  William Watkins (c. 1667–1785)
2 CONT # (Along with daughters Elizabeth, Mary, and Rachel).
2 CONT
2 CONT —-
2 CONT Source Information:
2 CONT Kinard, June. comp.. Early Immigrants to Virginia from the 1500s and 1600s [database on-line]. Provo, UT, USA: Ancestry.com Operations Inc, 2000. Original data: Published by The Researchers, PO box 39063, Indianapolis IN. 46239-00
2 CONC 63.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/176164541/henry-watkins
2 CONT __________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT [http://www.oursoutherncousins.com/watkins.html from Our Southern Cousins - Watkins Family]
2 CONT
2 CONT =Watkins Family
2 CONT My file on the Watkins family is the largest in my database.   Our direct ancestor (my greatx3 grandmother) was Mary Elizabeth Martha Watkins who married Charles DeLaigle in Augusta, GA.   I have met so many lovely people in the c
2 CONC ourse of this research, and I thank each and every one.   Most particularly, I thank Mrs. Eugenia Richards, whose friendship and help made learning more about our shared Watkins’  ancestors such a joy!  
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY WATKINS is documented in Henrico County, Virginia, and our immigrant ancestor.   Many sources say Henry was born about 1638 in Wales, possibly in Talgarth, Brecknockshire, South Wales.   He was a Quaker, a member of the Soci
2 CONC ety of Friends, and appears in officials records in Henrico County, VA as early as 1677.   One quote says:   "One of the most interesting families in Virginia from the point of view of economic, social and political development is
2 CONC  the distinguished family of which Henry Watkins is the immigrant ancestor."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1677:   assisted Richard Cocke, Col. Ligon, and Gilbert Jones in the survey of “Mawburne Hills,” which he owned.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1678:   Henry Watkins paid tithes in Varina parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1679:  Deed patented for 170 acres of land on the north side of the James River in Henrico County.   Henry Watkins held land in the "Turkey Island" district of eastern Henrico County, north of the James River, when a militia roll 
2 CONC was taken in June 1679.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1684:   Fined for continuing in his Quakerism.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1690:  Purchased 360 acres of land in Varina Parish, Henrico Co, south side of Chickahominy Swamp from Lyonel Morris.  That same year, he bought 60 acres of land adjoining his own land and touching a run of Turkey Island Creek.   
2 CONT
2 CONT 1699:   Subscribed 500 pounds of tobacco towards building the Friends meetinghouse at Curls
2 CONT
2 CONT 1703:   Paid 50 pounds of tobacco towards furnishing the Friends building.  
2 CONT
2 CONT 1704:   Quit Rents of Virginia lists Henry Sr. with 100 acres in Henrico County
2 CONT
2 CONT His religion, at times, caused him to clash with the ruling authorities in Virginia.  In 1660, the Virginia Assembly passed a strict law against Quakers, who they described as “"... an unreasonable and turbulent sort of people, wh
2 CONC o daily gather together unlawful assemblies of people, teaching lies, miracles, false visions, prophecies, and doctrines tending to disturb the peace, disorganize Society and destroy the peace, disorganize society and destroy all 
2 CONC laws, and government, and religion.”  You’ll find many mentions of Henry Watkins in "Quaker Records of Henrico Monthly Meeting" by F. Edward Wright.  When his wife was assaulted, he refused to prosecute the criminal because the la
2 CONC w required actions contrary to the Quaker doctrine.   In 1661 an act was passed that anyone who failed to attend services of the established church for a period of one month would be subject to a fine.   And again in 1666 an act w
2 CONC as passed imposing fines on 'refractory persons' for failure to comply with the militia laws and regulations.   In 1684 Henry Watkins was fined by the court for "continuing in his Quakerisms." His fine was later remitted.   Other 
2 CONC Quaker families in the area were the Pleasant’s, “Makeney’s,” and the Woodson’s.   
2 CONT
2 CONT One thing that endears Henry Watkins, to me, is his disbursement of his lands to his children prior to his death.   He mentions his love for his children when he gave land to each of his sons on the south side of Chickahominy Swam
2 CONC p in 1692.   The tradition of primogeniture was still common in the early colonies. However, by making a will you could distribute your property as you saw fit.   Henry went even further than this when he partitioned his land to h
2 CONC is sons prior to his death.    I feel it showed a great deal of confidence in the ability and integrity of his sons.  
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1691/2,  he gifted, “With love and affection:”
2 CONT
2 CONT William:  120 acres next to Edward Finch
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph:  120 acres “in the lower part”
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward: 120 acres “in the lower part”
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry (Jr.):  “the tract where I now live”
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas:  200 acres on “Three Runs”
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Watkins died 7 Feb 1714/1715 at his plantation near “Malvern Hills,” in Henrico County.   He had made his will in November 1714 it was proved 7 February 1715.    I have never seen a complete copy of his will.   All I know is
2 CONC  that it particularly left bequests to his grandson Thomas Watkins, son of Henry:    1 feather bed and bolster, 1 yarn "sett rugg", 1 pair nine quarter blankets, 1 pair sheets.
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY married CATHERINE.  Her last name has been assumed to be “Pride.”  Oddly, I have never seen any proof that was her last name.  I also don’t know if she survived Henry.   
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1)  Henry (Jr.) WATKINS (b.1660-Henrico County,VA d.1714/1715-Henrico County,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT  | sp: Mary CRISPE/CREWS
2 CONT
2 CONT 2)  Elizabeth WATKINS (b.Abt 1669-Henrico County,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT  | sp: John BOTTOMS/BOTTOMLEY (b. Abt 1665 m.1692)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3)   Rachel WATKINS (b. Abt 1670-Henrico County,VA d.Bef 1729)
2 CONT
2 CONT  | sp: Robert (Jr.) (called himself Sr.) WOODSON (b.Abt 1660 m.Abt 1710 d.1729-Henrico County,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT 4) THOMAS "of Swift Creek" WATKINS * (b.1672-Malvern Hills Plantation,Henrico County,VA d.1760-Cumberland Cty,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT  | sp: Elizabeth PRIDE * (b. Abt 1688-Swift Creek,Chesterfield,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT 5)  Edward WATKINS (b. Abt 1676 d.1771-Cumberland Cty,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT  | sp: (unknown-
2 CONT
2 CONT   | sp: Mary Bishop TAYLOR PENDLETON (m.Abt 1723)
2 CONT
2 CONT  | sp: (third wife of Edward Watkins-unknown)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6)  Joseph WATKINS (b.Henrico County,VA d.1723-Henrico County,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT  | sp: Anne
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) William WATKINS (b.1685/1690-Curles Plantation,Henrico County,VA d.Abt 1781-Cornwall Parish; Charlotte County,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT    sp: Martha HERNDON (m.Abt 1726 d.1781)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:   I have seen others reference Mary Watkins as a daughter of the elder Henry.   I have also seen her listed as a child of Henry Watkins, Jr.    If anyone has information about Mary, who married Nicholas Hutchins, please cont
2 CONC act me!   For now, I list her as a child of Henry, Jr.   As Henry, Jr. was the eldest child, he could have married and had Mary as an elder child, but I have no proof one way or the other.      
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT "Atkinson and Kidd Ancestry of Hollie Clare Atkinson":
2 CONT
2 CONT "There were a number of early Quakers in colonial Virginia. going back through a female ancestor, many of the Watkins are directly descended from Pocahontas through her son John Rolphe."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Hutchins-Hutchens, Descendants of Strangeman Hutchins":
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Watkins Sr. (II), a Quaker, was fined by the court in Henrico County, 1684, for "continuing in his Quakerism." His fine was remitted.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCES: "Hutchins-Hutchens, Descendants of Strangeman Hutchins", "Born 1707, of the James River in Virginia and Surry (Yadkin) County, North Carolina"; Vol. 1, pg. 2-6; Addenda, Appendix III., Ancestor Chart of the Children of St
2 CONC rangeman Hutchins, pg. 796-810; 3 Vols. by Rita Hineman Townsend; the book states that it is impossible to say who is the father of Mary, that it is either Henry Watkins, Sr. or Henry Watkins, Jr. ("Temple Archive Sheet" gives the
2 CONC  father as Henry Watkins, III, and his father as Henry Watkins, II). "Four Generations of the Family of Strangeman Hutchins and his wife Elizabeth Cox, as known January 10, 1935", (An old Virginia Family along the James River, by 
2 CONC Marriage joined to other Immigrant Families of the Colony), pgs. 3-4; Edited by Mrs. Gussie Waymire Crider and Edward C. Crider of Buck Creek, Indiana (now deceased). "Some Watkins Families," by John Hle Stutesman, "Henry Watkins 
2 CONC of Henrico"; To Va 1687 of Malvern Hall, Henrico Co., VA. Mary Watkins, wife of Nicholas Watkins-Ancestral lines is from "Temple Archive Sheet" from Salt Lake City Library; original source unknown; we have no further proof. Intern
2 CONC et Ancestry.com, Online Family Tree, "ReVeal-Favis Family Tree July 2003", Kacy Davis, whose source is unknown; "Atkinson and Kidd Ancestry of Hollie Clare Atkinson," by Hollie Bevan, fromadam2u@hotmail.com (Hone Page: Bevan Genea
2 CONC logy Services - Adam to You). "Ancestors of Kian Moring", by Bruce D. Johnson, brucedjohnson9@aol.com., from "Some Watkins Families," pg. 28, by John Hale Statesman (or Stutesman, 305 Spruce St., San Francisco, CA 94118) CS 71 W33
2 CONC 5 1989.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY WATKINS is documented in Henrico County, Virginia. Many sources say Henry was born about 1638 in Wales, possibly in Talgarth, Brecknockshire, South Wales. He was a Quaker, a member of the Society of Friends, and appears in o
2 CONC fficials records in Henrico County, VA as early as 1677. One quote says: "One of the most interesting families in Virginia from the point of view of economic, social and political development is the distinguished family of which H
2 CONC enry Watkins is the immigrant ancestor."
2 CONT
2 CONT 1677: assisted Richard Cocke, Col. Ligon, and Gilbert Jones in the survey of “Mawburne Hills,” which he owned.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1678: Henry Watkins paid tithes in Varina parish.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1679: Deed patented for 170 acres of land on the north side of the James River in Henrico County. Henry Watkins held land in the "Turkey Island" district of eastern Henrico County, north of the James River, when a militia roll was
2 CONC  taken in June 1679.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1684: Fined for continuing in his Quakerism.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1690: Purchased 360 acres of land in Varina Parish, Henrico Co, south side of Chickahominy Swamp from Lyonel Morris. That same year, he bought 60 acres of land adjoining his own land and touching a run of Turkey Island Creek.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1699: Subscribed 500 pounds of tobacco towards building the Friends meetinghouse at Curls
2 CONT
2 CONT 1703: Paid 50 pounds of tobacco towards furnishing the Friends building.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1704: Quit Rents of Virginia lists Henry Sr. with 100 acres in Henrico County
2 CONT
2 CONT His religion, at times, caused him to clash with the ruling authorities in Virginia. In 1660, the Virginia Assembly passed a strict law against Quakers, who they described as “"... an unreasonable and turbulent sort of people, who
2 CONC  daily gather together unlawful assemblies of people, teaching lies, miracles, false visions, prophecies, and doctrines tending to disturb the peace, disorganize Society and destroy the peace, disorganize society and destroy all l
2 CONC aws, and government, and religion.” You’ll find many mentions of Henry Watkins in "Quaker Records of Henrico Monthly Meeting" by F. Edward Wright. When his wife was assaulted, he refused to prosecute the criminal because the law r
2 CONC equired actions contrary to the Quaker doctrine. In 1661 an act was passed that anyone who failed to attend services of the established church for a period of one month would be subject to a fine. And again in 1666 an act was pass
2 CONC ed imposing fines on 'refractory persons' for failure to comply with the militia laws and regulations. In 1684 Henry Watkins was fined by the court for "continuing in his Quakerisms." His fine was later remitted. Other Quaker fami
2 CONC lies in the area were the Pleasant’s, “Makeney’s,” and the Woodson’s.
2 CONT
2 CONT One thing that endears Henry Watkins, to me, is his disbursement of his lands to his children prior to his death. He mentions his love for his children when he gave land to each of his sons on the south side of Chickahominy Swamp 
2 CONC in 1692. The tradition of primogeniture was still common in the early colonies. However, by making a will you could distribute your property as you saw fit. Henry went even further than this when he partitioned his land to his son
2 CONC s prior to his death. I feel it showed a great deal of confidence in the ability and integrity of his sons.
2 CONT
2 CONT In January 1691/2, he gifted, “With love and affection:”
2 CONT
2 CONT William: 120 acres next to Edward Finch
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph: 120 acres “in the lower part”
2 CONT
2 CONT Edward: 120 acres “in the lower part”
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry (Jr.): “the tract where I now live”
2 CONT
2 CONT Thomas: 200 acres on “Three Runs”
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Watkins died 7 Feb 1714/1715 at his plantation near “Malvern Hills,” in Henrico County. He had made his will in November 1714 it was proved 7 February 1715. I have never seen a complete copy of his will. All I know is that i
2 CONC t particularly left bequests to his grandson Thomas Watkins, son of Henry: 1 feather bed and bolster, 1 yarn "sett rugg", 1 pair nine quarter blankets, 1 pair sheets.
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRY married CATHERINE. Her last name has been assumed to be “Pride.” Oddly, I have never seen any proof that was her last name. I also don’t know if she survived Henry.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their children were:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1) Henry (Jr.) WATKINS (b.1660-Henrico County,VA d.1714/1715-Henrico County,VA)  | sp: Mary CRISPE/CREWS
2 CONT
2 CONT 2) Elizabeth WATKINS (b.Abt 1669-Henrico County,VA)  | sp: John BOTTOMS/BOTTOMLEY (b.Abt 1665 m.1692)
2 CONT
2 CONT 3) Rachel WATKINS (b.Abt 1670-Henrico County,VA d.Bef 1729)  | sp: Robert (Jr.) (called himself Sr.) WOODSON (b.Abt 1660 m.Abt 1710 d.1729-Henrico County,VA)
2 CONT
2 CONT ) THOMAS "of Swift Creek" WATKINS * (b.1672-Malvern Hills Plantation,Henrico County,VA d.1760-Cumberland Cty,VA)  | sp: Elizabeth PRIDE * (b.Abt 1688-Swift Creek,Chesterfield,VA) 5) Edward WATKINS (b.Abt 1676 d.1771-Cumberland Cty
2 CONC ,VA)  | sp: (unknown- | sp: Mary Bishop TAYLOR PENDLETON (m.Abt 1723) | sp: (third wife of Edward Watkins-unknown)
2 CONT
2 CONT 6) Joseph WATKINS (b.Henrico County,VA d.1723-Henrico County,VA)  | sp: Anne
2 CONT
2 CONT 7) William WATKINS (b.1685/1690-Curles Plantation,Henrico County,VA d.Abt 1781-Cornwall Parish; Charlotte County,VA)  sp: Martha HERNDON (m.Abt 1726 d.1781)
2 CONT
2 CONT "There were a number of early Quakers in colonial Virginia. going back through a female ancestor, many of the Watkins are directly descended from Pocahontas through her son John Rolphe."
2 CONT
