1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of PRIVATE.  DAR Ancestor #: A117340 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of Private.  [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A117340  DAR Ancestor # A117340]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Listed in the DAR Patriot Index - Henry: b c 1760 VA d 1843 TN m (1) Elisabeth X Pvt VA
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1769 he is on Stony Creek in Shenandoah Co., VA; in 1772 on New River; in 1774 Henry, Peter Jr., and sisters Eve (John) Teeter and Mary (John Valentine) Beeler are all on Beaver Creek of Holston River, between Abingdon, VA and 
2 CONC Bristol, TN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Noe: In Feb 1774 he registered land near Briston TN/VA
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:  Revolutionary War Muster rolls 1775-1783 show Henry Turney  as a private.  Roll Box 61 TN DAR 1939 Yearbook pg 63, SR 39-40, PA
2 CONT
2 CONT Note:  Prior to 1780 Henry settled in Cumberland Settlement, Fort Nashborough. Served at Fort Nashborough, NC (Nashville, TN) during the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Turney’s farm in 1790s was 1/4 mi. of the intersection of Flatrock Road and Grant Road, between Dixon Springs and Bridgewater.  The current address is 41 Edinburg Reservoir Road, Edinburg, VA.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Copy of will for Henry Turney taken from Will Book at the Tennessee State Library and Archives, County of De Kalb, Roll no. 77. Book 1838-1854. Page 48-49. (per William Turney - Bunker Hill. Illinois. listed in Wills Book of De Ka
2 CONC lb County Tennessee. Book A. Page 48 & 49)
2 CONT
2 CONT l.   Henry Turney, Senior, of the County of De Kalb, State of Tennessee do make and publish this my last will and testament hereby revoking and making void all other wills by me at any other time made. 1st, I desire that my funera
2 CONC l expenses and all debts be paid as soon after my death as possible out of my moneys that I may die possessed that may first come into the hands of my executors. 2nd, I give to my beloved wife. Elizabeth, my plantation during her 
2 CONC natural life, also all my stock of cattle, hogs and my main house-hold and furniture and all the money now on hand. 3rd, at the death of my wife, Elizabeth, I give and bequeath to my grandson, Joseph Turney, son of Isaac and Polly
2 CONC  Turney. my tract of land where I now live containing two hundred and fifty-nine acres lying in the District No third in De Kalb County, State of Tennessee and after the death of my wife, Elizabeth, the rest of my estate is to be 
2 CONC divided as follows: John Turney and Rebekoh Looney. I will them one dollar each. I also will my son Isaac Turney one dollar. 4th, I bequeath the so remainder of my estate be equally divided between the following legatees to wit Ja
2 CONC cob Turney. Mary Barns, George Turney’s heirs, Katherine Taylor. Joseph Turney. my son, Daniel Turney, Peter Turney. I do here by nominate and appoint my son Daniel Turney my executor in witness where of I do to this my last will 
2 CONC and testament. Sworn over, set my hand and seal this 29th day of September 1838.
2 CONT Henry + Turney (his mark) Signed, sealed and published in our present and we have subscribed our names hereto in the presence of the Testator the 29th day of September 1838.
2 CONT
2 CONT Probated November 1843 Attest: John W. Stark Francis Spurlock James Groom Jeremiah Hendrick
2 CONT
2 CONT November Term 1843 A paper purporting to be the last will and testament of Henry Turney was presented in open court fore probate and was duly proven in open court by the oaths of John W. Stark, James Groom and Francis Spurlock sub
2 CONC scribing witnesses to the same who being first duly sworn in open court depose and say that they are acquainted with Henry Turney, the testator, and that they signed the same will in his presence by his request at Joseph Turney’s 
2 CONC house and on notice the same will be admitted to court. P. M. Wade, Clerk of DeKalb County Court
2 CONT
2 CONT De Kalb County TN Administrators Settlements 1846-1907 abstracted & compile by Betty Moore Majors
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry built and defended Fort Nashborough during the Revolutionary War.  three of his sons moved to Arkansas - they had all fought in the War of 1812. (Source: Mary Turney Miller)
2 CONT
2 CONT Administrators Settlement Book, 1846 - 1856 p.41 4 Mar1847
2 CONT
2 CONT John Turner, administrator with will annexed of Henry Turney, decd. Following receipts filed: John Barnes          $46.70 Daniel Turney        $46.70 Jacob Turney         $46.70 Isaac Turney          $  4.67 Joseph Bogle          
2 CONC $  4.67 Joseph Turney        $  4.67 Francis Turner       $  4.67 William Turney       $  4.67 Catherine Turney    $  4.67 John Turner            $  4.67
2 CONT
2 CONT MTMs notes John Barnes m. Mary Turney - she died 1838 Daniel Turney - son of Henry an exec. of his will. Jacob Turney - think this is Henrys son Jacob B Turney SR The rest I think are children and in-laws of George Turney and wife
2 CONC  Catherine Barger.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tax lists of Wilson County Birth (~~~~ Wish Names in Court  Records 1802-1822 Compiled by Thomas E. Partiow Baltimore GENEALOGICAL PUBLISHING CO.. Pub. 1981  1803 CAPTAIN CRAWLEY'S DISTRICT 311.  Turney , Henry 640 acres
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1774 he was living in Fincastle Co., VA, 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1779 he was one of the appraisers of the estate of Martin Statzer and that same year both Henry and Peter Turney had cases before the same court. Under the provisions of the act of 1782 and 1783 the Cumberland Settlers who stay
2 CONC ed and defended the country were entitled to a right of preemption and were to make their entries without any price.  Henry Turney was one of 63 specifically named in the act.  1 June 1780 - prior to this date he settled in The Cu
2 CONC mberland Settlement
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Turney - October 23, 1783.  On the North side of Holston River not exceeding five miles below the mouth of German Creek beginning a small distance below the mouth of a spring branch running up the river including my improvem
2 CONC ent.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1795; 200 acres Hawkins Co., TN - 300 more acres 1820 Census Index, Smith Co. Turner, Henry  p.92 000001-00001
2 CONT
2 CONT E335 A 200 W June 7, 1784
2 CONT
2 CONT HAWKINS COUNTY, TENNESSEE GRANT BOOKS 1 & 2 1787 - 1819 Transcribed by Billie R. McNamara
2 CONT
2 CONT 255 29 Aug 1791 E/49 50 shillings 176 Hillsboro 10 Oct 1783 Grantee Turney Henry Location / Description 300 acres Sullivan County On Big Creek Beginning at a beech at the fork of the creek then south seventy west sixty pole to a w
2 CONC hite walnut at a sink hole then due south sixty pole crossing Big Creek to a beech then due east one hundred & fifty pole to a walnut under a ridge then along s ridge n 50 E two hundred & forty poles to a large white oak then n 40
2 CONC  W one hundred & forty four poles to 2 white oaks & hickory on Big Creek then down the creek as it meanders to the beginning
2 CONT
2 CONT (my note - Henry is mentioned in land descriptions for Archibald Fisher on north side of Holston River, Samuel Wilson in Hawkins Co., Steven Richards)
2 CONT
2 CONT 312 25 Apr 1792 F/44 50 shillings 198 Newburn 26 Dec 1791 Grantee Turney Peter Location/Description 200 acres Hawkins County On the north side of Holston River including David Blackwell's improvement Beginning at a black oak runin
2 CONC g thence east two hundred and fifty three poles to a pine then north one hundred and twenty seven poles to a pine then west two hundred and fifty three poles to a stake then to the beginning
2 CONT
2 CONT 1780 Henry had moved from the Holston River area (Washington Co VA at one point) which is eastern TN. He arrived sometime that spring/summer of 1780 at Ft Nashborough North Carolina (Nashville, TN today). For the main part of the 
2 CONC city of Nashville, there is a map of the original land claims, but not for the stations up and down the Cumberland River - that is all lost. So it is just a guess on my part, but I think Henry Turney must have gone to near Mansker
2 CONC 's Station/Salt Lick which is near Goodletsville TN today. That is a guess because in 1777 they were both in same area of eastern TN and both spoke German, etc. Also, I did find a claim put in later by Henry Turney for land which 
2 CONC sounds to be near Mansker's Station, and next to land of a Peter Looney. (Peter Looney later his claim was turned down, seems someone else put in a claim to part of that land before him).
2 CONT
2 CONT There is a record later that Henry Turney owned 640 acres from 17 April 1786 to 1797....but not that he ever lived on the land. 640 acres on both sides of Turney's branch on North side of Cumberland River. I figure this is probabl
2 CONC y where he went in 1780, but the Indians attacked Mansker's Station and all the other settlements and everyone moved into the Fort. So if Henry had built some kind of shelter or not, I don't know.
2 CONT
2 CONT About 1783 it seems Henry was back in Hawkins County on some land he registered in early 1780 before moving to the Cumberland. This was about the time Mansker rebuilt his station on the Cumberland I think.
2 CONT
2 CONT Records do show that in 17 April 1786 Henry Turney owned 640 acres on the Cumberland (on which Springhaven was later built) and he was of Hawkins Co.
2 CONT
2 CONT Records also show: 17 Nov 1797 Henry Turney of Hawkins Co gave 240 acres to John Looney proved by Peter Turney....and 200 acres to John Barnes. (Looney had married Henry Turney's daughter Rebekoh, Barnes had married Henry's daught
2 CONC er Mary in Gallatin, Sumner Co).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Tenn Gazette: to be sold at Cairo....24 June 1801....640 acres - Henry Turney - for non-payment of taxes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Apparently either Looney, Barnes, and Turney had not paid their taxes on this land, or some mistake had been made. By the way Cairo was the town in Sumner Co near this land, later it became a ghost town and Gallatin was built and 
2 CONC took its place. So don't think of Cairo Illinois as I did.....there was in late 1700s, early 1800s a town named Cairo on the Cumberland River in Tennessee and that is where the court house was located.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tax must have gotten paid because Henry sold the remaining 200 acres to Edward Sanders and it was filed 5 Oct 1801.
2 CONT
2 CONT Who built the house - Springhaven? I don't know, but interesting about the land, that the first white to own it was Henry Turney and that it was probably in the area where he was located in 1780.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now there is a house in Tennessee built by a Turney - Fairmont in Columbia, Tennessee. It was built by Eve Turney Smizer and I think she is buried there....it is still there and a private home now, but I have seen photos of it. (t
2 CONC he owners must wonder about all the crazy Turney’s driving by snapping photos). Eve Turney Smizer was the oldest child of Daniel Turney (b. 1758) and his wife Susan Matheny. Eve was born in Shenandoah Co VA, about 1787 the family 
2 CONC moved to Kentucky where she grew up and married Smizer. They went to Natchez for a while - I think her husband was involved in cotton buying or something like that - then to Columbia where they built their house.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reel 5 - book 16 - Smith County Entry Book for 1802-1803.
2 CONT
2 CONT Book 16 page 20 number 77 NC Warrant 3889. Henry Turney ase Reuben Goad...207 acs in Smith Co on Clear Fk, branch of Smith's fork to beg 10 poles above the Cave Spring.. by virtue of a certificate from the county surveyor bearing 
2 CONC date 5 May 1803. Henry Turney, Loc. 5 May 1803.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is the earliest I have found so far for Henry having land on Clear Forks, near Liberty, Smith Co TN....today right on the De Kalb and Cannon Co line. Mary
2 CONT
2 CONT Military Service: Class 5th Private in the 6th Company of the 2nd Battalion of the Northampton County Pennsylvania Militia. In 1780-1781 he assisted in the construction of Fort Nashboro, North Carolina (present day Nashville, Tenn
2 CONC essee). On April 12, 1781 he helped defend the fort against the Cherokee Indians.  Find a grave Memorial 28474761 Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000175883078847label=@S444@ Source Link: https://www.gen
