1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * '''Residence''': Smith, Tennessee - 1820
2 CONT ** Reference: [https://familysearch.org/tree/person/details/9VHS-D4S FamilySearch Genealogy] - [https://www.geni.com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Jul 10 2019, 9:04:28 UTC''
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter Turney was born in North Carolina (later TN), and died 1840-1842 in Van Buren Co., AR.  He and his wife Hannah arrived in Arkansas in 1835 from Smith Co., TN.  Several of his grandsons and also his younger sons fought for th
2 CONC e C.S.A. from the Higdon area. 
2 CONT
2 CONT tree
2 CONT The Battle of New Orleans General Andrew Jackson established his base of operations in New Orleans in late November 1814 to concentrate United States military efforts on the Mississippi River after discovering that British Vice-Ad
2 CONC miral Cochrane intended to direct the Gulf Coast campaign against New Orleans. Britain sent between 11,000 and 14,450 troops under the command of Major General Sir Edward Pakenham to fight in the Louisiana campaign. Jackson brough
2 CONC t with him a small force of frontiersmen. He was re-enforced in December by Carroll with 2,500 Tennesseans including Peter Turney, and by Coffee with 1,200 more. Militia units from surrounding states joined local troops including 
2 CONC mounted militia and dragoons; along with over six hundred of the First and Second Battalions of Free Men of Color; a group of Choctaws; and the legendary Baratarian pirates. In all his forces were between 6,000 and 7,000 men when 
2 CONC the action finally arrived. On December 23rd, the British made a move and Jackson attacked. He immediately ordered forward a detachment of Marines, a corps of artillery, the Mississippi Dragoons, the Orleans Riflemen, the Tennesse
2 CONC ans, a battalion of militia and the free men of color; about 1,200 men. The engagement which followed, while a hot one, reflected little credit upon the generalship of either commander. The British right attempted to outflank Jack
2 CONC son's left, but fell in with a division under General Coffee, the Tennessee Mounted Volunteers including Private Peter Turney. The night was very dark and foggy. The Tennessee unit found themselves cut off from the main line and a
2 CONC mongst the British. They had dismounted and were in the midst of the fight when they realized that they were caught in the middle. Companies lost themselves, many of them were separated from their horses and some of them were caug
2 CONC ht in friendly fire. There were long series of hand-to-hand combats as they had to fight their way back to their fellow Americans. The British loss was over 400. The American loss was 24 killed, 115 wounded, and 75 prisoners. A li
2 CONC ttle after daybreak on the morning of the 8th of January the British moved out of their camps and spread across the level ground between the river and the woods, about 6,000 men in line. At 8:30 a rocket went up on the British rig
2 CONC ht as a signal to begin the attack. A single cannon shot from the American line gave the corresponding signal. The foe moved to the attack in perfect order. At the opposite extremity of the line the attack was obviously a diversio
2 CONC n and Coffee's troops were able easily to repulse the feeble onset. The main fighting was else where. Throughout the battle the American sharpshooters were concentrating their fire especially upon the officers. Pakenham's troops p
2 CONC repared to scale the American parapets and when they were in position they discovered that the scaling ladders had been left behind. His troops under fire, there was no choice but to order the men forward; time and again. A charge
2 CONC  of grape shot struck Pakenham and he fell mortally wounded. Disabled, the British troops were shattered and the only thing left to do was to retreat and make for cover. The British loss was over 2,000 with 289 killed. The America
2 CONC n loss was 71, including only 13 men killed. The Battle of New Orleans had lasted not more than 25 minutes.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shared to Ancestry.com by Dpkizer
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT General Notes: Data from Mary Turney Miller, Berrien Springs, MI. Peter was born in a part of NC that is now in TN. He moved his family to AR from TN about 1834. They came by wagon train with his aunt , Mary Gilliam "Polly" Turney
2 CONC  (widow of Isaac). Peter's son, Henry, had been in trouble in TN , prompting the move. The Isaac Turney family moved on to Snowball, Searcy Co., AR, where one of Polly's sons (Pleasant) had visited about 1829 and liked the area.
2 CONT Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000175882890826label=@S33@
