1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_W._Summers
2 CONT
2 CONT George William Summers (March 4, 1804 – September 19, 1868) was an attorney, politician, and judge from Virginia and what became West Virginia) during the American Civil War.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early and family life
2 CONT
2 CONT Summers was born in Fairfax County, Virginia to George Summers and his wife, the former Nancy Ann Smith Radcliffe. His father represented Fairfax County in the House of Delegates for four terms, then moved his family to Kanawha Co
2 CONC unty (later Putnam County) in 1814. Young George Summers graduated from Ohio University in 1825. On February 7, 1833 in Charleston he married Ammazetta Laidley (1818-1892), and they had sons Lewis Summers (1844-1928) and George La
2 CONC idley Summers (1848-1863).
2 CONT
2 CONT Career
2 CONT
2 CONT Summers was admitted to the Virginia bar in 1827 and opened a law practice in Charleston.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1830, voters in Kanawha County elected Summers to the Virginia House of Delegates, where he served from 1830 to 1832 (when he was defeated by James H. Fry, whom he defeated two years later), and again in the part-time position 
2 CONC from 1834 to 1836.
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, in 1840, voters elected Summers was a Whig to the U.S. House of Representatives, where he represented what was then Virginia's 19th Congressional District. Summers served in the Twenty-Seventh and Twenty-Eighth Congresses, 
2 CONC and despite the abolition of the 19th district after the 1840 census. He won re-election to the restructured 14th Congressional district, but was defeated for reelection in 1844 by Joseph Johnson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Summers again represented Kanawha County as a delegate in the 1850 Virginia Constitutional Convention. However, his attempt to become Governor of Virginia failed in 1851, as he again lost to Joseph Johnson. The Virginia General As
2 CONC sembly, nonetheless elected Summers a circuit court judge for the Eighteenth Judicial Circuit.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1861, Kanawha County voters again elected Summers to represent them, at the Virginia Secession Convention of 1861. He vehemently opposed Virginia's secession from the Union and in March 1861 hoped, with associates, to call a bo
2 CONC rder state convention in Nashville or Frankfort (sometimes called the "Guthrie Plan" after James Guthrie of Kentucky) to forestall the looming conflict. After the secession vote, Summers resigned and was replaced by Andrew Parks.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death and legacy
2 CONT
2 CONT Summers died in Charleston on September 19, 1868.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1871, the West Virginia Legislature honored Summers by forming Summers County from portions of Fayette, Greenbrier County, Mercer County, and Monroe County.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT US Congressman. Elected to represent Virginia's 14th and 19th Districts in the United States House of Representatives, he served from 1841 to 1845. He also served as a Member of the Virginia State Legislature, State Court Judge, a
2 CONC nd as a Delegate to the Virginia Secession Convention in 1861.
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/12193318/george-william-summers?_gl=1*1gzc4rm*_ga*NjY1NzkyOTEzLjE2NjcyOTg4MDc.*_ga_4QT8FMEX30*MTY3MDAxMTMyOS40Mi4xLjE2NzAwMjEyMjcuNjAuMC4w Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni
2 CONC .com/projects/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Dec 2 2022, 22:49:27 UTC''
2 CONT
