1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Biological Facts
2 CONT Birth: Born on August 12, 1785, in North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage: Married Hannah Peters (sometimes recorded as Hannah Peter, 1789–1831) on March 5, 1807, in Washington County, Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Death: Died in October 1831 at the age of 46 in Pike County, Illinois. His wife Hannah died the exact same month, heavily pointing to an epidemic (such as cholera or a severe winter influenza outbreak) that tore through the Illino
2 CONC is frontier settlement.
2 CONT
2 CONT Migration and Geographic Timeline
2 CONT David's life follows a clear geographical path across three major early American territories:
2 CONT North Carolina (1785–c.1800): Born into the established Mize family line just after the Revolutionary War.
2 CONT Kentucky (c.1800–1828): Relocated to Washington County, Kentucky, where he married and fathered his early children, including Lila Dorinda (born 1808) and Samuel Peter (born 1811).
2 CONT Illinois (1828–1831): Packed up the family and migrated north to Pike County, Illinois. This is where the Mize and Neely families originally intersected, as the Neelys had migrated to the exact same Illinois county.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children and Legacy
2 CONT David and Hannah had a large household consisting of 11 children. Following David and Hannah's sudden deaths in 1831, their children stuck together as a tight-knit pioneer unit.When Lila Dorinda and her husband John Hunter Neely d
2 CONC ecided to move southwest to Texas in the 1840s, several of her brothers—including Samuel Peter Mize, David Mize Jr., Zachariah Mize, and James Mize—migrated along with them, helping to settle the regions of Grimes and Navarro coun
2 CONC ties.
