2 NOTE “Dr. Chalmers' Posthumous Works, History of Virginia” (no publication date) references Chief Powhatan and “his second daughter.” Chief Powhatan allegedly told Sir Thomas Dale's the messenger, “I have sold her in a few days past to
3 CONC  a great Werowance, three days journey from me, for three bushels of rawrenoke.” Opechancanough and Cleopatra certainly could meet these descriptions, but not uniquely so.
3 CONT
3 CONT Regarding dates, you will find many conflicting dates on the internet, none of which are reliable enough to make sound judgements. Pocahontas was only about 11 in 1607, and her sister (or half-sister) Cleopatra could have been bor
3 CONC n after that date. The dates fit well if you choose those that support your premise. We have few firm dates or ages to use as an anchor for research.
