1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Grattan Cabell Carrington'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/208739113/grattan_cabell-carrington
2 CONT
2 CONT RITES TODAY FOR SPANISH WAR VET
2 CONT
2 CONT Grattan Carrington, President of St. Mary's Holy Name Society, Buried with Army Honors
2 CONT
2 CONT With full military honors, Grattan Carrington, a Spanish War veteran and president of St. Mary's Holy Name Society, who died on Monday, was laid to rest this morning in St. Mary's Cemetery. Delegations from the Holy Name Society, 
2 CONC the Knights of Columbus and St. Mary's Lyceum were among the many friends of the deceased who attended the funeral.
2 CONT
2 CONT The cortege left his late home, 86 Ash Street, where he resided with his sister-in-law, Mrs. Thomas Foley, at 9:45 o'clock with a motorcycle escort from the local Police Bureau and a detachment of troops from the Second Division o
2 CONC f Infantry, Fort Slocum, preceding the hearse. In St. Mary's Roman Catholic Church, South Broadway, a Solemn High Mass of Requiem was sung, at 10 o'clock.
2 CONT
2 CONT The celebrant of the Mass was the Rev. Edward J. Doran, director of the Holy Name Society, of which Mr. Carrington was president. The deacon was the Rev. Charles T. Murphy. The Rev. Edward A. McGrath was the subdeacon. During the 
2 CONC Mass the choir of men and boys under the direction of Robert W. Wilkes rendered appropriate music. At the conclusion of the service a bugler sounded taps.
2 CONT
2 CONT A squad of soldier fired a volley over the grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT Numerous floral tributes from church societies, the Knights of Columbus and a huge floral designed American flag, from his former comrades at Fort Slocum, were banked around the flag-draped casket.
2 CONT
2 CONT Member of the Knights of Columbus, St. Mary's Holy Name Society, St. Marys' Lyceum and troops from the First and Second Divisions of Infantry and the First Division of Cavalry, were the honorary pallbearers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Carrington was prominently identified with St. Mary's Church since coming to this city nine years ago. He had a host of friends and was known for his charitable work in the parish. His wife died here in 1923.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his graduation from school he enlisted in the U.S. Army and fought all through the Spanish-American War, rising to the rank of First Lieutenant. During the World War he was assigned to Fort Slocum. He resigned his commission
2 CONC  in 1920.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is survived by a brother and a sister residing in Danville, Va.
2 CONT
2 CONT ~source:
2 CONT Yonkers Herald; Thursday, June 7 1928
2 CONT (also Jun 5 1928)
2 CONT
