1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Transcription of notes of Harriet Ann Venable (undated) from papers of her namesake, Harriet Venable Miller: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Just before the Civil war began Henry Watkins Edmunds joined the military company and was elected corporal. First fight got into he was wounded, captured and parolled. Came home and went to courting. Got engaged to Harriet Ann Ve
2 CONC nable. He was young and strong and vigorous and thought he could take care of a wife all right, but a short time after this engagement he was exchanged and volunteered the second time and joined a cavalry co. 3rd VA Cavalry, Fitz.
2 CONC  Lee's Brigade, J.E.B. Stuart's division, and was severely wounded at Kelly's Ford, CulpepperCo., VA, and returned home. His father sent for Dr. Mettener, a noted surgeon who when he saw him, said it would take several years for h
2 CONC is wound to heal (it took six years to heal). Heal so said that unless he had pnewmonia or pleurisy his wound would not shorten his days, but that he would always be short winded and never able to do continuous hard work. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He then wrote to his sweet heart, Hattie Venable and told her what the doctor had said and telling her as he could not ask her to sacrifice her life to the helpless cripple that he was, felt that he would not be able to take care 
2 CONC of her, that he would make no complaint if she should cancel the engagement between them. She replied at once that the engagement should not be broken and if he could not take care of her she coud and would take care of him, which
2 CONC  she did for 59 years. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1914 we celebrated our Golden Wedding and John D. Rockefellow(sic) celebrated his the same day and I feel that I have left mychildren with a better legacy by having such a mother and her example and precept than if I had had al
2 CONC l of John D.'s wealth to leave them."
2 CONT
2 CONT Civil War Veteran, wounded and disabled at Kelly's Ford, 1863, "an invalid for life." There were nine children in the family.
