1 NAME Joseph Dupuy /Eggleston/ Jr.
2 GIVN Joseph Dupuy
2 SURN Eggleston
2 NSFX Jr.
2 _MARNM Eggleston
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Union Presbyterian Seminary Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Farmville
3 STAE Virginia
3 POST 23901
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 13 NOV 1867
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 MAR 1953
2 ADDR
3 CITY Hampden Sydney
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU President, Hampden Sydney College
1 TITL Dr.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Joseph Dupuy Eggleston, Jr., PhD'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ''President, Virginia Tech University, 1913-19; President, Hampden-Sydney College, 1919-35''
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://www.encyclopediavirginia.org/Eggleston_Joseph_Dupuy_Jr_1867-1953
2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/135290192/joseph-dupuy-eggleston
2 CONT
2 CONT Eggleston was a pioneering educator who served as Virginia's first elected superintendant of public schools and made significant advances in Virginia education. He successfully increased funding for both public secondary schools a
2 CONC nd public universities (Virginia high schools grew under his tenure from 75 to 448), increased teachers' salaries, and lengthened school terms. Eggleston also served as the seventh president of Virginia Polytechnic Institute (Virg
2 CONC inia Tech), where his most ambitious fiscal projects were stalled by U.S. involvement in World War I (1914–1918) and a more wary state legislature. In 1919 he was named president of his alma mater, Hampden-Sydney College, where he
2 CONC  remained for twenty years, helping the institution to liberalize its curriculum and to weather the effects of the Great Depression. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Early Years''
2 CONT
2 CONT Joseph was born in Prince Edward County, Virginia on November 13, 1867 to Dr. Joseph Eggleston and Ann Carrington. He was the second of six children. He graduated from Hampden-Sydney College near Farmville in 1886 and taught in Ge
2 CONC orgia, North Carolina, and Virginia before serving as superintendent of schools in Asheville, North Carolina, from 1893 until 1900. Upon his appointment as editor and secretary of Bureau of Information and Publicity of the Souther
2 CONC n Education Board at the University of Tennessee in 1902, he was charged with studying education conditions with the goal of improving social, economic, and cultural circumstances in the South by improving the quality of education
2 CONC  throughout the region. He then returned to Prince Edward County to serve as its superintendent of schools, a position he held from 1903 to 1905.
2 CONT
2 CONT Reflecting the progressive spirit of the day, the General Assembly of Virginia revised the Virginia Constitution of 1902 to make the position of superintendent of public instruction an elected official instead of a gubernatorial a
2 CONC ppointment. In 1905, Eggleston became Virginia's first elected superintendent of public instruction.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early in the 1900s, Virginia was in the midst of a crusade to upgrade a woefully inadequate public education system. Led by Governor Andrew Jackson Montague (who served from 1902 to 1906) and Richmond Education Association members
2 CONC  Lila Meade Valentine and Mary-Cooke Branch Munford, the May Campaign of 1905 carried the message of educational reform to the people of the state. While campaigning for state superintendent, Eggleston placed himself squarely on t
2 CONC he side of the reformers. "Education," he proclaimed, "should be the chief business of the State." Though acknowledging that it was still the localities' responsibility to support schools, he advocated an expanded role for the sta
2 CONC te, especially in establishing high schools and libraries and increasing teachers' salaries, which, he claimed, had increased by only a dollar a month over the previous thirty-three years. Eggleston's reform agenda also included c
2 CONC onsolidation of small rural schools, state control of textbook adoptions, and improvements in agricultural training.
2 CONT
2 CONT State Superintendent of Schools During his administration from 1906 to 1912, Eggleston was successful in implementing much of his program in spite of opposition from parsimonious legislators. His ideas were supported by the progre
2 CONC ssive temper of the day, improved state revenues, and the support of key officials, including Governor Claude A. Swanson; Edwin A. Alderman, the widely respected president of the University of Virginia; and several legislators, no
2 CONC tably William Hodges Mann of Nottoway County. Curbing some of his enthusiasm for change, Eggleston worked closely with reluctant assemblymen, reminding them of their own campaign promises to improve schools.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1906, the Virginia General Assembly passed the Mann High School Act to provide localities $50,000 annually, on a matching basis, to establish and maintain high schools. At the time, Virginia had only seventy-five high schools, 
2 CONC many providing only rudimentary instruction above the elementary level. When Eggleston left office six years later, there were 448 high schools in the state. The assembly also authorized local school boards to borrow funds from th
2 CONC e state Literary Fund for school construction, doubled teachers' salaries, and provided more funding for traveling libraries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Not neglecting higher education, the state took over ownership of the College of William and Mary and significantly increased appropriations to the University of Virginia. In 1908, responding to the demand for more facilities for 
2 CONC training teachers, the legislature, after a fierce battle, established three normal schools for women, which opened in Harrisonburg in 1909, Fredericksburg in 1911, and Radford in 1912.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eggleston had a broad vision of the role of education in the development of the larger community. "Good schools, good roads, good churches, rural telephones, rural free delivery of mails, and traveling libraries are needed," he sa
2 CONC id, "and only by the irresistible strength of social cooperation can these blessings be assured to every community in the Commonwealth." One of his favorite projects, arising from his experience with rural education, was farm demo
2 CONC nstration work, whereby farmers would be instructed in procedures that would improve their productivity. He invited the nationally noted expert Seaman A. Knapp to Richmond to explain his methods. The first Virginia demonstration f
2 CONC arms were set up in 1907. T. O. Sandy was the first state agricultural agent, John B. Pierce the first agent to work with African American farmers, and in 1910 Ella G. Agnew became the first full-time home demonstration agent in t
2 CONC he nation. With Eggleston's encouragement, the general assembly established an agricultural high school in each congressional district to provide instruction in the latest techniques for students pursuing careers in farming.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eggleston's handiwork produced remarkable results: significant increases in teachers' salaries, length of school terms, and increased per-pupil expenditures. Teacher certification, school consolidation, and school privy requiremen
2 CONC ts were also established. Virginia's commitment to education improved at every level. Although Governor Swanson is rightly remembered as Virginia's "educational governor," he owed much of his success in that endeavor to school sup
2 CONC erintendent Joseph Eggleston.
2 CONT
2 CONT In spite of these efforts, Virginia's progress paled alongside that of the wealthier and more enlightened states of the Northeast. And the educational needs of black students remained woefully neglected. As the reform fervor faded
2 CONC , Virginia retreated to a traditional indifference and parsimony towards education; conflicts over control of the schools increased. "The terrific political fight made on me … in the assembly in 1910 took every ounce of strength I
2 CONC  had," Eggleston observed. "Since then the fight has continued; and it is a fight of ignorance in partnership with political knavery—a determination on the part of … the ringster to get possession of the schools."
2 CONT
2 CONT ''University President''
2 CONT
2 CONT Worn out by such combat, Eggleston readily accepted the presidency of Virginia Polytechnic Institute in March 1913, after only six months as chief of the Division of Rural Education in the U.S. Bureau of Education (January to July
2 CONC  of 1913). He brought to the position experience in agricultural education, but his policies at Tech in this and other areas were challenged by the same political opposition that he had confronted as superintendent. He lobbied for
2 CONC  additional funding for the university, undertaking a prolific letter-writing campaign with alumni, newspaper editors, and legislators, but with little success. He urged Tech board members, alumni, and faculty to become "walking, 
2 CONC talking, living ambassadors" to inform the public of Tech's role in education in the state, particularly its agricultural extension work. Eggleston believed such work would benefit the citizens of Virginia through increased food p
2 CONC roduction and improved rural living. He eventually won support for shifting the farm demonstration program to the university, a program for which he temporarily served as acting director.
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite his limited success with the Virginia legislature, Eggleston received much praise at Tech. His appointments, consultations with faculty, and call for a new field house won support from all groups. He cemented relations wit
2 CONC h alumni through his letters to them, and by creating elaborate commencement exercises that drew them back to campus for reunions. With construction of the McBryde Building of Mechanic Arts in 1917, he established a new architectu
2 CONC ral model for the campus, in the neo-Gothic style.
2 CONT
2 CONT The onset of World War I (1914–1918) sidetracked some of Eggleston's long-term projects. An Reserve Officer Training Corps (ROTC) unit was established on campus and the university became a national training center for war. To supp
2 CONC ort the war effort, Eggleston offered the services of the university's extension service to the Federal Food Commission, to promote food production and conservation. His struggles with legislators in Richmond continued, however. A
2 CONC  request for funds to increase faculty salaries was approved, but a bond issue for new buildings was defeated. He was more successful with federal officials, who approved his plan to train all teachers in vocational agriculture so
2 CONC lely at Tech.
2 CONT
2 CONT Discouraged with his failure to gain greater support from the legislature for programs at Tech, he resigned as university president early in 1919 and became the president of his alma mater, Hampden-Sydney College. Eggleston's plan
2 CONC s to transform Tech into a first-class university had been stymied by political opposition, but he had succeeded in improving morale among Tech's constituents and prepared the groundwork for the notable achievements of his success
2 CONC or, Julian Burruss.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Final Years''
2 CONT
2 CONT Reflecting his deep religious convictions, Eggleston returned to Hampden-Sydney with hopes of fostering Christian education at a church-related institution free of political infighting and student misbehavior. Unfortunately, colle
2 CONC ge students of the 1920s were alike everywhere in their libertine social habits, especially their Prohibition-era drinking. Adding to the challenge, the control wielded by the Synod of the Presbyterian Church, which had taken over
2 CONC  supervision of the college in 1919, was as exasperating to him as the General Assembly had been. The Synod's unwillingness to support the floundering little college financially threatened its closing. Nonetheless, Eggleston was n
2 CONC ot deterred. He embarked on an ambitious fundraising campaign and proposed a grand master plan that included a gymnasium, a swimming pool, a student activities building, and a new library. "The College," he said, "must either go a
2 CONC head or go to pieces."
2 CONT
2 CONT It did neither. Lack of support from the Synod, combined with the dire economic climate of the Great Depression of the 1930s, frustrated Eggleston's dreams, but his efforts ensured that the college would survive those difficult fi
2 CONC nancial times. Enrollment increased during the depression as low tuition attracted students who could not afford to go elsewhere; and few left because Eggleston found them work cutting wood, caring for chickens, and maintaining th
2 CONC e grounds. Professors' salaries were cut 10 percent, but no wage reductions were imposed on the staff of buildings and grounds. On a brighter note, Eggleston's genealogical studies of the college's founders located John Morton, wh
2 CONC o paid for a new classroom building in memory of an ancestor who had been a founding trustee.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eggleston never stopped trying to improve the institution. With faculty support, he liberalized the curriculum, created a freshman advising system, and made significant additions to the faculty. He resigned in 1935, but stayed on 
2 CONC for four years until a successor was found. He continued his historical research at his home on campus until his death in 1953. For a man known for his strong religious and professional convictions, Joseph Eggleston had an accommo
2 CONC dating attitude and an optimistic spirit that enabled him to achieve many goals. As he said late in his life, "Almost any problem of misunderstanding can be solved when disinterested men, having a common purpose, get together and 
2 CONC talk things over."
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Time Line''
2 CONT
2 CONT November 13, 1867 - Joseph Dupuy Eggleston Jr. is born in Prince Edward County.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1886 - Joseph Eggleston graduates from Hampden-Sydney College.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1893–1900 - Joseph Eggleston serves as superintendent of schools in Asheville, North Carolina.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1902 - Joseph Eggleston is appointed editor and secretary of the Bureau of Information and Publicity of the Southern Education Board at the University of Tennessee.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1903 - Joseph Eggleston returns to Prince Edward County to serve as superintendent of schools.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1906 - Joseph Eggleston becomes Virginia's first elected superintendent of public instruction after the General Assembly revises the Virginia Constitution of 1902 to make the superintendent an elected official instead of a guberna
2 CONC torial appointment.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1906 - The General Assembly passes the Mann High School Act to provide localities $50,000 annually, on a matching basis, to establish and maintain high schools.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1909–1912 - Responding to demands for more facilities to train teachers, the Virginia legislature establishes three normal schools for women in Harrisonburg (1909), Fredericksburg (1911), and Radford (1912).
2 CONT
2 CONT July 1, 1913 - Worn out by fighting with Virginia's stubborn General Assembly for a greater commitment to public education, Joseph Eggleston begins his six-year tenure as president of Virginia Polytechnic Institute.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1917 - In constructing the McBryde Building of Mechanic Arts, Joseph Eggleston shepherds in a neo-Gothic architectural model for Virginia Tech's campus.
2 CONT
2 CONT July 2, 1919 - Joseph Eggleston resigns as president of Virginia Polytechnic Institute to assume the position of president for his alma mater, Hampden-Sydney College.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1935 - Joseph Eggleston resigns as president of Hampden-Sydney College. He will stay on an additional four years until a successor is found.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1953 - Joseph D. Eggleston Jr. dies.
