1 NOTE {geni:about_me} A Patriot of the American Revolution for VIRGINIA with the rank of COLONEL.  [http://services.dar.org/public/dar_research/search_adb/?action=full&p_id=A005856  DAR Ancestor # A005856]
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT James, son of James Barbour and his wife Sarah Todd, was born in 1734 in St Mark's Parish, Orange County, Virginia.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT James Barbour married Frances Throckmorton[1] of Gloucester County, Virginia.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Sons
2 CONT # Mordecai Barbour, married Elizabeth Strode[3][1]
2 CONT # James Barbour (died unmarried)[3]
2 CONT # Thomas Barbour, married Mary Taylor[3][1]
2 CONT # Richard Barbour,[1][4] married Mary Moore [3]
2 CONT # Gabriel Barbour[3][1]
2 CONT # Philip Barbour, married (1) Lucy[3] (Lucinda) Taylor, (2) Elizabeth Hopkins Branch[1] (Eliza Hopkins)[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Daughters
2 CONT # Frances Barbour, married John Moore[3][1]
2 CONT # Sarah Barbour, married John Harrison[1] (Col. John or James Harrison)[3]
2 CONT # Mary Barbour, married Col. David Walker[3]
2 CONT # Lucy Barbour[3] (Lucy Reed Barbour),[1] married Wythe Baylor[3][1]
2 CONT
2 CONT James Barbour, "third of the name in Virginia in descent, was burgess from Culpeper county, assembly of 1761-1765. He was an ensign in the Culpeper militia, 1756, under Col. Thomas Slaughter, and 'marched on an expedition against 
2 CONC the Indians above Winchester.' In 1775 he was county lieutenant of the Culpeper militia, and was afterwards an officer in the American revolution."[5]
2 CONT He "was also a judge in the first court ever held in Kentucky, being the head of a commission appointed by the Legislature of Virginia to settle disputed land titles."[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT James Barbour died in 1804 in Lancaster "near Garrard County, Kentucky".[1]
2 CONT
