1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Claude was born in Collierville, Shelby County, Tennessee, on the 25th of December, 1877 as the son of Samuel James Leake and Lucy Helen Boyd (1st Cousins). He lost his parents during the yellow fever epidemic when
2 CONC  he was an orphan. E. K. Leake became his (and his siblings’) guardians on November 12, 1878 in Shelby County, Tennessee. Then E. K. Leake sent him and his siblings back to Albemarle, Virginia to live with their maternal grandfath
2 CONC er. He was raised by his maternal grandfather and his wife. (Not Claude's biological grandmother.) In the 1880 Census, he was listed as 2 years old and living with his grandparents in Samuel Miller, Albemarle County, Virginia. He 
2 CONC was listed as living in house number 117 and dwelling number 255. (Not too much information is shared beyond this. Claude was 32 in 1910 (this is listed in the census), and he was a Broader, who lived with unrelated people. (It is
2 CONC  unknown when exactly he moved out of his grandparents' house.) He worked as a carpenter, and was able to read and write. (It is unknown how long he worked as a carpenter.) (At some point in time, Claude was a well known contracto
2 CONC r up until his death.) He soon married his wife, (a farmer) on the 18th of January, 1910. Her name was: Mary Elizabeth Oliver, and he worked alongside her on the farm. Claude registered for WWI on the 17th of September, 1918. (He 
2 CONC was 40 years old.) His physical build and height are described to be Medium, his eye color is listed to be Grey, and his hair color is shared to be light. Claude would be admitted to the UVA Hospital on January 15th, 1924. He woul
2 CONC d eventually lose his life due to Influenza and then lastly Pneumonia. His demise would occur approximately at 7 PM, still in the UVA Hospital. Burial would occur in Mooreland Baptist Church Cemetery in North Garden, Albemarle Cou
2 CONC nty, Virginia. Claude's house (built himself) still can be found in the town of Red Hill; it is owned by someone unrelated to the family.
