2 TITL U.S., Find A Grave Index, 1600s-Current
1 NOTE LIFE AND TIMES OF SARAH WINSTON 
2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=0ff92b37-4a0f-4dc8-94ad-b9573b4938bc&tid=12140672&pid=-322153129
1 NOTE Nothing is known of Sarah after 1644, and the death of Dr. John Woodson per "The Woodsons and Their Connnections."
2 CONT
2 CONT When John was killed, Sarah remarried a Mr. Johnson and the inventory of her estate was recorded in January 1660 in Henrico County.
2 CONT
2 CONT I also found this information which regards the slavery issue. It appears that the "slaves" that John and Sarah bought, were not listed as slaves but as limited term indentured servants. I was looking into this because I have info
2 CONC rmation that reads that Sarah Winston was Quaker and found it difficult to believe that she was also a slave-owner.
2 CONT
2 CONT [Reply from Harry Bradshaw Matthews]
2 CONT
2 CONT Available via interlibrary from the Stevens-Germany Library at Hartwick
2 CONT College (WorldCat Accession No. 37508773) is the 1995 edition of my
2 CONT writing, THE FAMILY LEGACY OF ANTHONY JOHNSON: FROM JAMESTOWN,
2 CONT VA TO SOMERSET, MD 1619-1995
2 CONT "A Multi-Racial Saga in Black, Red and White: The Negro-Johnson and Indian
2 CONT Puckage Lineage."
2 CONT
2 CONT Several points of note:
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. The transporting of the Africans to Jamestown resulted from the piratical
2 CONT raids of the ship "Treasurer" and cover of its activities by the "Dutch
2 CONT man-of war", both ships owned by the Englishman, Robert Rich, Earl of Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is from America's First Families, found on the internet
2 CONT
2 CONT America's First Families
2 CONT Ancestor Roll Of Honor
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT DR. JOHN WOODSON
2 CONT VIRGINIA
2 CONT (1586-1644)
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. John WOODSON, the emigrant ancestor of this family was among the founders of the Virginia Colony. He came to Virginia in the ship GEORGE in 1619, as surgeon to a company of British soldiers. A native of Dorsetshire. An Oxford 
2 CONC Student in 1608. He brought with him his wife Sarah. They settled at Fleur de Hundred where their sons John and Robert were probably born.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fleur de Hundred, now known as Flowerdew Hundred, is probably named after Temperance FLOWERDEW, wife of Sir. George YEARDLEY, Virginia's first Governor, who came to Virginia in 1619 on the same ship with the WOODSON'S.
2 CONT
2 CONT The YEARDLEY'S owned the plantation and in 1624 sold it to Abraham PIERSEY. Flowerdew Hundred had a representative in the first House Of Burgesses in 1619 and when counties were established in 1634 it was part of Charles City Coun
2 CONC ty and in 1702 was included in the new Prince George County. Presently Flowerdew Hundred Foundation, 1716 Flowerdew Rd., Hopewell, Virginia 23860 owns and maintains the plantation as a Public Trust.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah WOODSON was a brave pioneer woman. In the absence of her husband during the Indian Uprising of April 18, 1644, aided by Robert LIGON, she resisted an attack by the Indians, killing nine. She loaded the gun while LIGON fired,
2 CONC  and hearing a noise up the chimney she threw the bed upon the coals, the stifling smoke bringing two Indians down, whom she dispatched. Her sons in the potato hole were saved.
2 CONT
2 CONT Over the years this story has been passed on from one WOODSON generation to the next and as passed among the various families has varied a bit in details but not in Sarah's bravery in defending her children. John WOODSON caught, i
2 CONC n the open was killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT The old time muzzle loading gun, eight feet long, was preserved and now owned by The Virginia Historical Society and on exhibit in the Virginia Museum in Richmond.
2 CONT
2 CONT After John WOODSON'S death his Sarah married..........DUNWELL and then ...........JOHNSON. On her death she left a combination inventory and nuncupative will which was recorded January 17, 1660/1. Bequests included John WOODSON, R
2 CONC obert WOODSON and Deborah WOODSON and Elizabeth DUNWELL. Henry Morton WOODSON, Historical Genealogy of the WOODSONS And Their Connections (pub. Memphis 1915) states that 20 of the 25 charter members of The First Families of Virgin
2 CONC ia are descendants of John WOODSON.
2 CONT
2 CONT A family account written about 1785 by Charles WOODSON (1711-1795) son of Tarteton WOODSON survives and provides details on John WOODSON his sons Robert and John and their descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT Robert and John WOODSON were among Tythables at Curles Plantation in 1679. Curles was on the North side of the James River near Flowerdew Hundred. This plantation was once owned by Robert WOODSONs' father-in-law Richard FERRIS, fa
2 CONC ther of his wife Elizabeth FERRIS.
2 CONT
2 CONT Several generations of WOODSONS were born at Curles including Robert's son Benjamin and his grandson John, who married Mary MILLER. Many members of the WOODSON family were Revolutionary War Veterens.I have found three JOHN WOODSON
2 CONC 'S including the John WOODSON that married Mary MILLER, serving as County Lieutenant in the Virginia Militia, all Colonels.
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia formed Committee's of Safety to take control of County Government at the start of the Rev. War. The Committee of Safety in Goochland County in 1775 included John WOODSON, Joseph WOODSON and Matthew WOODSON. In 1776 they w
2 CONC ere joined by Miller WOODSON and his son-in-law Frederick HATCHER.
2 CONT Submitted by Leola Copp O'Hara of Vallejo, CA.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Children of John WOODSON and Sarah WINSTON were.
2 CONT 1. Robert b. 1634; d.1707-1711 Jamestown,VA; m Elizabeth FERRIS.
2 CONT 2. John b.1632; d.1684; m.(1) Mary PLEASANTS; m.c.1677 (2) Sarah BROWNE d.1702-1704.
2 CONT 3. Deborah b.c.1641; d.c. 1660.
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith,
2 CONT
2 CONT Thank you for your reply. Here is a summary of the information that I found
2 CONT over the past year:
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------
2 CONT In April of 1619, the Governor of the Jamestown colony, Sir George Yeardley,
2 CONT sent an English ship named the "Treasurer" on a supposed "routine trading
2 CONT voyage." The Treasurer was accompanied by a Dutch (Man of War) ship. The
2 CONT Captain of the Dutch ship was named Jope. In fact, the Treasurer's true
2 CONT purpose was to act as a privateer and raid Spanish shipping and the Dutch
2 CONT ship was to cover its activities. Both ships were owned by the Englishman,
2 CONT Robert Rich, the Earl of Warwick.
2 CONT
2 CONT During their joint voyage, the two heavily armed vessels captured a Spanish
2 CONT slave ship and its cargo of approximately 100 African slaves. The Dutch
2 CONT ship returned at the end of August of 1619 to Old Point Comfort (near
2 CONT Jamestown) with approximately 20 of the Africans. The Dutch sold most of
2 CONT the Africans to Governor Sir George Yeardley and the colony's wealthiest
2 CONT resident, a merchant named Abraham Peirsey. Smaller vessels smuggled the
2 CONT stolen Africans from Old Point Comfort to Jamestown.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Spanish had considered the Africans to be slaves. However, because
2 CONT slavery had been eliminated as a classification in English law, the Africans
2 CONT had to be classified legally as "indentured servants." Based on a census
2 CONT taken in March of 1619, there were already 32 blacks (15 men and 17 women)
2 CONT "in the service" of Jamestown planters prior to the August arrival of the
2 CONT Dutch ship.
2 CONT
2 CONT There are indications that, after years of servitude, some of the 20 stolen
2 CONT slaves brought to Jamestown eventually obtained their freedom. However,
2 CONT unlike most white indentured servants who voluntarily contracted their
2 CONT services for a specific period of time, these Africans were not given such
2 CONT options and most of them probably remained in servitude for the rest of
2 CONT their lives. Indeed, by 1625, the Jamestown census listed ten "slaves."
2 CONT Over the next decades, the number of African slaves in the colonies would
2 CONT increase by the thousands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after the return of the Dutch ship to America in late August of
2 CONT 1619, the Treasurer also returned to America and dropped off an African
2 CONT slave woman named Angela. She was the first African-Virginian whose name is
2 CONT known. The Treasurer then set sail for Bermuda with twenty-nine of the
2 CONT original 100 Africans stolen from the Spanish ship.
2 CONT ----------------------------------------------Sources:
2 CONT Virginia Genealogist, Vol. 20, 1976.
2 CONT Historical Genealogy of the Woodsons and Their Connections, Compiled by Henry Morton Woodson of Memphis, Tenn., Published in 1915.
2 CONT Louise Goode Shannon, The House of Goode, (Dogwood Printing, P.O. Box 716, Ozark Mo. 65721, 1997).
2 CONT Genealogies of Virginia Families, From the William and Mary Quarterly Historical Magazine, Volume V, Thompson-Yates (and Appendix), Baltimore, Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc, 1982.
2 CONT Information provided by William Stephen Woodson.
2 CONT
2 CONT What fun..Ann - Never give up on Sarah or Sarah (s)!! More source
2 CONT records are coming out every year. The answer to the parentage of both
2 CONT John & Sarah is surely going to be found in England (or Wales etc.)
2 CONT
2 CONT I am using only 1 Sarah for this outline but add possible 2nd.
2 CONT
2 CONT FACT: SARAH & JOHN CAME ON THE GEORGE 1619
2 CONT Possibles: (1) They were married before coming to America
2 CONT (2) Sarah was a "maid"
2 CONT (3) Sarah was married to someone else & then married John
2 CONT
2 CONT Unknown: If any other relatives came to America on the George or
2 CONT another ship
2 CONT
2 CONT FACT: SARAH & JOHN WERE MARRIED WITHOUT CHILDREN- Jan 1624
2 CONT (since it is Jan - this is basically a 4 yr period)
2 CONT
2 CONT FACT: (ALMOST CERTAIN) JOHN WOODSON (JR) IS OLDER THAN ROBERT
2 CONT So his birth would be 1724-1732
2 CONT
2 CONT FACT: ROBERT WOODSON WAS BORN 1634
2 CONT (If one wanted to be very technical it could be 1633-5,
2 CONT depending on the unknown month & day)
2 CONT
2 CONT FACT: UNKNOWN IF THERE WERE ANY OTHER CHILDREN - given the very high
2 CONT mortality rate of the times it is easily assumable that there
2 CONT were more children who died young..if fact there could have been
2 CONT one or more births before 1724.
2 CONT
2 CONT FACT: (ALMOST CERTAIN) JOHN WOODSON (SR) was killed in the massacre
2 CONT 18 APRIL 1644 - 25 years after arriving. Robert about 10 years
2 CONT old & John older.
2 CONT
2 CONT FACT: INVENTORY/WILL OF WIDDOE SARAH JOHNSON presented in Henrico by
2 CONT John Woodson 17 Jan 1660. She must have died late 1659. {this
2 CONT is 40 years after the trip on the George}.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 cows
2 CONT Feather Bed & furniture
2 CONT 1 chest
2 CONT 1 pott
2 CONT 1 pewter dish
2 CONT 1 pewter ? (word not readable)
2 CONT 1 wooden dish
2 CONT 1 spitt
2 CONT A Taylors ....Iron & shayres
2 CONT 1 stuff wascott with Sarge Peitcott & other clothes
2 CONT
2 CONT "This was disposed of a little before her death & by her order as
2 CONT Follloweth"
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. To DEBORAH WOODSON - 1 cowe, fether bed & furniture & what
2 CONT tobackoes was left, her debts first being satisfied except a debt dew
2 CONT from ROBERT WOODSON. This to be for her maintenace.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. To JOHN WOODSON - the other cowe to be killed at his pleasure
2 CONT but if he let it live then what calves from sd Cow to be raised for the
2 CONT use of DEBORAH WOODSON.
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. TO ELIZABETH DUN... The hiefer & Cowe Calfe & Stuffe Wascott
2 CONT & Sarge Peticott. The Calf to Elizabeth Dunwell (Dunwele, Dunnell ect)
2 CONT to remayne in the hands of John Woodson till the sd Elizabeth comes of
2 CONT age.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. TO ROBERT WOODSON - what tobacco he owed, a spitt, pott &
2 CONT pewter dish.
2 CONT
2 CONT **********************8
2 CONT
2 CONT NOTES: The Chest, Taylors..Iron & shayres & ould clothes - not allocated
2 CONT
2 CONT John Woodson would be around 40 (+ or -) years old & Robert Woodson 36
2 CONT in 1760.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Elizabeth Dunwell is not "of age" so she is born after 1740.
2 CONT The possible relationships of ELIZABETH DUNWELL to Sarah are
2 CONT endless, a child, a grandchild, a niece, an orphan she raised,
2 CONT a step-child, and so on......
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. Deborah Woodson - "for her maintenance" "for the use of Deborah" - 
2 CONT could this mean she is a CHILD in 1760? She could
2 CONT be a daughter but one must add the possibility that she is a
2 CONT grandchild, daughter of an unknown dec'd son. Strangely the name
2 CONT Deborah never surfaces again as a name of a daughter in the
2 CONT early Woodson family
2 CONT
2 CONT Judith Woodson Martin <searchmyfamily@aol.com>
1 NOTE  John Woodson Sr, son of Dr. John Woodson, married (1) an unknownwoman
2 CONT and had two sons.
