2 CONT
2 CONT ROANOKE TIMES
2 CONT DATE: Sunday, January 24, 1999 TAG: 9901250202
2 CONT SECTION: VIRGINIA PAGE: B1 EDITION: METRO
2 CONT RESEARCHERS DISCOVER WHO FIRST AFRICANS IN VA. WERE
2 CONT 'WHAT WE'RE FINDING OUT IS REVOLUTIONARY'
2 CONT
2 CONT SUMMARY:
2 CONT Evidence suggests that these unwilling immigrants were
2 CONT likely to have been Christians and spoke a common language.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the scant history of forgotten persons, many people are faceless.
2 CONT But few have been swallowed by the dark shadows that obscure the
2 CONT first blacks known to have lived in Virginia.
2 CONT Except for a few passing references from Capt. John Smith and
2 CONT members of the Virginia Company, these ''20-odd Negroes'' left
2 CONT virtually no trace after disembarking from a Dutch ship in late
2 CONT summer 1619.
2 CONT And for nearly 400 years that lack of evidence made it hard for
2 CONT anyone, including many determined scholars, to talk about one of
2 CONT early America's most historic moments.
2 CONT A recent survey of Portuguese colonial shipping records, however,
2 CONT may have turned up the very vessel in which these unwilling
2 CONT immigrants came to the New World. New studies of the Portuguese
2 CONT African colony of Angola have shed unexpected light on the subject.
2 CONT ''When I gave a talk on the arrival of the first Africans in 1994,
2 CONT I really had very little to say,'' said Jamestown Settlement curator
2 CONT Tom Davidson.
2 CONT ''But in five years the whole story has changed - almost completely.
2 CONT Gradually, we're taking what was the poorest known segment of
2 CONT 17th-century Virginia's population and moving into a realm where we
2 CONT can talk about them as people.''
2 CONT Davidson gave a lecture recently that focused on several studies,
2 CONT including two pioneering works that appeared in the scholarly journal
2 CONT William & Mary Quarterly over the past two years.
2 CONT The first revolutionized the field, he says, by pinpointing the
2 CONT name, nationality and port of origin of the ship that carried the
2 CONT blacks from Africa to the New World.
2 CONT Sifting through Colonial shipping records, California historian
2 CONT Engel Sluiter came across a Portuguese merchant-slaver that lost its
2 CONT human cargo to English and Dutch privateers in the West Indies. The
2 CONT timing and description of the attack almost certainly tie that ship,
2 CONT known as the San Juan Bautista, to the Dutch adventurers who brought
2 CONT the first blacks to Virginia. They also link that human cargo to the
2 CONT Angolan port town of Luanda.
2 CONT ''Before this, we knew nothing about the Africans themselves. We
2 CONT didn't know if they were slaves. We didn't even know if they were
2 CONT Africans or Creoles from the West Indies,'' Davidson said.
2 CONT ''Now we have not only a probable origin - the Portuguese ship
2 CONT sailed from Angola - but a specific locale in Angola. And that's
2 CONT enabled us to discover what kind of people these first Africans
2 CONT were.''
2 CONT Other scholars, including William & Mary Quarterly editor Philip
2 CONT Morgan, an award-winning author in the field, believe Sluiter's
2 CONT careful work leaves little doubt about the identity of the Portuguese
2 CONT vessel.
2 CONT And that crucial missing link has led to a fast-growing chain of
2 CONT information about the first blacks who landed in Virginia, he says.
2 CONT In 1998, the journal published a study by Pennsylvania historian
2 CONT John Thornton that examined the Portuguese colony of Angola during
2 CONT the early 17th century.
2 CONT Thornton's search through the records of the period turned up not
2 CONT only the region in Angola from which the blacks came, but also the
2 CONT military campaign in which they were probably captured.
2 CONT He also turned up evidence suggesting that these Africans were
2 CONT likely to have been Christians, that they had years of experience in
2 CONT trading and dealing with Europeans and that they spoke a common
2 CONT language.
2 CONT Such traits would have made them better able to adapt to their lot
2 CONT in Virginia than the ethnically and linguistically diverse groups of
2 CONT blacks that began to arrive from West Africa later in the 1600s,
2 CONT Davidson says.
2 CONT Continued trading with Portuguese Angola, he adds, may help explain
2 CONT why the first generations of Africans were so much more successful in
2 CONT working their way out of servitude than those that followed.
2 CONT It may also help scholars understand why attitudes about race
2 CONT hardened in the late 1600s, when the concept of limited-term
2 CONT indenture began to mutate into the institution of lifelong slavery.
2 CONT ''What we're finding out is revolutionary,'' Davidson said.
2 CONT Copyright (c) 1999, Roanoke Times
2 CONT
