2 CONT http://trees.ancestry.com/rd?f=document&guid=704c5cea-4589-4785-9de3-a4d7a3f0e4e6&tid=10320707&pid=-179610633
1 NOTE  Sir Walter Raleigh returned to England after his second voyage to the
2 CONT American continent and submitted his report.  He gave the name
2 CONT Virginia, after the virgin Queen Elizabeth, to all that part of the
2 CONT continent which was claimed by England.  England tried several times
2 CONT to start a colony in the new and unknown world on the western shores
2 CONT of the Atlantic Ocean.  One attempt after another failed, leaving the
2 CONT promotors discouraged to the point of quiting.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, on 20 Apr 1606, King James I issed letters of patent to a few
2 CONT English noblemen.    According to the patents there were two companies
2 CONT formed in England, one at Plymouth and the other in London.  The
2 CONT territory assigned to the Plymouth Company extended from the mouth of
2 CONT the Hudson River to the eastern point of Maine and was called North
2 CONT Virginia.  The London Company was allotted land between Cape Fear and
2 CONT the Rappahannock River and was called South Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT The London Company sent three ships from Blackwall, England, with 105
2 CONT male emigrants, one of whom was Captain John Smith.  The three ships
2 CONT entered Chesapeake Bay between the two headlands (promotories) which
2 CONT they named Cape Charles and Cape Henry in honor of the Prince of Wales
2 CONT and his brother.  They landed at a point across the Bay that was so
2 CONT restful and peaceful that they named it Point Comfort.  After a few
2 CONT days rest from their weary voyage, they travelled  up the beautiful
2 CONT James river, named in honor of their King.  About 40 miles up the
2 CONT James River they landed on a peninsula on 13 May 1607 and began
2 CONT building their settlement.  They named it Jamestown, again after their
2 CONT King.  Thus, the first permanent English colony in North America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captain John Smith was appointed the third president of the colony and
2 CONT the colony faired rather well under this leadership.  In 1608, the
2 CONT colony was joined by 120 male emigrants.  On 23 May 1609, the London
2 CONT Company was given a new charter which gave them all the land 200 miles
2 CONT north and south of Point Comfort and extending from the Atlantic Ocean
2 CONT to the Pacific Ocean, the distance unknown to the King or any of the
2 CONT Company.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1609, the Company send nine ships to Jamestown with 500 more
2 CONT emigrants and huge quantities of supplies.  But within the next year,
2 CONT many of the colonists died  and by the close of 1610 there were less
2 CONT than 100 white people still living in Jamestown.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 29 January 1619, the ship George left England and landed at
2 CONT Jamestown, Virginia, the following April.  On board the George were
2 CONT Sir George Yeardley and his wife, Temperance Flowerdew, Lady Yeardley,
2 CONT and Dr. John Woodson of Dorsetshire and his wife, Sarah (?).  Sarah's
2 CONT surname is undocumented but many believe she was Sara Winston of
2 CONT Devonshire.  Also, there is some confusion about John Woodson's
2 CONT birthplace; some say Dorsetshire and others say Devonshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. John Woodson was a man of high character and was of great value to
2 CONT the young colony.  He came as a surgeon to a company of soldiers who
2 CONT were sent over to protect the colonists from the Indians who were
2 CONT quite unhappy about the white man coming and taking more and more.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1620, a ship landed at Jamestown with 20 negro captives that the
2 CONT skipper had kidnapped somewhere off the coast of Africa.  The 20
2 CONT negros were sold to the colonists as slaves which proved to be quite
2 CONT profitable in the cultivation of tobacco which was the staple crop at
2 CONT the time.  Dr Woodson, bought six of these Africans who were
2 CONT registered in 1623 as part of his household as Negars [sic] with any
2 CONT given names.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was also in 1620 that the London Company sent over 100 women who
2 CONT desired to seek their fortunes in the new world.  These young women
2 CONT possessed no wealth but were of respectable character.  However, they
2 CONT could n ot stay in the colony in a single state--they had to mary.
2 CONT
2 CONT The relationship between the white colonists and the Indians appeared
2 CONT to be friendly but this was proven to be untrue on 22 Mar 1622.  On
2 CONT that day, while the colonists were going about their daily chores, the
2 CONT Idians attacked the settlements and killed 347 men, women, and
2 CONT children in an incredible short time.  The remaining white men took up
2 CONT arms and chased after the Indians, killing without mercy and driving
2 CONT them even deeper into the wilderness.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. John  Woodson and his family were located at Fleur de Hundred or
2 CONT Piersey's Hundred, as it was sometimes called, located about 30 miles
2 CONT above Jamestown on the south side of the James River in what is now
2 CONT Prince George County.  He, his wife, and his six slaves were
2 CONT registered at Fleur de Hundred in Feb 1623 and there is no doubt that
2 CONT this is where his two sons were born.  John in 1632 and Robert in
2 CONT 1634.
2 CONT
2 CONT The colonists lived in constant fear of the Indians since there had
2 CONT been no real peace between the two forces since the Indian massacre of
2 CONT March 1622.  Twenty-two years passed with incident but revenge still
2 CONT lived in the heart of Chief Opechankano.  And on 18 April 1644, the
2 CONT Indians made a sudden attack on the settlements and killed about 300
2 CONT more of the colonists.
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. Woodson was returning home after attending a patient and he was
2 CONT killed by the Indians within sight of his home.  Sarah Woodson looked
2 CONT around for a place to hide her sons.  She placed John uder a tub and
2 CONT Robert in the potato bin [pit/hole].  For many years, their
2 CONT descendants were known as "Tub Woodsons" and "Potato Hole Woodsons,
2 CONT She passed her husband's gun to her indentured servant, Ligon, and
2 CONT looked around for a weapon she might use.  Ligon placed the gun in a
2 CONT tree-notch to brace the 8-foot muzzle-loading gun, killing seven
2 CONT Indians as they approached the house.  Sarah, back in the house was
2 CONT met by two Indians who were in the process of coming down the chimney.
2 CONT The disabled the first with a poty of boiling water and felled the
2 CONT second with a roasting spit.  It wasn't until after the Indians fled
2 CONT that Sarah and Ligon discovered that her husband had been killed.  The
2 CONT 8-foot muzzle loading gun was last known to be in the possession of
2 CONT the Virginia Historical Society.
2 CONT
2 CONT For three generations, we follow the Woodson family line through
2 CONT individuals with the given name of John Woodson.  Reference to each
2 CONT are made as follows:  Dr. John Woodson (1st generation), John Woodwon
2 CONT Sr (2nd generation) and John Woodson Jr (third generation).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT 1.  Adventurers of Purse and Person VIRGINIA 1607-1624/5 by Meyer and
2 CONT Dorman.
2 CONT 2.  Tidewater Virginia Families by Hutcherson.
2 CONT 3.  The Woodson Family by __________.
2 CONT 4.  Woodson Watchers Newsletter, 1982, VolumeI, #1, DAR.
2 CONT 4.  Historical Genealogy of the Woodsons by Henry Horton Woodson.
2 CONT 5.  Woodson Descendants and Allied Kin by Wade and Mary Lou Roberts.
2 CONT 6.  Family charts by Wade Roberts, Arlene Wright, and James Cockerham.
