1 NOTE {geni:about_me} For two very interesting stories about the backgrounds of John Woodson and his wife, Sarah, parents of John Woodson, Jr. see:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://huskey-ogle-family.tripod.com/ancestorarchives/id47.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Massacre of 1644
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18 April 1644 the smoldering Indian resentment against the colonists broke out again when Chief Opechankano led a massive attack against the settlers, slaughtering around 300 settlers before the colonists were able to drive the
2 CONC  attackers away.  Casualties at Fleur de Hundred (Piersey’s Hundred) were not high, probably because it was a palisaded settlement.  (A palisade is a fence of long, strong stakes, pointed at the top and set close together as a def
2 CONC ense.)  John and Sarah might still have been living at Piersey’s Hundred on 18 April 1644, but they had probably already moved to Curles.  In any event, both John and Sarah survived.  The next day, however, the Indians struck agai
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
2 CONT The account of John and Sarah’s fate during the Massacre of 1644 can be found in almost every discussion of the Woodson family.  The details vary somewhat from story to story, but in each case Sarah’s heroism, quick thinking, and 
2 CONC bravery are paramount. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On 19 April 1644, the day after the 1644 massacre, Dr. John Woodson had gone to check on the welfare of some of his patients.  While he was gone, the Indians attacked again.  Sarah was in their home with her sons, John, Jr., and R
2 CONC obert, who were at the time 12 and 10 respectively.  
2 CONT
2 CONT A visitor named Thomas or Robert Ligon was also in the Woodson’s home.  (Most accounts do not mention his first name and simply call him Ligon.)  Ligon may have come to warn the Woodsons, for they did have a little time to prepare
2 CONC .  Sarah gave Ligon her husband’s gun, an old, 8-foot-long, muzzle loading rifle.  He quickly found a notched tree branch in the yard to use to brace the gun.  In the meantime, Sarah hid John, Jr., under a washtub and had Robert g
2 CONC et into a hole in the floor that the family used for storing potatoes.  She hoped that the boys would be safely hidden if the attackers managed to get inside the house.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sarah also put a large pot of water on the fire to boil so that it could be used as a possible weapon. Sarah and Ligon worked as a team to use the large gun:  Sarah loaded it, and Ligon fired it.  When the Indians attacked the cab
2 CONC in, Ligon killed three Indians with his first shot.    With the second shot he killed two more.   Suddenly Sarah realized two Indians were on the roof, trying to come down the chimney.  She took her bedding off the bed and threw i
2 CONC t into the fireplace.  The resulting smoke overcame the Indians who fell down the chimney.  One fell into the boiling water and was scalded.  (Some versions say Sarah threw the water on him.)  Next, Sarah grabbed the roasting spit
2 CONC  from the fireplace and brained the other.  Both Indians were killed. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Turning once more to the threat of howling Indians outside, Sarah and Ligon put the gun to work again, killing two more attackers.  In all, they had fired 3 shots.  With 9 Indians dead, the remainder fled.  As soon as the Indians 
2 CONC were gone, Sarah called her two sons from their hiding places. Tradition has it that for several years afterward the boys bore the nicknames “Tub” and “Potato Hole.”  In addition, Woodson researchers often ask the question, “Are y
2 CONC ou a Tub Woodson or Potato Hole Woodson?” 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://huskey-ogle-family.tripod.com/ancestorarchives/id47.html
