2 CONT # Genealogy.com Data recorded by Steven Wacker
2 CONT # http://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/4:1:LCM5-DKT
2 CONT #  [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curles_Neck_Plantation '''Curles Neck Plantation'''] (also known as Curles Neck Farm) is located between State Route 5 and the north bank of the James River in the Varina district of Henrico Count
2 CONC y, Virginia. One of the great James River Plantations, Curles Neck has remained in active use for almost 400 years and remains a privately owned working farm which is not currently open to the public. As "Curles Neck Farm", a 5600
2 CONC -acre property was listed on the U.S. National Register of Historic Places in 2009.[1][2] In November 1635, a year after the formal designation of Henrico County as one of 8 shires (or counties) in the Virginia Colony, a land pate
2 CONC nt for 750 acres (3.0 km2) was granted to Captain Thomas Harris, who had apparently served under Sir Thomas Dale. The tobacco farm was referred to by early settlers as "Longfield", but soon thereafter became known as Curles Neck. 
2 CONC Captain Harris served in the House of Burgesses at Jamestown as a Burgess for Curles Neck.
2 CONT # ''Tidewater Virginia Families: Chapter 15: The Woodson Family (also Butler Ancestors)''. Page 403.  < [https://www.ancestry.com/sharing/240445?mark=51c6fc78c361780bab75a441bce9fa1e22d3906d3d913f7eef543678e9b71715 AncestrySharing
2 CONC ] > “Curles” is not to be confused with the present day “Curles Neck Farm,” though it lies just a short ways down river from “Curles” of there seventeenth century. “Curles Neck Farm,” today, lies within Charles River County, while
2 CONC  “Curles” was located, in the early days of the Colony, within the curl of the James River, farther up river in Henrico County. It was where present day Varina District is located.
2 CONT # Discovering Archaeology, "The Curles Neck Project"  < [http://www.avalar.net/archaeology.html link] > 
2 CONT # Mouer, L. Daniel. “''Thomas Harris, Gent., as Related by His Second Sonne''.” Historical Archaeology, vol. 32, no. 1, 1998, pp. 4–14. JSTOR, http://www.jstor.org/stable/25616587. Accessed 21 Mar. 2023.
