1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From [https://books.google.com/books?id=GuhfAAAAMAAJ&lpg=PA275&ots=Ugqx8WafSS&dq=edward%20east%20elizabeth%20woodson&pg=PA30#v=onepage&q&f=true link] to Historical Genealogy of the Woodsons and Their Connections, P
2 CONC art  l H. M. Woodson, 1915 - and [https://books.google.com/books?id=GuhfAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA275&lpg=PA275&dq=edward+east+elizabeth+woodson&source=bl&ots=Ugqx8WafSS&sig=WIi4BprB5Z1UAmzVGGGvtVXx-4Q&hl=en&sa=X&ved=2ahUKEwiR0YXy-PDcAhVKi1QK
2 CONC HedsB3EQ6AEwCXoECAEQAQ#v=onepage&q&f=true Page 32]
2 CONT
2 CONT Issue of 6 JOHN WOODSON3 (Robert,2 John1) and Judith Tarleton.
2 CONT
2 CONT VIII 28 Elizabeth,4 born about 1708, in Henrico county, Virginia. She married Joseph Pleasants, of Pickanockie, who was a son of John Pleasants, who emigrated from Norwich, England, in 1665, to Virginia, settling at Curles on Jame
2 CONC s River, in 1668. He died there May 12, 1698, aged 54 years. The name Pleasants is a no less prominent one in Virginia and the southern and western states than that of Woodson. A majority of the family until the last generation ha
2 CONC ve lived in the gentle tenets of the Society of Friends. The collateral connections of Randolph, Bates, Atkinson, Webster, Brooke, etc., have reflected honor on the lineage. (R. A. Brock, Pres., Va. Historical Society.) It is beli
2 CONC eved that 28 Elizabeth Woodson4 was the second wife of Joseph Pleasants. There was no issue to this marriage. By his first wife, whose name was Martha Cocke, he had probably, several children, one of whom was John Pleasants3 of Pi
2 CONC ckanockie, who married 81 Susannah Woodson.6
2 CONT
2 CONT ————-
2 CONT
2 CONT *http://www.jstor.org/stable/1919804
2 CONT **"4 JOHN8 WOODSON (Robert2, John1) was the "cousin" (nephew) John Woodson named in the will of John Woodson, Sr. He was a merchant and married Judith, daughter of Stephen Tarleton, of New Kent county. Charles Van dcr Veer Woodson
2 CONC  wrote to Mr. Brock that he had a looking-glass, framed in walnut, which bears the following inscription: "This glass belonged to Stephen Tarleton, who was my great-grandfather, and died in the year 1687. I have had the present fr
2 CONC ame put on it this 14th of December, 1794.-Charles Woodson." In 1712, Judith Woodson relinquished her dower rights in land deeded by her husband, John Woodson, merchant, of the parish and county of Henrico. He made his will in 171
2 CONC 5, and was dead in 1716. Issue: 13 Tarleton, 14 John, 15 Robert, Jr., 16 Josiah, 17 Stephen, 18 Jacob, 19 Judith, "married Stephen Cox." 20 Elizabeth, married "John Pleasants, of Pickanockie."
