1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 13 TARLETON WOODSON (John8, Robert2, John1) made a deed in 1721 in Henrico county to William Randolph for land conveyed by Nathaniel Bacon to his grandfather, Robert Woodson, in 1674. In 1735 he presented in Henric
2 CONC o court an account of his deceased father's estate. "He married Ursula, daughter of Charles Fleming, of New Kent county." His will was dated in Chesterfield county December 4, 1761, and names six children, 21 Charles, 22 Tarleton,
2 CONC  23 Susanna Pleasants, married "John Pleasants, of Pickanockie." 24 Sarah Terrill, "wife of Henry Terrill." 25 Mary Pleasants, married "John Pleasants, of Henrico." 26 Judith Cheadle, married "Thomas Cheadle," had issue George, na
2 CONC med in will of father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Susannah Fleming (Tarleton) and Judith Ursula Woodson (Tarleton), mothers of Ursula Woodson (Fleming) and Tarleton Woodson, were sisters, making Ursula and Tarleton first cousins. Quakers strongly disapproved of marriages between 
2 CONC first cousins but made an exception for them.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Notes=
2 CONT ''Based on post by Julia Clark on Genforum on 6/7/2009:''
2 CONT
2 CONT Ursula Fleming and Tarleton Woodson proposed their intentions of marriage 17th day 4th mth 1710 at the Henrico Quaker Meeting House. Ursula Fleming was the daughter of Charles Fleming and Susanna Tarleton. Since the couple were fi
2 CONC rst cousins, (their mothers were sisters), some members, including John and Joseph Pleasants, were of the opinion that first cousins should not marry. The agreement reached was finally stated. "Whereas the said Tarleton and Ursula
2 CONC  Fleming were contracted by Solemn vows and contracts, being ignorant of any orders or practices among Friends, that first cousins shall not marry, therefore be suffered than dissolve the meeting that they have liberty to take eac
2 CONC h other in marriage, when they shall think fit.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Fleming and Woodson families were well-acquainted. Susannah Fleming and Judith Woodson were sisters. Charles and Susannah Fleming lived in St. Peter's Parish and were members of the Henrico Monthly Meeting. Tarleton Woodson an
2 CONC d his father, John Woodson, had been engaged in a number of land transactions with Charles Fleming. Their families often met at meetings of the Friends and at family gatherings. Susannah Fleming (Tarleton) and Judith Woodson (Tarl
2 CONC eton) kept in touch with their children, and the Fleming family often gathered to celebrate marriages and holidays. Tarleton and Ursula had played together as children with all of their sisters and brothers and cousins. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Ursula and Tarleton were married: 3rd da 6th mth 1710. The couple had numerous children, most of whom married in the Quaker faith, and were substantial (a respected Quaker).
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children of Tarleton and Ursula Woodson=
2 CONT ''Need to verify''
2 CONT # Tarleton Woodson b. c. 1711 m. Anne Shepard Vanderveer
2 CONT # Charles Woodson b. c. 1712 md Mary Pleasants and Agnes K. Parsons
2 CONT # George Woodson (1712-1763)
2 CONT # Frederick Woodson (1713-1761)
2 CONT # Susannah Woodson (1714-1776) m. John Cocke Pleasants on 9/7/1731
2 CONT # Mary Pleasants (Woodson) (c. 1718-c.1766) m. ___ Pleasants
2 CONT # Lucy Woodson (1722-1763)
2 CONT # Jacob Woodson (1723-1761) m. Elizabeth Morton
2 CONT # Sarah Terrell (Woodson) (c. 1724-c.1764)  m. Henry Terrell on 7/6/1744
2 CONT # Judith Cheadle (Woodson) d. 1802 m. John Cheadle (?) and Thomas Cheadle (11/3/1744)
2 CONT
2 CONT Note that two of the children (Charles and Susannah) married children of Joseph Pleasants, who, along with his father John, had objected to the marriage of Tarleton and Ursula. Mary Woodson also married into the Pleasants family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------
2 CONT
