1 NOTE {geni:about_me} John Morton and Elizabeth Anderson lived at Morton Hall, which was located on "the back road" from Farmville to Hampden-Sidney. There they raised fifteen children, many of whom were prominent in their respective co
2 CONC mmunities. Nathaniel Morton, the great-grandfather of Samuel Packwood Morton, Jr., moved to Baltimore, Maryland, where he was a member of the --PAGE 123 Ð
2 CONT
2 CONT The shipping and coffee-importing firm of Bedford and Morton.,Nathaniel and his descendants were active in this trade for nearly a century, during which time fast-sailing clipper ships brought their cargoes from Rio de Janeiro. Ma
2 CONC jor James Morton, whose home "Willington" was the site of many social gatherings in Prince Edward County, served with distinction in the revolution. George Washington dubbed James "Old Solid Column," because his troops could alway
2 CONC s be counted on to hold their positions in battle. Captain Hezekiah Morton, the surety for Carter Thompson and Nancy Morton's marriage, was another of John Morton's six sons who served in the Revolutionary War. John Archer Morton,
2 CONC  born November 18, 1772, was too young to take part in the war; in later life, however, he served his country as United States minister to Bordeaux, France. Called "French John" by his relatives at home, John Archer Morton is said
2 CONC  to have married Cora Henri, a daughter of the governor of Haiti. French John's son W. J. Morton, of Bordeaux, France, was a student at the preparatory school at Hampden Sidney College in 1824. Alfred Morton, of the great wine fir
2 CONC m of Bordeaux, was a descendant of John Archer Morton. . .
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT '''Mary Elizabeth Anderson''' was born on November 9, 1736 in Hanover County, Virginia. Her parents were [https://www.geni.com/profile-20434724 Charles Anderson] and [https://www.geni.com/profile-16838589293 Elizabeth Anderson]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Mary married [https://www.geni.com/profile-52745619 Capt. John Morton] on September 15, 1750 in Farmville, Prince Edward County, Virginia, Colonial America. Together they had the following children:
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34668938472 Charles Morton];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-59575293 Elizabeth Morton];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34761331042 Lt. James Blythe Morton];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-45730136 Josiah Morton];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34656410058 Nathaniel Morton];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-16828095293 Nancy Thompson];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-61833149 Benjamin Morton];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34663721763 Sarah 'Sally' Fuqua (Morton)];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-61816275 Obediah Morton];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-34663724810 Mary 'Polly' Fuqua (Morton)];
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/profile-117926790 Hezekiah Morton]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT She died on November 20, 1822, in Prince Edward County, Virginia. 
2 CONT ----
