2 CONT [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/124099737/elizabeth-woodson Find A Grave Memorial ID # 124099737]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Edward County: Herbert Clarence Bradshaw, History of Prince Edward County, VA, (Richmond, VA: The Dietz Press, Inc., 1955)
2 CONT
2 CONT Some Morton families were prominent in Prince Edward County from its inception: Joseph Morton who married Agnes Woodson; Senator Abraham Venable, whose daughter Elizabeth married Josiah Morton [b. 1737]. Mortons, Venables and Vaug
2 CONC hns lived at Slate Hill. Families with which the Mortons were associated in North Carolina and Kentucky were also seen in Prince Edward County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Census, Prince Ed Co. 1785 Census/Tax:
2 CONT 	p. 101
2 CONT 	Morton, Josiah 4 - -
2 CONT 		Thos Sr 3-1-
2 CONT 		Saml 100-1-2
2 CONT 		Thos Jr 10-0-0
2 CONT 	Venable
2 CONT 		Chas
2 CONT 		Nathl
2 CONT 		Robt
2 CONT 		Elizabeth 5-1-17
2 CONT 		Abraham 1-1-5
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Crain, John 10-1-  -
2 CONT 	Morton, John 10-1-9
2 CONT 	 	John Jr. 7-1-1
2 CONT 		Rich 7-1-2
2 CONT 		James 35-1-4
2 CONT
2 CONT 	Vaughn, Thom 12-1-6
2 CONT 		Nich 8-1-2
2 CONT 	Barnett, John 2-1--
2 CONT
2 CONT Pittsylvania County: Maude Carter Clement, History of Pittsylvania County, VA (Lynchburg VA, J.P. Bell Co. Inc, 1929)
2 CONT
2 CONT A Virginia patent issued to Joseph Morton on Sandy Cr., 1751 (land in what became Pittsylvania County)
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of Joseph Morton, Sr. dated 1749 proven in Halifax Court March 20, 1753 naming wife Elizabeth, children: John, Joseph, Jehu; execrs: Joseph Morton, Jr., wife and John. Wits: Thomas Morton, Saml Morton and Joseph Morton Jr. Jo
2 CONC seph Morton Sr. said to be brother of John Morton of Henrico and Thomas Morton Sr. (father of Thomas Morton wit.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Pittsylvania 1785 Census p. 99 
2 CONT John Morton 10 white souls, 3 bldgs (same page as James Crane)
