1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Judge Wood Bouldin'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wood_Boulden Judge Wood's Wikipedia Page]
2 CONT * [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/111649882/wood-bouldin Find A Grave Memorial ID # 111649882]
2 CONT
2 CONT Bouldin was a prominent attorney, member of the Convention of 1861, and a judge of the Virginia Supreme Court of Appeals from 1872 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born in Charlotte County, Virginia to the former Ann Lewis and her husband, Congressman Thomas Bouldin (1781-1834), he was named for his grandfather Wood Bouldin, who had married Joanna, the aunt of U.S. President John Tyler. Thus
2 CONC  among the First Families of Virginia even in his early youth, Bouldin was sent to Richmond to receive a private education from Mr. Turner. He then was sent to Bedford County, Virginia to attend the New London Academy conducted by
2 CONC  Rev. Nicholas H. Cobbs, later the bishop of Alabama. After teaching school for a year, Bouldin moved to Halifax County, Virginia and studied law under William Leigh.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Maria Louisa Barksdale on December 22, 1837 in Charlotte County, and they had a son, Major Wood Bouldin (1838-1911). After her death he married Martha Baldwin Daniel (1819-), sister of judge William Daniel of Lynchburg,
2 CONC  who would bear daughters Elvina, Martha, Ann, Alice and Virginia, as well as sons Charles Ellett, Briscoe Baldwin and Frank Deane Bouldin.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Career''
2 CONT
2 CONT After admission to the Virginia Bar, Bouldin moved to Charlotte Courthouse to begin practice, but discovered the estate of his father was greatly embarrassed. In 1840, his household of four white males (including his brothers Will
2 CONC iam and Thomas and one boy) also included 13 slaves. Seeking a larger practice, Bouldin moved to Richmond in 1842 and entered a law partnership with Robert Stanard, who soon became a Judge of the Virginia Court of Appeals.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1853 Bouldin purchased a plantation on Staunton River formerly owned by John Randolph of Roanoke as well as practiced law in Charlotte, Halifax and Mecklenburg Counties. By 1860, Bouldin owned more than 69 enslaved people in Ch
2 CONC arlotte County.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''American Civil War''
2 CONT
2 CONT Elected to the Virginia Secession Convention of 1861, Boulden favored secession on the second vote, which resolution passed. His son Wood Bouldin, a recent University of Virginia graduate, would suspend their legal partnership in 
2 CONC order to become a Confederate artillery lieutenant with the Staunton Hill artillery throughout the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the Civil War, Charlotte County voters elected Boulden to the Virginia House of Delegates, and he served in that part-time position throughout the war.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pardoned by U.S. President Andrew Johnson, Bouldin failed to win election to the Virginia Constitutional Convention of 1868, losing to freed slave Joseph R. Holmes. Bouldin was present to hear the decision of the Virginia Supreme 
2 CONC Court concerning the contested Richmond, Virginia mayoral election of 1870 when the overcrowded balcony collapsed and killed several men and injured many more. Severely shocked but sustaining no serious injury, Bouldin took a shor
2 CONC t rest.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1872, Virginia legislators elected Bouldin to fill a vacant seat on the Court of Appeals but his judicial career was relatively short as he died on October 10, 1876.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Death and Legacy''
2 CONT
2 CONT Bouldin died on his plantation and was buried there. His son of the same name (1838-1911) became active in the state Democratic party and was elected and re-elected Commonwealth's attorney for Halifax County. He would represent it
2 CONC  at the Virginia Constitutional Convention of 1902, and play a key role in disenfranchising African Americans.
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Reference: [https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/111649882/wood-bouldin?_gl=1*wt2c1t*_ga*NjY1NzkyOTEzLjE2NjcyOTg4MDc.*_ga_4QT8FMEX30*MTY2ODU5Mzk1Ny4yMi4xLjE2Njg2NDI5MTQuNy4wLjA. Find A Grave Memorial] - [http://www.geni.com/proje
2 CONC cts/SmartCopy/18783 SmartCopy]: ''Nov 16 2022, 23:56:07 UTC''
