1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.familysearch.org/tree/person/details/K2MJ-SN3<br>
2 CONT https://canmore.org.uk/site/52590/edinburgh-charlotte-square-st-georges-parish-church
2 CONT
2 CONT One record shows his burial: 'September 1935; Hampshire, England, United Kingdom', only in 1974, the Isle of Wight was made a separate ceremonial county from Hampshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine, US patent 420,331: 1890 was put forward by Alexander Francis Garden Brown of Swindigemuir, Ayrshire. That's Garden, not Gordon. It appeared in the journal Industries, and uniquely, full details o
2 CONC f trials run on it were given. The engine was run at between 560 and 600rpm and yielded between 19 and 22 BHP. The engine was built by Messer John Lang & Sons of Johnstone, and it was at their works that the trials were conducted.
2 CONT
2 CONT A F G Brown took out three US patents for rotary steam engines; No 420,331 in January 1890, No 478,843 in July 1892, and No 507,780 in October 1893. The engine illustrated above is believed to use the design patented in 1892. The 
2 CONC 1892 design was patented in England in 1889 as No 16,913. The 1890 and 1892 engines are very similar but the 1893 design is quite different in its mode of working.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Another engine by Brown appeared in 1912; see below on this page. It looked very similar internally to the 1892 model.
2 CONT From http://www.museum.alibaba.sk/MUSEUM/POWER/rotaryengines/rotaryeng6.htm#brown
