2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/4b/72/1e/4a/534448626fcb4dab/the_brown_rotary_expansive_engine_pg8_original.jpg?hash=b25a5f31dc6f1b5c0d773e1d36b0db2448110bee7b06e65bd1ae02dbb434a153.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine pg8
2 NOTE The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine
3 CONT This is a cleaner view of the cross-section of the engine, without all the arrows and lettering. It is not a copy as there are small differences such as the extra exhaust port at top right.
3 CONT Note the admission valves are here much larger in diameter than in the patent drawing.
3 CONT Source unknown
3 CONT From http://www.museum.alibaba.sk/MUSEUM/POWER/rotaryengines/rotaryeng6.htm#brown
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/67/17/7d/73/534448626fc90b9a/the_brown_rotary_expansive_engine_pg7_original.jpg?hash=875138430c20a4594552cd002d1e283fa6a041da04035b7beedf6554f915845d.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine pg7
2 NOTE The Brown rotary steam engine; section in plan. This diagram gives a better view of the sealing arrangements.
3 CONT How it works:
3 CONT The steam is admitted by rotation of the left steam-valve SV.
3 CONT The piston rotates under this pressure, the steam from the previous admission leaving via the right exhaust-port EP, while the right exhaust-door ED maintains the sealing (?) of the piston.
3 CONT On the second half of the piston's revolution, the same actions occur with the right-hand steam-valve SV, and the left exhaust door and exhaust ports.
3 CONT This seems very unpromising to me, with all those heavy doors banging in and out; it's not exactly free of reciprocating parts, as noted at the time. It also looks as if it would be impossible to reverse it, as the rotor seal woul
3 CONC d catch on the noses of the exhaust doors.
3 CONT A one-door version of the engine could also be built.
3 CONT From The Book of Modern Engines by Rankin Kennedy. Volume 2, p16. Pub Caxton Publishing Company, London, in 1912.
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/a8/84/3e/ed/534448626fcfa6e1/the_brown_rotary_expansive_engine_pg6_original.jpg?hash=bd3c6c690f1eda3f33c267af925f558a63121aa4e7656fdf6631f7ca634694c5.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine pg6
2 NOTE Interesting research Andrew, so this might be one of the patents that AFG registered to assist in his work as a 'Woollen Manufacturer'? Which your Uncle, Dennis Brown had referenced as being noted on his marriage certificate. This
3 CONC  from extract from Dennis Brown's book 'Brown's of Auchlochan'
2 NOTE Another engine by Brown appeared in 1912; see below on this page. It looked very similar internally to the 1892 model, but it has not so far been confirmed that it is the same man.
3 CONT THE BROWN ENGINE: 1912
3 CONT The Brown design is described in a book dated 1912 as "the last and best attempt on a practical scale". It certainly wasn't the last rotary steam engine, but this does seem to suggest that it enjoyed some success. However, this is
3 CONC  contradicted by a statement at the end of the description saying "...the number of reciprocating parts is great, and it did not come into practical use". This is resumably the same A F G Brown that came up with the Brown Expansiv
3 CONC e Engine in 1891, judging by the similarities of the internals. See above.
3 CONT Note that the cylinder is mostly surrounded by incoming steam and is thus steam-jacketed. Steam-jacketing improves efficiency with normal steam engines by reducing condensation, but I think that would be the least of your problems
3 CONC  here.
3 CONT The Brown rotary steam engine; cross-section.
3 CONT Naming of parts:
3 CONT C cylinder
3 CONT ED exhaust door
3 CONT EP exhaust port
3 CONT P piston
3 CONT PS packing springs
3 CONT S output shaft
3 CONT SP steam port
3 CONT SV steam valve (admission)
3 CONT VS valve shaft (admission)
3 CONT SS spiral springs
3 CONT From The Book of Modern Engines by Rankin Kennedy. Volume 2, p16. Pub Caxton Publishing Company, London, in 1912.
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/c6/85/20/98/534448626fd1354d/the_brown_rotary_expansive_engine_pg5_original.jpg?hash=dd005b25c71bd7c51c1dfabfbff8aef965a361e2a595e3af1c438146e9ebefb0.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine pg5
2 NOTE The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine, US patent 507,780: 1893
3 CONT This is an alternative form of engine given at the end of the 1893 patent; it is very different to those that have gone before. A slipper piece bearing on the eccentric rotor is now mounted in a plunger or piston at the top of the
3 CONC  engine. The part is explicitly referred to as a "piston" in the patent, which leads me to suspect this is some sort of unique hybrid between a rotary engine and a conventional piston engine. The rocking of the slipper piece contr
3 CONC ols the inlet and exhaust ports shown at b and b3. There appears to be no way the steam admission period could be varied for efficient expansive working.
3 CONT From US patent 507,780 dated 31st October 1893
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/f1/bb/d6/83/534448626fd0bc45/the_brown_rotary_expansive_engine_pg4_original.jpg?hash=cecca62ac2ac86a9fcdfe2720af2c4989910f2a21bb209f687473f114cb76407.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine pg4
2 NOTE The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine, US patent 507,780: 1893
3 CONT The internal arrangements of this version are quite different from the previous two patents. The operation is not clear but there is a slipper piece B' which contacts the eccentric rotor over a large area. This may mean that Brown
3 CONC  had given up on trying to seal the line-contacts in his engine. There is another piece b' which presumably oscillates to control either steam inlet or exhaust.
3 CONT From US patent 507,780 12 July 1892
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/7f/e4/6c/88/534448626fc92300/the_brown_rotary_expansive_engine_pg3_original.jpg?hash=5401ee285a09c54b4fc06d7a9e93d99c98f68102fa4176503f5239fb47f9ec26.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine pg3
2 NOTE The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine, US patent 478,843: 1892
3 CONT The general principle is the same but now the rotor is egg-shaped and the swinging abutments have two wings instead of one. Note the spring-loaded packing set into the rotor.
3 CONT Once more there are unsealable line contacts and big bits of metal banging about.
3 CONT From US patent 478,843 dated 12th July 1892
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/cd/95/08/d4/534448626fcf83b6/the_brown_rotary_expansive_engine_pg2_original.jpg?hash=0a725b3c8a70edfca50b3a68ef9ec16e050b974b3a85e8f12c65df7ecb758d5d.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine pg2
2 NOTE The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine, US patent 420,331: 1890
3 CONT A F G Brown took out three US patents for rotary steam engines; No 420,331 in January 1890, No 478,843 in July 1892, and No 507,780 in October 1893. The engine illustrated above is believed to use the design patented in 1892. The 
3 CONC 1892 design was patented in England in 1889 as No 16,913. The 1890 and 1892 engines are very similar but the 1893 design is quite different in its mode of working.
3 CONT Figure 3 shows the internal arrangements of the Brown engine, with a wedge-shaped rotor and two swinging abutments; these appear to act as exhaust valves. The engine does appear to use steam expansively, as billed in the title.
3 CONT Figure 4 is an external view, showing how the admission valves L,L on each side are driven from a small-diameter crank N' on the main shaft. Steam is supplied through the pipe at the top and exhausts through the pipe at bottom rig
3 CONC ht. This valve arrangement is quite different to that in the illustration, which shows continuously rotating inlet valves driven by gearing from the main shaft.
3 CONT In the background of both figures appears a heavy flywheel.
3 CONT There are unsealable line contacts and big bits of metal banging about, which is hard to reconcile with Professor Jamieson's positive report.
3 CONT From US patent 420,331 dated 28th January 1890
1 OBJE
2 FILE https://media.geni.com/p14/a5/a7/c1/d8/534448626fcdd221/the_brown_rotary_expansive_engine_pg1_original.jpg?hash=bd63ac59928a41c19ec0f5a558b2edb166fe693a642936da935c931a03ce6e35.1795075199
2 FORM jpg
2 TITL The Brown Rotary Expansive Engine pg1
2 NOTE This engine was put forward by Alexander Francis Garden Brown of Swindigemuir, Ayrshire. That's Garden, not Gordon. It appeared in the journal Industries, and uniquely, full details of trials run on it were given. The engine was r
3 CONC un at between 560 and 600rpm and yielded between 19 and 22 BHP. The engine was built by Messer John Lang & Sons of Johnstone, and it was at their works that the trials were conducted.
3 CONT The trials were conducted by Professor Jamieson of the Glasgow and West of Scotland Technical College. He said: " ..the gross consumption of steam is only 27.2 lb per IHP-hour, results which surpass not only any other rotary engin
3 CONC e, but equal those of the very best simple expansion, fast speed, no-condensing engines with which I am acquainted or have seen recorded." This seems like a pretty good report; perhaps it was lack of durability that doomed the Bro
3 CONC wn engine, for so far as is currently known it was never heard of again, except at the patent office.
3 CONT From Industries 17 July 1891, p65
3 CONT Unfortunately, Industries did not give any clear details of the internals. We are told that the cylinder was 10.5in diameter with a length of 8.625in, but such laudable precision tells us nothing about how it worked. We learn that
3 CONC  there were "two oscillating valve doors... ...form part of the working surface of the cylinder when they are closed" which suggests an engine something like The Chapman Engine of 1810. It also suggests an engine that would not ke
3 CONC ep working very long, but the Brown engine ran for a good five hours during the trials. Examination of the Brown patents (below) shows that the Industries description was concise but accurate.
3 CONT The steam supply came in through the bifurcated pipe at the top, and was admitted by rotary valves on each side, driven by the gearwheels seen in front of the engine. The cut-off could be adjusted manually or automatically by a go
3 CONC vernor.
