2 NOTE The home in this photo fits the description of Swindridgemuir House and estate, Ayrshire, Scotland. 
3 CONT “Central door in shallow advanced bay approached by broad splayed flight of steps with delicate cast-iron railing. 2 pairs of Ionic columns support entablature. Cill course to ground floor. All windows with moulded architrves and 
3 CONC rosettes, groundfloor windows corniced. 12-pane sashes throughout.”
3 CONT The neat box-like mansion house of circa 1830 is A-listed[4] and is similar in architectural style to Ladyland House and also to Monkcastle House
3 CONT Swindridgemuir House and estate has a Wikipedia page: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swindridgemuir_House_and_estate
3 CONT More photos can be seen: https://canmore.org.uk/site/42033/swindridgemuir 
3 CONT Swindridgemuir House is privately owned, but can be seen on Google maps when in satellite view. You can see that the mansion house is still there, but in street view, you can only see the wooded grounds and gate posts. 
3 CONT Note: A F G Brown called it 'Swindrigemuir' and the spelling on the memorials at Ayr Auld Kirk is 'Swindrigemuir'. This was the address of A F G Brown when he took out three US patents for rotary steam engines; No 420,331 in Janua
3 CONC ry 1890 – 1893.
