1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Mary, who married Nicholas Hutchins of Henrico in 1701, though it is not improbable that Mary was a daughter of Henry Watkins, the elder. However, the record of the  marriage (Minutes of Henrico Meeting, 1699-1756,
2 CONC  p. 12) gives "Nicholas  Hutchins of the County of Henrico and Mary Watkins, daughter of Henry  Watkins," and the first name signed among the witnesses, is that of "Henry  Watkins, Jur."
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.jstor.org/pss/1915104
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT ===Origins
2 CONT
2 CONT Biographical Summary #1:
2 CONT
2 CONT "...One of the most interesting families of Virginia is the distinguished Watkins Family, whose earliest positive identified family is Henry Watkins, born 1637, at Malvern Hills. There were earlier Watkins.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the General Court Records, a Henry Watkins is mentioned 1623, 1624, 1627 as overseer of the plantation of Lady Dale, not far distant from Malvern Hills.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1634, a deed mentions a Henry Watkins owning land in such position that we have a Henry Watkins living in 1634, not more than ten miles from Malvern Hills, where Henry Watkins was born 1637. It has been impossible so far to con
2 CONC nect Henry Watkins, 1637, to the other Watkins of that time.
2 CONT
2 CONT This family distinguished socially and politically has been an object of research for many years.
2 CONT
2 CONT The great Statesman, Henry Clay, was a descendant of this family.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Watkins, senior, who died 1717, did not leave a will. He had previously deeded his land to his sons, William, Joseph, Edward, Henry, and Thomas. Court records show that he had a daughter, Elizabeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henry Watkins, junior, born 1660, in his will 1714, left his small estate to his wife, Mary, and his sons, John, Benjamin, Joseph, Henry, and Stephen.
2 CONT
2 CONT The most we can say is that Mary Watkins Hutchins was a daughter or a grand daughter of Henry Watkins, 1637.
2 CONT
2 CONT SOURCE: "Four Generations of the Family of Strangeman Hutchins and His Wife Elizabeth Cox,” [http://www.seekingmyroots.com/members/files/G002921.pdf PDF] page 4
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT "Hutchins-Hutchens, Descendants of Strangeman Hutchins," pg. 2:
2 CONT
2 CONT "1701-6mo-8 Nicholas Hutchins and Mary Watkins, daughterof Henry, proposed intentions of marriage: 'At a meeting held at William Porter's Jr., the 8th day of ye 6th month, 1701, Nicholas Hutchins and Mary Watkins did at this meeti
2 CONC ng propose their intentions of marriage the first time. Henry Watkins, the father of the young woman consenting thereto and saying he would not be their hindrance. This meeting therefore ordered that William Lead [Ladd] and Benjam
2 CONC in Woodson do inquire into the clearness and conversation of the said Nicholas Hutchins and make return the next month meeting.' (Original Henrico MM Record, 1699-1756, p 11)." (We did not find this item listed in "The Encyclopedi
2 CONC a of American Quaker Genealogy, 1750-1930"; the Criders did list this in their book; they had gone through the Quaker Records personally, as had Rita Hineman Townsend and her husband).
2 CONT
2 CONT "1701-7mo-12 'At a meeting held at William Porter Jrs., the 12 day of ye 7th month of 1701, the parties appointed to inquire into the clearness and conversation of Nicholas Hutchins, report that they have found nothing to the cont
2 CONC rary but that he is clear in each respect. They were therefore suffered to proceed in their intentions and publish themselves the second time, according to the order of Friends.' (Orginal Henrico mm Record, 1699-1756, p 11, and H:
2 CONC 6:184)." (The Criders missed this item).
2 CONT
2 CONT "1701-8mo-9 Nicholas Hutchins, Henrico Co.; m. in Friends Meeting House at a public mtg of the Friends, Mary Watkins, dt Henry, Henrico Co. (H:6:184, Henrico MM Record). From the original Henrico MM Record, 1699-1756, p. 12."
2 CONT
2 CONT "Here followeth a copy of Nicholas Hutchins' marriage Certificate;
2 CONT
2 CONT "WHEREAS Nicholas Hutchins, of the County of Henrico and Mary Watkins, daughter Henry Watkins, of the Same County, have proposed their intentions of marriage before two several meetings of the people, in scorn called Quakers, whic
2 CONC h after the due inquiry of their clearness, and it appearing that the relations of the said Mary were consenting to their marriage, did give consent that the said parties might accomplish their said intentions.
2 CONT
2 CONT "We therefore, whose names are underwritten do certify all whom it may concern, that the said Nicholas Hutchins and the said Mary Watkins did at the meeting house of the aforesaid people in the county aforesaid, the 9th day of the
2 CONC  8th month, 1701, then and there take each other for wife and husband. He, the said Nicholas Hutchins, taking said Mary by the hand and declaring that in the presence of the Lord and before this congregation, 'I take Mary Watkins 
2 CONC to be my lawful wife, promising to be to her a true and loving husband till death.'
2 CONT
2 CONT "And then the said Mary Watkins, then and there declaring 'that I take Nicholas Hutchins to be my husband, promising to be a true and loving husband (meaning wife) till death.'
2 CONT
2 CONT "And for confirmation thereof the said Nicholas and Mary did set their hands.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Signed: Nicholas Hutchins and Mary Hutchins..."
2 CONT
2 CONT There were 26 signatures of persons who were witnesses to the ceremony. Among the 26 witnesses to the ceremony, we found: Henry Watkins, Jr., Thomas Watkins, and Mary Watkins
2 CONT
2 CONT "E. C. Crider noted, 'We have never been able to find proof as to whether Mary who m Nicholas was the dau of Henry Watkins Sr. or of Henry Watkins Jr.' (See Addenda)." (Rita Hineman Townsend).
2 CONT
