1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ===Early Settlement in Salem, Massachusetts
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pride was born about 1590 in England. His wife's name was Edith.[1] They arrived in Massachusetts in the early 1630's.
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pride's name first appears when, as a fisherman, he was granted lands on Juniper Point and Winter Islands. The town records of Salem show that he was given half an acre of land on Winter Harbor by their home and five acres on
2 CONC  "Darbie Fort side" on Mar. 20, 1636/7.
2 CONT
2 CONT === First Recorded Potter in New England
2 CONT
2 CONT John was the first recorded potter in New England where he was registered in Salem as early as 1641.[6] Settlers in New England imported much of what they needed from England, but redware pottery was something they could make for 
2 CONC themselves. Having a strong British redware tradition to draw on, red clay deposits were plentiful, lead for the glaze was readily available and all they needed for equipment was a potter's wheel and a kiln capable of firing at th
2 CONC e relatively low temperature of 1800 degrees.[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Death and Remembrance
2 CONT
2 CONT John Pride of Salem died intestate, and his widow brought in an inventory of his estate, the last, 12m:1647, which was valued at 88 pounds, 16 shillings.[10] The Court ordered distribution to his son, under twenty-one years, £ 8, 
2 CONC and two daughters, under eighteen years, £ 4 each. The mother was to bring them up. House and land bought of Mr. Holgrave security.[11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Pride's Crossing, named for John Pride, is a historic district in an old neighborhood of Beverly, Massachusetts on Hale Street, situated to the north by Beverly Farms and to the south by Beverly Cove. Other locations in Beverly na
2 CONC med for this family are Prides Crossing Railway Station, Prides Crossing Post Office and Prides Beach.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
