1 NOTE {geni:about_me} JOHN CHAPPELL, died in Amelia County, Virginia, in April, 1775, at the age of fifty-five years.  His will, proved on the 27th of that month, recorded in that county.  His eldest son, ROBERT and half-brother WILLIAM
2 CONC  CRAWLEY JR,, were appointed executors, and ARCHER JOHNSON, WILLIAM BOOTH, and WILLIAM MOORE  were the witnesses.  The total value of the estate, exclusive of lands, as fixed by the appraiser's was *800 10 s., a sum equal in our c
2 CONC urrency to $3,874.64.  It must be remebered however, that,  owing to the scarcity of money, the purchasing value of a dollar, previous to the Revolution, was four or five times greater than today.  The following is a 'verbatim' co
2 CONC py of the will.
2 CONT
2 CONT                                                         " WILL OF JONH CHAPPELL"
2 CONT
2 CONT      "In the name of God. Amen.  I, JOHN CHAPPELL of Amelia County, being in good health and perfect Sences do make this my Last Will and Testament in manner and form following. 
2 CONT
2 CONT      "Item.  I leave and lend to my loving wife PRUDENCE CHAPPELL, the plantation I now live on with one-third of my land, to be laid off to her by my executors.  I also lend unto her seven negroes,namely: Sparks, Phillis, Goller,
2 CONC  Abram, Jack, Bob, and Dilly and my stock of every kind with my household furniture, all of which I leave and lend unto her durng her natural life and after her decease I give the above mentioned negros with my stock and household
2 CONC  furniture as followeth.
2 CONT
2 CONT      "Item,  I give unto my son ROBERT CHAPPELL one-third part of my land adjoing the lines of ABRAM GREEN, JOHN POWELL, JOHN OULD, and WILLIAM CRAWLEY.  I also give him one negro by, Anthony, Which he has now in his possession; a
2 CONC ll of whch I give to him and his heirs forever.
2 CONT
2 CONT      "Item.  I given unto my son JOHN CHAPPELL JR. half my Tract of land he now lives on, on Namazene Creek, joining, etc, etc.
2 CONT
2 CONT ROBERT CHAPPELL:  Genealogical notes; Chap. 5, The Mayes Family of Mississippi, pg 60. "ROBERT, son of ROBERT and ELIZA PETERSON-CHAPPELL, was born April 2, 1732, baptz. May 7, 1732. When an old man, probably 80 yrs of age, he exe
2 CONC cuted the following deed of gift, which is of record in Amelia County:
2 CONT
2 CONT         "Amelia County  "Feb'y 17, A.D. 1779:
2 CONT
2 CONT       I ROBERT CHAPPELL, SR., being in a low state of life, but in perfect memory, doth make a free gift unto my beloved daughter, MARTHA MAYES, of the following slaves.  To Wit:  A wench named Mary, Ellick, George and a girl name
2 CONC d Little Mary.  They and their increase to be hers during life, then at her decease to be equally divided among her present children.  "Witnesses:  RICH'D FERGUSON, JOHN COMBS, DRURY MOORE.
2 CONT
2 CONT      On the 28th of October, of the same year [1779] This ROBERT CHAPPELL sold his home, containing 180 acres, to his half-brother WILLIAM CRAWLEY.  The land is described as being in Raleigh Parish, Amelia County.  The considerati
2 CONC on expressed in the deed is *4,000, equal to $20,000.  It must be remembered however, that this was during the darkest days of the Revolution, and the currency of the country was so greatly depreciated as to be of little actual va
2 CONC lue.
2 CONT
2 CONT      No will of ROBERT CHAPPELL is of record in Amelia County.  It is believed that when he sold his home to WILLIAM CRAWLEY, he went to live with his daughter, MARTHA MAYES, in Prince George, and died there.
