1 NAME Edward /Hughes/
2 GIVN Edward
2 SURN Hughes
2 _MARNM Hughes
2 NICK John
2 NICK Edward Hughes
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1650
2 ADDR
3 CITY St. Peters Parish
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1715
2 ADDR
3 STAE Virginia
3 CTRY Colonial America
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Edward Hughes'''. There is no direct evidence he was son of Rice Hughes, but he appears in the same Quaker records as Rice's son Robert. 
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRICO COUNTY, VIRGINIA DEEDS 1677-1705
2 CONT
2 CONT BY BENJAMIN B. WEISIGER III VIRGINIA STATE LIBRARY F232.H5 W444 1996
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 38, 30 March 1689 John Woodson Sr of county and parish of Henrico to William Randolph of same, Gent. for valuable consideration, 400 acre, part of 1800 acres, bounded by Chickahominy (being a quarter built and belonging to sa
2 CONC id Randolph) Boar Swamp . Signed John Woodson. (FAM Note: John Woodson was a Quaker, as was Edward Hughes--their connection follows)
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 39 John Woodson of Henrico County for 1700 lbs tobacco to Benjamin Hatcher of same, 200 acres on south side Chickahominy Swamp. 30 March 1689. Witness John Field, Rachel Hughes. Signed John Woodson 1 April 1689
2 CONT
2 CONT (FAM Note: Hatcher is a name associated with the Stephen Hughes family.  Stephen was a son of Robert, b. c1650.  Robert was a son of Rees Hughes, Sr).
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 109/ 161 James Gerrard of New Kent County, Planter, for 2700 lbs tobacco, to '''Edward Hughes''' of Henrico County, a tract I brought of John Woodson, Sr., 1 August 1697, bounded by Childers, sd Hughes and Horners. Recorded 1
2 CONC  Feb. 1699. Witness by Giles Webb, C. Evans Signed James (I.G.) Garrard
2 CONT
2 CONT COLONIAL WILLS OF HENRICO COUNTY, VIRGINIA 1677-1737 PART 1 STATE LIBRARY OF VIRGINIA F232. H5 W44 1995
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 72/ 169 Will of John Woodson Jr.; Wife Mary Sons: John Woodson Jr, Samuel Woodson and Benjamin Woodson Daughters: Jane, Cousin: John Woodson Sr.Witness '''Edward Hughes''' 1 May 1700 Recorded.
2 CONT
2 CONT >1706/7, 12, 15. Edward Hughes ordered to remove obstacles that caused disturbance or he will be disowned.
2 CONT
2 CONT HENRICO COUNTY, VIRGINIA DEEDS 1737-1750
2 CONT
2 CONT Virginia State Library F232.H5 W446 1995 By Benj. B. Weisigner, Pub. By Iberian Pub. Co 548 Cedar Creek Dr Athens, Ga 30605-3408
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 5/ 1169  .3 May 1742 Israel Winfree of Henrico County, to William Hughes of St Pauls Parish, Hanover County for 50 pds, two tracts of 650 acres on northside of James River on Branch of Tuckahoe Creek called Deep Run, bounded 
2 CONC by James Spears a branch of Chickahominy called Merediths Branch, John Walters, 400 acres; the tract of 250 acres by Abraham Childers, and by him transferred both tracts to sd William Hughes 2 Nov., 1741
2 CONT Witness Thomas Cottrell, Wm, Israel Winfree. Recorded 1st Monday May 1742. (FAM Note: This deed may show a connection between the St Pauls Parish, Hanover Co. Hughes with the Edward Hughes family.  William of Hanover buys land clo
2 CONC se to (within 2 miles) where Edward lived).
2 CONT
2 CONT  Page 94/ 182   May Court 1742 Israel Winfree to William Hughes, deed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 101/250 William Hughes to Thomas Smith, deed. March Court 1743.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 49/ 334. 1 Jan. 1747. Thomas Hughes of County of Henrico to Jon Jude of James City County, for 55 pds land on North side of James River, where sd Hughes now lives. Which he purchased of Daniel Marrow, the deed for which was m
2 CONC ade by Nathaniel Vandenall, Exc of Robert Hardwick, dec'd Wit: Wm Dowsing, Thomas Hughes, Jr. Edward Wills, and Giles Letcher. Signed Thomas Hughes. Recorded 1st Monday, Jan. 1747.
2 CONT
2 CONT Page 49/335. Thomas Hughes and Mary his wife, of County and Parish of Henrico, having right of dower in land which belonged to Thomas Harding, dec'd. In Henrico Parish on a branch of Tuckahoe Creek called Harding Branch, which, Th
2 CONC omas Harding gave his two sons: Thomas Harding and William Harding .Recorded 1st Monday Jan., 1747
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT FAM Notes: The land (500 acres) that William Hughes (wife, Susannah) sold to Martin Burton in 1770 was on/at Deep Bottom in Henrico County.
2 CONT
2 CONT Looking at my DeLorme Map Program, I see there is an area called “Deep Bottom” today  (37.40892585149544, -77.30405330657959). It sits just north of Jones Neck (the large loop in the James River closest to Richmond), also called C
2 CONC urles Neck.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham Childers (1613-1678) lived at Curles (37.37261278657825, -77.28675842285157).  Abraham had a son named Philemon Childers (1657-1717) who, in 1703 made a deed to his  son, Philemon Childers, Jr. of two tracts on the north s
2 CONC ide of the James River where Philemon Jr. then lived.  The land was bounded by William Randolph (of Turkey Island, (37.35978819838075, -77.26221084594726) just east of Curles) and (get ready) EDWARD HUGHES.
2 CONT
2 CONT The area around Deep Bottom was very close to William Porter’s house, where the Henrico Quakers met for many years. Edward Hughes, Robert Hughes, and Rees Hughes, Jr. all attended meetings at William Porter’s house.
2 CONT
