1 NOTE {geni:about_me} === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT
2 CONT 1850 census of Independence Co., TN shows Pleasant, 44, Farmer, and 4 daughters with names and ages. No wife is shown.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Independence County Chronicle, October 1980, shows that Pleasant Turney was a 2nd Lieutenant in Captain Bateman's Company, Independence County's Volunteer Mounted Gunmen of 1836-1837.  He was mustered into Co. B, Mounted Ark V
2 CONC ols. on 8 Oct 1836 at Fayetteville, AR.  Mustered out on 30 March 1836 at Fr. Gibson.  Later he was 1st Lt. Co. E, Ark Battalion & Mtd Rifles, enrolled 26 June 1846 at Batesville.  Mustered out 28 Feb 1847, Ft. Wayne, Cherokee Nat
2 CONC ion.  His name is listed as a member of the Peace Society.  He is mentioned in an article about the First Battalion Six Months Arkansas Union Infantry.  He died with about 150 of his men from disease.  He was a Captain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pleasant was a captain in the First Arkansas (Six Months) Infantry Volunteers, Company C.  he died in Helena on October 3, 1862.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Ref: "First Battalion Six Months Arkansas Union Infantry; From the Report of the adjutant General of the State of Arkansas", by Albert W. Bishop, Adjutant General of Arkansas, 1867
2 CONT www.couchgenweb.com/civilwar/1stbatcd.htm Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000175883078823label=@S93@ Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000175886863825label=@S308@
2 CONT
2 CONT Pleasant migrated to Humboldt, Kansas in the summer of 1860 with Richard E Copeland and Clinton Bradley, who were all from Independence Co. Arkansas and their families had been neighbors since 1830. They show up in Allen Co on Jul
2 CONC y 14, 1860 census and are all back in Independence Co. Arkansas on July 30, 1860 census living with the same families they were living with in 1850 in Independence Co. Ark. They all joined the union army in the 1st Arkansas Infant
2 CONC ry and all subsequently died during the war. 
2 CONT
2 CONT According to testimony of various neighbors in the Estate File #92, Allen Co., Kansas, filed 1866 of Pleasant Turney, he came in the summer of 1860, went back to Arkansas and again came back to Humboldt in 1861, staying until Oct 
2 CONC 31, 1861, when he again returned to Independence Co., Arkansas. Richard Copeland and Clinton Bradley were in the 1st Arkansas Infantry of which Pleasant was the captain and their commanding officer. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Two land patents for Clinton Bradley: one dated 1 June 1861 Woodson Co., KS, for 120 acres in Sec 3, Twp 26, Range 17, was purchased by Pleasant from Bradley and after Pleasant died ended up in the possession of his daughter, Sara
2 CONC h Jane Turney, wife of Joseph Bond alias Joseph Addison Brown. The 2nd patent was in Allen Co. on the Woodson Co. line, dated 10 July, 1861, for 40 acres in Sec 2, Twp 26S, Range 17E. Pleasant also purchased this piece from Clinto
2 CONC n Bradley. Pleasant obtained his own land patent in Woodson Co., Kansas, for 160 acres on 1 June 1861 in Section 34, Twp 25S, Range 17E. This was a total of 320 acres that Pleasant was in possession of at the time of his death. He
2 CONC  had left his property in the care of Sanford Webb by written agreement when he returned to Arkansas in the fall of 1861, planning to return. However, he died on 3 Oct 1862 while serving in the Civil War. After his death and durin
2 CONC g the war in Allen Co, KS, his property was pillaged by the Union Army and whoever it was that had connections in Topeka, accused him of being a confederate officer in Missouri on 17 July 1862 and the district court ordered the co
2 CONC nfiscation of his property, including his land. An order was issued to the Marshall on 18 Aug 1863 to confiscate the property and report to the court on the next term of 12 Oct 1863 in Topeka. The U.S. Marshall did seize what was 
2 CONC left and sold it at public auction in Humboldt. The confiscation report was No 159, in the District Court of the US District Court of Kansas of Dec 4, 1863, and gives an inventory of all they took. After the war his daughter, Sara
2 CONC h Jane Turney, came to Kansas on 28 May 1866 to settle her father's estate. When she arrived she discovered all had been sold and nothing was left and thought the Webb's had embezzled the property. However, she did find out that t
2 CONC he U.S. Government had seized everything under the false pretense that he was a confederate therefore enabling them to confiscate his property. Of the 3 parcels that he owned the 160 acres was bought by the sheriff, Josiah C Redfi
2 CONC eld, and the other two by Jonathan Coleman Burnett the land receiver. She pursued them in federal court to get it back and on 15 April 1871 J. C. Redfield and his wife Hannah executed a quit claim deed on the 160 acres in Woodson 
2 CONC Co. to Joseph and Sarah J Bond.  
2 CONT The land was purchased in 1870 for $1,000 by Sarah from her remaining sisters, Mary B. Turney Bateman, Maria Louella Turney Vaughan, and Josaphine B. Turney Moore Tate. All of Pleasant's property stayed in Sarah's hands until she 
2 CONC gifted it to her nephew, Thomas Jefferson Moore, son of her sister, Josaphine B. Turney Moore Tate in 1901, subject to her life estate. Thomas lived on the farm until Sarah died then moved to Sarah's house at 404 Bridge St in 1910
2 CONC . He then sold the entire farm in 1917. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1860 Humboldt, Allen Co. Kansas July 14
2 CONT  Richard E. Coopland 26 
2 CONT Pleasant Fanely indexed as but is Pleasant Turney 56 Tenn real estate 6000 personal 2875
2 CONT Pleasant Fanely 11 male Ark
2 CONT Henry C. Harpin 22
2 CONT  Clinton L Bradly  21 Ark
2 CONT
