2 CONT
2 CONT Parents of David Epperson:
2 CONT
2 CONT John EPPERSON , Sr. •Birth: 19 Sep 1703 in New Kent Co., VA  •Death: 20 Feb 1737 in New Kent Co., VA
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth MICHHAUX  •Birth: 1707 in New Kent, VA  •Death: AFT 1738 in New Kent, VA
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 1728 in St. Peter's Parrish, New Kent Co., VA
2 CONT
2 CONT Children:
2 CONT
2 CONT # William EPPERSON b: 10 Jul 1729 in New Kent Co., VA
2 CONT # Elizabeth EPPERSON b: 1731 in New Kent Co., VA
2 CONT # DAVID EPPERSON b: 22 Jun 1734 in New Kent Co., VA
2 CONT # John EPPERSON, Jr. b: 13 Aug 1736 in St. Peter's Parrish, New Kent Co., VA
2 CONT
2 CONT It appears John and Elizabeth Epperson, parents of David, both died soon after their last son, John Jr., birth in 1736.  This would have made 'David', Sr. 4-5 years old when his parents died.  
2 CONT
2 CONT David's son, John, was a soldier in the 'Revolutionary War'; both served in the 14th Virginia Regiment.  David Epperson, Jr. died in the 'Revolutionary War'.
2 CONT
2 CONT "David Epperson and his Family of Albemarle County, VA", by Edna Epperson Brinkman, Published 1933
2 CONT
2 CONT Pages 16 & 17
2 CONT
2 CONT The integrity of David Epperson is the same whether he came from England or whether he was born in St. Peter's Parrish in the Virginia Colony.  Either record places him on the far Eastern shore, in that part of Virginia known as "
2 CONC Tidewater".  Twenty-three years later we find him owning a large plantation on one of the Blue Ridge Mountains, which was called "Epperson Mountain".  He had pushed his way through the wilderness and chosen one of the most beautif
2 CONC ul spots in all Virginia for his home, having traveled from the coast or the "Tidewater" section of Virginia up the "James River Territory" to the "Piedmont" country, or foot of the mountains. Here, in 1757, twenty miles southwest
2 CONC  of Charlottesville, "The Heart of Virginia", his son Thompson was born.  Thompson was later to give noble service to his country. 
2 CONT
2 CONT At the time of his death, David Epperson and his children owned a combined acreage of 1,400 acres on the bordering 'Epperson Mountain', St. Anne's Parrish, in Albemarle Co., VA
2 CONT
2 CONT According to family tradition, when David died in December 1799, his family "laid him away in a little burial plot near his home".  The families of his sons, William, John, and Charles , and those of his daughters, Elizabeth, Judi
2 CONC th, Susanna, and Lucy, were living on plantations adjoining his. Thompson sold and moved to North Carolina and then Georgia; James sold and no mention of where he went; and Robert moved to Kentucky.
2 CONT
2 CONT Will of David Epperson  Albemarle County, VA Will Book 4; p. 33. transcribed by Michael Vaughn Sims - May 19, 2002 from a photocopy made by Doris Sketoe - July 1982. 
2 CONT
2 CONT In the name of God, amen. 
2 CONT
2 CONT I David Epperson of Virginia and County Albemarle, being through the mercy of God of sound mind and memory, do make appoint constitute and ordain this my last will and testament and desire it to be received by all as such. Imprimi
2 CONC s my soul I humbly bequeath to God who gave it me. My body I commit to the earth from whence it was taken to be decently buried at the discretion of my Executrix and executors hereafter mentioned. My will is and I positively order
2 CONC  that all my just debts and funeral expenses be paid out of my estate, my will is and I desire that my estate be neither appraised nor sold. Item the 1st I lend unto my beloved wife all my estate real and personal during her natur
2 CONC al life. Item the 2nd I give and bequeath unto my son Charles Epperson his heirs and assigns forever one half of my land so as to include the mansion [sic] spot whereon I now live. Item I give and bequeath unto my son John Epperso
2 CONC n his heirs and assigns forever the other half of my land. Item 3d I give unto my eleven children, viz, Robert, James, Thompson, William, John, and Charles Epperson, Judith Gardner, Elizabeth Proctor, Susanna Langford, Hannah Free
2 CONC man and Lucy Burke [Burch?] to them and their heirs and assigns forever, all my stock of every kind whatsoever and household and [kitchen?] furniture together with the rest of my estate whatsoever not heretofore divided to be equa
2 CONC lly divided among them. Lastly I do nominate and appoint my wife Hannah Epperson Executrix James Epperson and Jonathan Langford Executors of this my last will and testament. In witness whereof I hereunto set my hand and seal this 
2 CONC twenty 2 day of February one thousand seven hundred and ninety nine. 
2 CONT  Signed, sealed and delivered  In the presence of Test.  John Hall  Nathan Hall Candis Hall  David Epperson (Seal) his D mark  At Albemarle October Court 1799 
2 CONT
2 CONT This last will and testament of David Epperson dec'd. was produced unto court and proved by the oathes of John Hall Nathan Hall and Candis Hall witnesses thereto and ordered to be recorded. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "David Epperson and his Family of Albemarle County, VA", by Edna Epperson Brinkman, Published 1933
2 CONT
2 CONT From the 'Bio' of:  Hannah Judith Thompson Epperson:
2 CONT
2 CONT When Hannah left her home on the 'mountain' (Epperson Mountain), she left her dead in the little lot on the hillside overlooking the great beautiful valley toward the East. From the description in the deeds of the property sold at
2 CONC  this time, David's home and grave seem to be a part of the 'Blue Ridge Mountains'.  
2 CONT
2 CONT There is an unmarked stone in "Drowling Creek Church Cemetery" near the little station of Panola, on the Louisville and Nashville Railroad. A cut of this church and cemetery is given herein.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this cemetery, with an unmarked stone, is probably the grave of Charles' mother, Hannah. The family story was that "Hannah' died in Kentucky. Land sold about this time by James Gardner and his wife, Judith, in Madison County, K
2 CONC entucky, would indicate that Hannah's daughter, Judith Epperson Gardner, was in Kentucky at this time with her mother.
2 CONT
