2 CONT Alex Luken found this fascinating and remarkable document-- how I'd like that cherry table! 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Jefferson Co. Bond & Power of Attorney Book 2 
2 CONT
2 CONT September 10th, 1799 
2 CONT
2 CONT I, Nathl Floyd 
2 CONT *am bound by obligation dated Feby. 7th 1797 to William White for the payment of L110, the penalty of the said obligation is L220 which was to have been paid August 1st, next after the date of the said obligation and whereas the s
2 CONC aid William White is bound as my security in an obligation, I gave Samuel Lawrence for delivery of 500 bus. of good sound merchantable Indian Corn between November 15th and December 15th in the present year and as it is out of my 
2 CONC power at this time to pay the obligation of L110 due to said White, without braking up my family on account of greatt scarcity of money & whereas the said White apprehends danger of his suffering in conscience of his securityship 
2 CONC for me to the said Samuel Lawrence and the case of my making him the said White secure in both the aforesaid obligations he is willing to wait further payment on the said L110. For security I have therefore sold unto the said Will
2 CONC iam White the following: 
2 CONT **All the horse and cattle I now own, 
2 CONT **3 feather beds, bedsteads & furniture, 
2 CONT **1 Cherry dining table, 
2 CONT **1 square walnut table, 
2 CONT **1 large trunk, 
2 CONT **1 chest, 
2 CONT **1 waggon, 
2 CONT **Jack Screws and 4 pair Geirs, 
2 CONT **1 barshear plow, 
2 CONT **17 large queensware plates, 
2 CONT **13 breakfast plates, 
2 CONT **1 tureen, 
2 CONT **1 pitcher, 
2 CONT **1 tea and coffeepot, 
2 CONT **two quart bowls, 
2 CONT **2 sugar dishes, 
2 CONT **2 cream jugs, 
2 CONT **3 queensware dishes, 
2 CONT **3 weaters, 
2 CONT **1 pint decanter, 
2 CONT **3 cruetts and quart flowered mug, 
2 CONT **2 looking glasses, 
2 CONT **2 puter dishes, 
2 CONT **2 tin boxes, 
2 CONT **6 flag bottom chairs, 
2 CONT **2 narrow and 1 broad ax, 
2 CONT **1 hand saw & 3 hammers, 
2 CONT **1 frying pan, 
2 CONT **1 cupboard, 
2 CONT **9 head of geese, 
2 CONT **a parcel of flax & cotton, 
2 CONT **9 stocks of blades and the whole and entire crop of Indian Corn made by me at the plantation whereon I now reside and I defend all of the said articles, etc., sold William White against all other claims. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Nathl Floyd 
2 CONT *Sept. 10th, 1799 
2 CONT *Wt: William Hodge, Wiate P. Tuley" 
2 CONT
2 CONT (Editor: Some report that the early Wyatt Powells all spelled their name Wiatt.... this is the Wiatt Preston Tuley in these notes elsewhere... and Wm White must be Mary's father...) 
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage Notes for NATHANIEL FLOYD and HANNAH TULEY:
2 CONT *Courtesy Alex Luken of Louisville: Nathaniel Floyd to Hannah Tuley 25 July 1790. Bond John Sloan. Samuel and Mary Tuley give consent for marriage of their daughter Hannah to Nathaniel Floyd. Witness: Ozwell Phillop (Oswell Philli
2 CONC ps) and John Tuley 
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for MARY WHITE:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Nathaniel Floyd, the youngest brother, who married Mollie Thomas in Louisville in 1793, was a soldier in Thomas Joyes' regiment at the battle of New Orleans," says Kathleen Jennings in her work Louisville's First Families -A SERI
2 CONC ES OF GENEALOGICAL SKETCHES, Published by THE STANDARD PRINTING CO.- Louisville, KY 1920. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Was Mollie a Mary and a White before a Thomas?? Or vice versa? (editor: it seems so. See Tuley's record) 
2 CONT
2 CONT --
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of NATHANIEL FLOYD and HANNAH TULEY is: 
2 CONT *1. 54.	i.	 	SARAH POWELL6 FLOYD, 
2 CONT **b. 1791; 
2 CONT **d. 21 March 1867, Carlisle, Cumberland Co., PA.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of NATHANIEL FLOYD and MARY WHITE are: 
2 CONT *1.  	ii.	 	WOODFORD6 FLOYD [227], 
2 CONT **b. Abt. 1795 [228]; 
2 CONT **d. Abt. May 1824; 
2 CONT **m. MARY MYRTLE [229], 24 February 1824, Jefferson Co., KY [230]; 
2 CONT ***b. Abt. 1795.
2 CONT ****Notes for WOODFORD FLOYD: I believe Woodford Floyd was named for General William Woodford, Revolutionary War hero, who may have also been a neighbor of the Floyds... (Alex Luken, 2002) 
2 CONT ****Notes for MARY MYRTLE: Mary Field was the widow of Reuben Field who had gone on the Lewis and Clark expedition. Reuben died in 1823 or late 1822. She is the sister of William Churchill Myrtle, elsewhere married with the Floyds
2 CONC . Her maiden name was Myrtle, as noted in Reuben Field's will. 
2 CONT ****Marriage Notes for WOODFORD FLOYD and MARY MYRTLE: The wonderful Filson Club Collection has this: Moore, James Francis, d. 1809 and John Jones, d. 1850. Papers, 1782-1872. .33 cu. ft. Papers of pioneer James Francis Moore and 
2 CONC his son-in-law, John Jones, both of Jefferson Co., Ky. Included are land records, 1782-1839, particularly re: the salt works at Mann's Lick; Moore's appointment, 1789, as a colonel in the Jefferson Co. militia; legal papers of Joh
2 CONC n Jones, 1814-1841; and Mary Field's 28 Feb. 1824 marriage contract with Woodford Floyd. 
2 CONT ****James Francis Moore is the owner of Fishpools... (ed.: the site of John Floyd's death.) 
2 CONT *2. 55.	iii.	 	MARY WHITE FLOYD, 
2 CONT **b. Abt. 1800.
2 CONT *3. 56.	iv.	 	ABADIAH DAVIS FLOYD, 
2 CONT **b. Abt. 1802; 
2 CONT **d. 06 August 1886, Louisville, KY.
2 CONT *4. 57.	v.	 	WILLIAM PRESTON FLOYD, 
2 CONT **b. 1805; 
2 CONT **d. Bef. 1860.
2 CONT *5. 	vi.	 	JOHN J. FLOYD, 
2 CONT **b. Abt. 1807 [231].
2 CONT *6. 58.	vii.	 	ANN ELIZA FLOYD, 
2 CONT **b. 1808, KY.
