1 NAME Calvin Davenport /Venable/
2 GIVN Calvin Davenport
2 SURN Venable
2 _MARNM Venable
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Ransom Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 STAE Tennessee
1 BIRT
2 DATE OCT 1815
2 ADDR
3 CITY Allen
3 STAE Kentucky
1 DEAT
2 DATE 28 DEC 1862
2 PLAC Versailles
2 ADDR
3 STAE Tennessee
1 OCCU
1 TITL Colonel
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} U.S., Civil War Soldier Records and Profiles, 1861-1865 View Record  Name Calvin D Venable Rank at enlistment Lieutenant Colonel State Served Tennessee Service Record Commissioned an officer in Company S, Tennessee
2 CONC  5th Infantry Regiment.Promoted to Full Colonel. Sources Index to Compiled Confederate Military Service RecordsField Officers, Regiments & Battalions of CS Army Colonel Venable was seriously wounded at Perryville, and died in Dece
2 CONC mber, 1862; 
2 CONT
2 CONT The regiment was organized at Paris, Henry County, Tennessee, in the Provisional Army of Tennessee, and moved to Camp Brown, Union City, Obion County. Here it was reported on July 31, 1861, with 860 men present, armed with flintlo
2 CONC ck muskets. It was accepted into Confederate service at Camp Brown on August 9, 1861
2 CONT
2 CONT 	1815 Kentucky, USA Death: 	Dec. 28, 1862 Versailles Rutherford County Tennessee, USA
2 CONT
2 CONT Occupation before War: Clerk for Henry County Tennessee County Court  Civil War Career: 1861: Adjutant for 5th Tennessee Infantry Regiment  1861 – 1862: Lt. Colonel of 5th Tennessee Infantry Regiment  1862: Colonel of 5th Tennesse
2 CONC e Infantry Regiment  1862: Wounded during the Battle of Perryville  1862: on Recruiting Duty in Western Tennessee
2 CONT
2 CONT 24th Tennessee Infantry Regiment in Strahl's Brigade
2 CONT
2 CONT While General Braggs Army was camped at Murfreesboro, TN, Dec 1862, Col Venable, together with about six officers including one surgeon, left the army under orders to go to West Tennessee and seek recruits for their command. Col V
2 CONC enable's home was in Henry County and he was a member of the 24th Tennessee Infantry Regiment in Strahl's Brigade. Saturday in the Christmas week, the detail proceeded to Versailles, 12 miles southwest of Murfreesboro, where they 
2 CONC stopped overnight with Richard Ransom's mother, then Elizabeth Snell Ransom Covington. Col Venable complained of not feeling well, suddenly became worse, and died about 10:00 p m that night, presumably of pneumonia or heart attack
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried by his comrades the next day, Sunday, assisted by members of Mrs Covington's family, in what is now known as the Ransom Cemetery in Versailles. Col Venable is believed to be the first to be buried in the cemetery. In
2 CONC  1890, Mrs Covington was in her 81st year and personally seeing that the graveyard received appropriate care as a number of her family members were buried there by that time. A few Negroes were buried near the cemetery but remain 
2 CONC unnamed due to the attitudes of the times. B B Taylor of the Versailles neighborhood placed a stone slab marking Col Venable's grave.
