1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Although it has not been proven, it is probable that Johnathon Unthank was the father of Ewell Venable Unthank.  Johnathon left the Quaker faith in 1800, possibly for marrying a non-Quaker.  It is suspected, but un
2 CONC proven, that his first marriage was to a Venable girl.  A letter dated 6-3-1896 from Stephen Venable of Surrey County, North Carolina to John Milton Unthank (Ewell's oldest son) at Pineville, Kentucky claims that his (Stephen's) f
2 CONC ather Jesse and Ewell Venable Unthank's mother were brother and sister.  Jesse and Stephen Venable can be located in census and marriage records but nothing has been found regarding the possible marriage of Johnathon Unthank to a 
2 CONC Venable.  A letter dated 8-31-1895 written by John Milton Unthank says of Ewell Venable Unthank "He was almost an Irishman, I never knew any of my ancestors before him.  I am his oldest son.  I have heard him say that his father w
2 CONC as a red-mouthed Irishman and that he crossed the ocean in a Sugar Trough and I think he said his father's name was Johnathon Unthank. . . "  It is known that Johnathon Unthank made his home in Surrey County, North Carolina where 
2 CONC the small town of Mt. Airy is located.  Surrey County was formed in 1770 from Rowan County.  It is known that Mt. Airy was named after the nearby plantation of Thomas Perkins, a relative of Johnathon's known wife Sarah Panill Fran
2 CONC klin.  It is thought that Johnathon Unthank was a post rider going towards Fancy Gap, Virginia.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
