1 NOTE {geni:about_me} "Pattie" met and fell in love with her husband-to-be when he visited "Mt Airy" near Boydton, Virginia in the company of his sister, Mildred, and her husband, Joseph C. Hutcheson. She helped her sister-in-law, Mildr
2 CONC ed, in Houston with her ever growing family until her own son, Fontaine, was born. Complications due to the birth of her second child felled her. Her disconsolate husband wrote to his sister-in-law, Alice Love Young, the following
2 CONC  lines on 4/11/1880: "I take but little interest now in doing anything more than make a comfortable living for my babies and leave them enough to keep the wolf from the door when I am gone. All my aspirations above that are subdue
2 CONC d; and if God will but give me the strength of life to discharge my trust and raise Pattie's children properly, and then take me to where she is, I have nothing more to ask of him. Nine tenths of the pleasure of success in busines
2 CONC s, of winning a case, or of anything else was always the pleasure it gave Pattie. Now I care but little, except to press through it, and do the best I can."
