1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Lt. Col. Fontaine Carrington Weems'''
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/6474082/fontaine_carrington-weems
2 CONT
2 CONT Carrington finished high school at the age of 15, and went to work for several years before entering Princeton University; from which he received his Bachelors of Litt. and Masters of Arts in 1907 & 1908 respectively. While there 
2 CONC he became fast friends with Professor Edgar Odell Lovett and later served as the professor's administrative assistant on that gentleman's European tour of universities. Lovett was preparing to become the first President of Rice Un
2 CONC iversity in Houston, Texas, a position recommended for him by then President of Princeton University, Woodrow Wilson (future U.S. President). 
2 CONT
2 CONT After completing the tour, Carrington became a newspaper correspondent and later worked for J.P. Morgan as a financial specialist. During WWI he served as a Lt. Colonel on the General Staff and was assigned to assist in the establ
2 CONC ishment of war reparations since he was known by then President Wilson as a brilliant mathematical genius. 
2 CONT
2 CONT For this service he received the Distinguished Service Medal July 9, 1918 and was made an Officer of the Order of the Crown by the Italian Government. After the war he returned to a banking career, but his true passion was big gam
2 CONC e hunting in Alaska with a principal interest in mountain goats and sheep. While on a 1937 expedition in the lower Baja California in Cajon de Tecomaja, Sierra de la Gigante, he discovered a new species of the Dessert Big Horn She
2 CONC ep named by him as the ''"Ovis Canadensis Weemsi."'' In 1947 at the age of 60 he married his long time acquaintance, Katherine Lane, a noted sculptress from Boston. They lived in several homes to include a dwelling at 825 on 5th A
2 CONC ve. New York City. 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Uncle Canny" was the nick name given to him by his sibling's children.
