0 @N6000000003412783006@ NOTE
1 CONT Col. Thomas Barbour b. 1735, Knox Co., Ky., about 1-l/4 miles east of Barboursville Village, and 1-l/4 miles south of the turnpike, d.1825 Barboursville, Ky., m. Mary Pendleton Thomas of Orange Co., Va., a sister of Catherine Thom
1 CONC as, who married his brother Ambrose Barbour, d. 1825 Barboursville, Ky. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Thomas was a member of House of Burgesses from Orange Co., and signed the non-importation Act of 1769 between this Country and Great Britain, and after the formation of the union, was a member of the Legislature. Thomas was the fa
1 CONC ther of the famous Governor James Barbour and his brother Judge Philip Pendleton Barbour.
0 @N6000000002867139518@ NOTE He was an elder of the Cove Presbyterian Church, which he often  represented in the Presbytery of Hanover.  He presented a petition, with  others, to the General Assemby of Viriginia for relief of the payment of tithes to the Epis
1 CONC copal Church.
1 CONT He was a captain in the Revolutionary war and was at Yorktown with his sons William and Walter.
0 @N6000000002867149434@ NOTE He was the deputy surveyor of Albemarle County in 1784 and was admitted to the Albemarle bar in 1793.  He was a fine lawyer and was a member of  the Virginia Legislature in 1805.  Soon thereafter, he moved with his  family to Hind
1 CONC s County, MS, where he had been appointed judge of the  Mississippi Territory by President Jefferson.  Upon the admission of  Mississippi to the union, he was elected its first United States Senator  in 1817.  He held this office 
1 CONC until 1820, when he resigned to accept the  nomination for Governonr, which office he held from 1822 to 1825.  He  died in 1825 while serving his second term.  Leake County, MS is named in  his honor.
1 CONT From the Richmond Enquirer:
1 CONT Walter Leake was Governor of Mississippi when Lafayette visited that state in 1824.  He was then confined to his bed by a disease of which he  died a year or two after; but sent his aide-de-camp to pay his respects  to the illustr
1 CONC ious French-American.  As soon as his name was mentioned,  Lafayette repeated it: "Walter Leake, I think I remember him.  Is he not  from Virginia?"  He was told that he was. "Well," said Lafayette, "he was  a  deserter during the
1 CONC  Revolutionary War." He then related an incident  which he believed to be authenticated. When Captain Mask Leake was about  to leave home in 1781 to lead his company into war, Walter (then  seventeen years old) insisted on going w
1 CONC ith him, but the old man told him  that he had given up his first born to the cause of his country, and he  was to dedicate himself to the same cause; and that he (Walter) must stay  at home to aid in taking care of his mother and
1 CONC  the little ones. He  accordingly left him. In two days after, the old lady, a true  Revolutionary matron, fitted up Walter and told him to go and fight for  his country, that she would take care of the farm and little ones  herse
1 CONC lf. The boy accordingly went and later participated in the siege of  Yorktown. 0n reaching that point, his father carried him to Lafayette and  stating the circumstances, begged some easy berth for his runaway son,  which was gran
1 CONC ted. "And so," said Lafayette "Walter Leake deserted home to fight for his country."
