0 @N6000000003017896046@ NOTE It is believed that his name is "John" Hughes, although he is usually referred to in books as Trader Hughes, Cavalier Hughes, etc. And someone who comes from a very prominent Virginia family.
1 CONT But so far have not been able to find who his parents were.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following is part of an article from, Amherst Families Trace Bloodlines to Earliest Days of County History. By Jessie Martin, Staff Writer.
1 CONT
1 CONT A census taken in 1761 shows 5,296 whites and 2,750 blacks - which included Indians. Little is known about the Indians who lived in the Piedmont area because they kept only verbal records. Colonists, who did not venture into the B
1 CONC lue Ridge Mountains until the middle of the 1600s, recorded fear of crossing the paths of Indians.
1 CONT To get into Virginia, the Indians had to overcome the physical obstacles of the land - Allegheny and Blue Ridge Mountains, the Valley of Virginia, the Piedmont Plateau and the Coastal Plain.
1 CONT Widespread Disease and social disarray in Indian Villages during the late 1600s wiped out many details of American Indian culture from the Piedmont, or midsection of the state.
1 CONT Historians believe the Indians were hunter-gatherers, meaning they moved throughout the year so as to best use available resources. Through archaeological digs, scientists have discovered the remains of Monacan Indian villages. Th
1 CONC e artifacts help confirm theories the local Monacans lived from above Otter Creek, on the Southwestern edge of the county, to John Lynch's Ferry, at various times of the year. some of the first recorded travel into what is now Amh
1 CONC erst county was written by John Smith, who explored Virginia in the early 1600s and later constructed maps showing five Siouan villages in the Piedmont.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to folklore, one of the first settlers of Amherst County was "Trader" Hughes, a Scottish hunter and fur trader. Hughes was married to a full blooded Indian named Nicketti, supposedly a niece of the famous Indian Chief Po
1 CONC whatan. (It has since been proven that she was the granddaughter of the famous Indian Chief Wahunsonacock Powhatan)
1 CONT Arriving in the colonies in the 1630s, it is uncertain exactly when Hughes and his wife set up a trading post by Otter Creek, about half a mile from the James River. The store, with a stone chimney that later served as a landmark,
1 CONC  also served as a home for the family.
1 CONT Although interracial marriages (white men and Indian women) were not looked favorably upon by aristocrats, they were fairly common in Amherst County, Virginia, where there were not many white women. The marriages also helped trade
1 CONC rs establish their business credibility with the Indians.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Alexander Brown in his 1895 book, "Cabell's and their Kin", Hughes was the first known white man to open a post for Indian trade above "The Falls". He built his cabin deep in the silent forests along the Blue Ridge. H
1 CONC ughes traded with the local Monacan Indians and was accepted by them because of his wife's heritage.
1 CONT The stone chimney attached to the trading post was a well established landmark and was used as a reference point for many surveys done by William Cabell. Part of the chimney remains today.
1 CONT
1 CONT The following comes from Vince Hughes, email bvh@bellsouth.net.
1 CONT "Trader" Hughes was the first permanent settler in Amherst County, Virginia. He and his Indian wife established a trading post on the north side of the James River, west of the Tobacco Row Mountains. He has been the subject of my 
1 CONC research for a number of years and I have been unable to connect him to my direct line of Amherst County Hughes' who settled on the east side of the Tobacco Row Mountains. He is finally entered into this database through his wife'
1 CONC s side. 
1 CONT
1 CONT On November 7, 1999, (Vince Hughes) sought out the old remains of the Trader Hughes cabin and trading post. The location was found just off the trail around Otter Lake at coordinates 37.55627 degrees North by 79.35203 degrees West
1 CONC . The ruins are just off the hiking trail and covered by thick growth. All that remain are the bottom 4 feet of the chimney and a raised earthen outline of the building's foundation.
1 CONT
1 CONT Trader Hughes' grandson, Robert Davis developed a second trading post further down the James River at the mouth of the Pedlar River.
1 CONT
1 CONT "Most histories of Amherst County, Virginia recount the first settler in the area as being an Indian trader known as "Trader Hughes". He, along with his Indian wife, Nicketti, established a trading post on the James River about a 
1 CONC half-mile west of the mouth of Otter Creek. This location was where several Indian paths intersected and near the river access to the "Valley of Virginia"...According to Dr. William Cabell, Hughes had the first stone chimney in th
1 CONC e area, which qualified him as the first permanent settler. 
1 CONT During the period between 1730 and 1750, at least 500 settlers in the county are documented through official documents and personal diaries.
1 CONT One of the settlers who arrived in the county at about that time was Dr. William Cabell, who married Elizabeth Burks, the great granddaughter of Trader Hughes.
0 @N6000000003017894892@ NOTE The following comes from Genealogies of Virginia Families, Volume I, Edmunds Family.
1 CONT
1 CONT Abraham Venable came to Virginia about 1685; his second, wife Elizabeth Lewis, widow of John Hicks or Nicks or Hix or Nix (daughter of Captain Hugh Lewis, and an older sister of Abadiah Lewis who married Robert Davis and were the 
1 CONC parents of Martha Davis, who married Abraham Venable II.
0 @N6000000003017894806@ NOTE Amherst County Deed Book B Page 372-373. This indenture made the fifth day of September in the Eighth year of the reign of our Sovereign Lord George the third by the Grace of God of Great Britain, Frances & Ireland King defender o
1 CONC f the faith and in the year of our Lord Christ one thousand seven hundred and sixty eight, between Robert Davis out of the paternal affection which I have and do beare to my said nephew John Floyd...Tract or parcel of land which i
1 CONC s at this time laid off fore him situate lying and being in the east side of the Wilderness Creek in the county and parish of Amherst. Containing by estimation twenty six acres...hereby given, granted, donated and made over with t
1 CONC heir and every of their appurtenances unto my nephew John Floyd...In Witness whereof I the said Robert Davis have hereunto set my hand and seal the date above mentioned (Signed) Robert Davis
1 CONT Signed Sealed & Delivered in Presence of Jos. Goodwin, William Floyd, Joseph Layne, Wyatt Powell.
1 CONT
1 CONT At a court held for Amherst County at the Court House the fifth day of September 1768. This indenture with the memorandum endorsed from Robert Davis to John Floyd was proved by the oaths of Joseph Goodwin, William Floyd and Joseph
1 CONC  Layne, three of the Witnesses thereto and ordered to be recorded.
1 CONT Teste.
1 CONT Edmd Wilcox, County Recorder
1 CONT
1 CONT William Fleming documented the death of Robert Davis and his son John in a diary he kept during trips between Virginia and Kentucky to serve as magistrate in the latter state. Using Fleming as a reference, Robert Kincaid described
1 CONC  the event as follows in his book "The Wilderness Road" (Kincaid, Robert, The Wilderness Road, Bobbs-Merrill Co., Kingsport, Tennessee, Kentucky, 1947, pages 152, 155-156)
1 CONT Page 152: Dr. Kincaid writes that the winter of 1779-1780 was the worst anyone had ever seen. The streams were frozen several feet deep and the trees were exploding because the sap froze. I assume he is taking this from Colonel Fl
1 CONC eming's diary. "Many families caught in the severe weather on their way to Kentucky perished beside the road. Colonel Flaming learned of a Davis family who stopped to camp on a creek near Rockcastle River. The travelers were maroo
1 CONC ned by rising water. Davis attempted to swim to another camp for help. The ice-filled current swept him downstream. During the night the mother and children froze to death in their fireless camp."
1 CONT Page 155: "Weary from his labors and spent with illness, Colonel Fleming started back along the Wilderness Road for his home early in May 1780. A party of twenty men had gone ahead. He caught up with them on a small stream flowing
1 CONC  into Dick's River near English's Station, a new settlement recently begun three miles east of Crab Orchard".
1 CONT Page 156: "Fleming and his party followed a new road to Skaggs Creek and joined another group of fifteen men. They crossed Rockcastle River and encamped on Raccoon Creek three miles from its mouth. Here they saw the graves of the 
1 CONC Davis family who had perished in the cold in December.
1 CONT They observed the huge rock which sits like a castle on the ridge by Rockcastle River. It is from this immense boulder, still plainly descernible from Highway 25 a few miles south of Livingston, Kentucky, that the river and county
1 CONC  are named. After passing Hazel Patch, where the Boonesborough road came into the trail, the party continued to Raccoon Spring, seven miles south of the present town of London. Here they camped a half mile from the place where two
1 CONC  men had been killed and a Negro taken by Indians a short time before. In the neighborhood of Cumberland Ford, Fleming and his fellow travelers met three white men and a Negro - - the survivors of a party of twelve men from Lexing
1 CONC ton ambushed five miles ahead. In silent marching order, Fleming and his men rode on to the scene. They found the bodies of John and Robert Davis of Amherst, Virginia, lying scalped and mangled on the road. Two war clubs lay by th
1 CONC e bodies and on one of them was a figure of a lizard, which Colonel Fleming believed belonged to Chief Spring Lizard of the Chickamauga. The travelers buried the bodies and continued their journey across Cumberland Gap and camped 
1 CONC near Walker's Creek in Powell Valley.
1 CONT Joined by another man from the scattered Lexington party, Fleming continued up the valley and camped eight miles beyond Martin's Station, then he followed the main road to the Holston settlements, passed Glade Spring, present site
1 CONC  of Jonesville, Virginia. Two more men joined from the defeated Lexington party after he had crossed the Wallins Ridge. On On May 19, Fleming noted in his journal:" (The note in his journal just goes on about the travels, but it i
1 CONC s interesting that he leaves early in May, finds the bodies of John and Robert Davis and the next entry is May 19.)
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Mentioned in the Will of John Floyd (3/4/1794)
1 CONT
1 CONT William Floyd and his wife's brother, Robert Davis, Jr., with their families, emigrated to Kentucky with the first settlers, and finally located in the Bear-Grass region, near Louisville, where the kinsmen (Floyds and Davises) had
1 CONC  a fort, called " Floyd's Station".
