1 CONT
1 CONT Vlgallivan@aol.com BARBOUR FAMILY. "The name of Barbour will long be remembered in Virginia, mainly because of the two Barbours who rose to such high positions in the Government. One became Governor of the State of Virginia, and t
1 CONC he other a Justice of the Supreme Court of the United States. Higher honors than these would be hard to realize. James I m. Miss Taliaferro. James emigrated from Scotland in the 17th Century as a merchant and settled in King and Q
1 CONC ueen Co., or in Gloucester Co., Va." 
1 CONT
1 CONT "See Barbour papers @:www.lexisnexis.com Records of Ante-Bellum Southern Plantations From the Revolution Through the Civil War Series M: Selections from the Virginia Historical Society." 
1 CONT
1 CONT "Full account of Barbour Genealogy in Genealogieal and Historical Notes on Culpeper Co., Va., by Dr. Philip Slaughter." 
1 CONT
1 CONT "According to his great grandson he was a merchant from Scotland who came to Virginia in the 17th Century. Possibly lived in King and Queen County., Va or Gloucester County, Va." 
1 CONT
1 CONT There is record of a James Barbour having served in the Revolutionary War out of Virginia, with the rank of lieutenant in the Continental Army.  He served for 3 years, beginning on 6-21-1783.
0 @N6000000003412782963@ NOTE Biographical Directory of the United States Congress, 1774-2005 <http://www.ancestry.com/search/db.aspx?dbid=1064&enc=1> 
1 CONT about James Barbour 
1 CONT Name:James Barbour Date of Birth:10 Jun 1775 Date of Death:1821 [7 Jun 1842]  Elected Office(s):Senator, Delegate, President  Elected Date(s):1814 [3 Mar 1815]  State:Virginia, District of Columbia, Alabama  Country:United States 
1 CONC of America, England  Relationship:Brother of Philip Pendleton Barbour and Cousin of John Strode Barbour  Biography:(brother of Philip Pendleton Barbour and cousin of John Strode Barbour), a Senator from Virginia; born at ''Frascat
1 CONC i,'' near Gordonsville, Orange County, Va., June 10, 1775; attended the common schools; deputy sheriff of Orange County; studied law; admitted to the bar in 1794 at Orange Court House; served several terms in the Virginia house of
1 CONC  delegates between 1796 and 1812, serving as speaker from 1809 to 1812; Governor of Virginia 1812-1814; elected as an Anti-Democrat and State Rights candidate to the United States Senate in 1814 for the term commencing March 4, 18
1 CONC 15; subsequently elected to fill the vacancy in the term ending March 3, 1815, caused by the death of Richard Brent; reelected in 1821 and served from January 2, 1815, to March 7, 1825, when he resigned to accept a Cabinet portfol
1 CONC io; served as President pro tempore of the Senate during the Fifteenth and Sixteenth Congresses; chairman, Committee on Foreign Relations (Fifteenth, Sixteenth, and Eighteenth Congresses), Committee on the District of Columbia (Se
1 CONC venteenth Congress); appointed Secretary of War by President John Quincy Adams and served from March 7, 1825, to May 26, 1828, when he resigned to accept a diplomatic position; United States Minister to England from May 26, 1828, 
1 CONC to September 23, 1829; chairman of the Whig National Convention in 1839; founder of the Orange County Humane Society, established for the advancement of education; died in Barboursville, Orange County, Va., June 7, 1842; interment
1 CONC  in the family cemetery.|Bibliography: Dictionary of American Biography; Lowery, Charles D. James Barbour, a Jeffersonian Republican. University, AL: University of Alabama Press, 1984.
