0 @N6000000006788800918@ NOTE From  [Broderbund WFT Vol. 4, Ed. 1, Tree #1649]
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Dr. John Woodson was born in the year 1586 in Devonshire, England. He married
1 CONT                 Sarah Winston who was born in the year of 1590, also in Devonshire, England. Dr.
1 CONT                 John Woodson came to Jamestown, Virginia as a surgeon with Sir George Yeardly. The
1 CONT                 young couple embarked on the ship GEORGE, January 29, 1619 and landed in
1 CONT                 Jamestown, Virginia in April 1619. (This was one year before the Pilgrims landed in
1 CONT                 Plymouth, Mass. on the Mayflower.)
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Dr. John Woodson located at Flowerdew Hundred (also called Fleur de Hundred,
1 CONT                 Flour De Hundred, or Piersey's Hundred), which is on the south side of the James
1 CONT                 River some thirty miles above Jamestown, in what is now Prince George County.
1 CONT                 Two Woodson sons were born at Flowerdew Hundred; John born in 1632 and
1 CONT                 Robert born in 1637.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 In 1632, Dr. Woodson was listed as the Surgeon of the Flour De Hundred Colony
1 CONT                 in Virginia. On April 19, 1644, Dr. Woodson was killed in sight of his house by
1 CONT                 Indians, who had called him out apparently to see the sick. After killing him, they
1 CONT                 attacked his home which was successfully defended by his wife and a shoemaker
1 CONT                 named Ligon. Ligon killed seven of the Indians with an old muzzleloading gun eight
1 CONT                 feet long, now one of the prized possessions of the Virginia Historical Society. Mrs.
1 CONT                 Sarah Woodson killed two Indians who came down the chimney; One with boiling
1 CONT                 water and one with a roasting spit. The boys, John and Robert, were concealed
1 CONT                 during the attack under a tub and in a potato pit, respectively.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 The Indians were led by Chief Opechancano, who was the son of Powhaten and had
1 CONT                 killed 300 settlers on April 18, the day before. Opechancano had also led the
1 CONT                 Massacres of 1622 at Martin's Hundred. Several weeks later Opechancano was
1 CONT                 captured by the colonists and executed. The Indians were permanently driven out of
1 CONT                 that part of Virginia as a result of the uprisings of 1644.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Dr. John Woodson is the progenitor of the Woodson Family in America. Among his
1 CONT                 descendants are Dolley Todd Madison, wife of President James Madison and the
1 CONT                 famous outlaw Jesse Woodson James.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Graduated from St. John's College, Oxford, 1604; came to Virginia in the "George",
1 CONT                 1619.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Dr. John Woodson attended Cambridge. Sarah was a Quaker, and rather than make
1 CONT                 her give up her religion, he immigrated with her to the colonies.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Dr. John Woodson's father died in Bristol, England. John was his fourth son.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 "John Woodson came to Virginia in the George, which left England January 29,
1 CONT                 1619, bearing the new Governor, Sir George Yardley, and about one hundred
1 CONT                 passengers" (Genealogies of Virginia Families, From the William and Mary Quarterly
1 CONT                 Historical Magazine, Volume V, Thompson-Yates (and Appendix), Baltimore,
1 CONT                 Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc, 1982).
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Arrived in Virginia on ship "George" in 1619. Survivor of Indian massacre, March
1 CONT                 22, 1622. Killed in Indian massacre, April 18, 1644 within site of his own home,
1 CONT                 (Virginia Genealogist, Vol. 20, 1976, p3-8)
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Flower de Hundred, sometimes called Peirsey's Hundred was on the south side of
1 CONT                 the James River. Curls (or Curles) was a plantation on the north side of the James
1 CONT                 River, above Flower de Hundred. (Genealogies of Virginia Families, From the
1 CONT                 William and Mary Quarterly Historical Magazine, Volume V, Thompson-Yates (and
1 CONT                 Appendix), Baltimore, Genealogical Publishing Co., Inc, 1982)
1 CONT
1 CONT                 The following story was sent by William Stephen Woodson:
1 CONT                 (please excuse any prejudicial remarks)
1 CONT
1 CONT                 "There are many stories told about these Woodson, like the one about Dr. John
1 CONT                 Woodson and his family in April 1644. There was an Indian uprising during which
1 CONT                 the savages made a sudden attack on Fleur de Hundred. Dr. Woodson, returning
1 CONT                 from visiting his patients was killed as he returned home. His wife and two children
1 CONT                 were alone in the house with the exception of an old schoolmaster. Their only
1 CONT                 weapon was a huge old-fashioned gun which the schoolmaster used so effectively
1 CONT                 that at the first fire he killed three Indians and at the second, two. Meanwhile two
1 CONT                 Indians tried to come down the chimney to the house. Mrs. Woodson seized a pot of
1 CONT                 boiling water from the fire and scalded the first; she snatched up the iron spit from the
1 CONT                 fireplace and with it brained the second. The howling savages began to retreat, but
1 CONT                 the schoolmaster fired a last shot, killing two more of the enemy.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Then the mother called the two little boys from their hiding places: the ten-year old
1 CONT                 had been concealed under a large wash tub and the twelve-year old crawled out
1 CONT                 from a hole in which potatoes were stored in winter.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Even today when there is a gathering of Woodsons, a favorite question is, 'Are you a
1 CONT                 wash-tub Woodson or a potato-hole?'
1 CONT
1 CONT                 In the early part of the 16th century, one of Dr. Woodson's ancestors was granted a
1 CONT                 coat of arms by Henry VIII; along with this privilege came the right 'to bear arms.'
1 CONT                 Nothing was said about his wife's right, though!"
1 CONT
1 CONT                 From "Adventurers of Purse and Person":
1 CONT                 JOHN WOODSON and his wife Sarah came to VA, 1619, in the George and
1 CONT                 settled at Flowerdew Hundred, known by Feb. 1624/5, when the muster was taken,
1 CONT                 as Peirsey's Hundred. They had been fellow passengers on the ship with Governor
1 CONT                 Sir George Yeardley and his wife Temperance Flowerdew, Lady Yeardley. No
1 CONT                 further documentary evidence has been found relating to them until 1660. a family
1 CONT                 account written about 1785 by Charles Woodson (1711-~1795), son of Tarleton
1 CONT                 Woodson, however, survives and supplies details which link the first generations of
1 CONT                 Woodsons and Robert Woodson, John Woodson, Senr., and John Woodson, Junr."
1 CONT                 who were among the tithables at Curles, 1679.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Tradition states that John Woodson was killed in the Indian massacre of 18 April
1 CONT                 1644. His children were very young and Mrs. Sarah Woodson soon remarried (2)
1 CONT                 ___ Dunwell, who died leaving her with a daughter Elizabeth, and (3) ___ Johnson.
1 CONT                 As a widow again she left a combination inventory and nuncupative will which was
1 CONT                 recorded 17 Jan 1660/1. This made bequests to John Woodson, Robert Woodson,
1 CONT                 Deborah Woodson (apparently under age) and Elizabeth Dunwell (under age). John
1 CONT                 Woodson was the implied executor.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 The family record of 1785, with no evidence to the contrary presented during two
1 CONT                 centuries, has posited this descent: issue: John, Robert, Deborah, left a cow and a
1 CONT                 feather bed by her mother, not mentioned in the 1785 account.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 "Woodsons and Their Connections", Henry Morton WOODSON, 1915
1 CONT                 excerpts from that book. ---Lorraine (KWDLAD@aol.com)
1 CONT
1 CONT                 "1604-5 1 March, St. Johns, JOHN WOODSONNE; Bristol, gent. f. matriculated
1 CONT                 age 18". Meaning that our Dr. John Woodson graduated from St. Johns College in
1 CONT                 Bristol England in 1604 (before coming to America in 1619).
1 CONT
1 CONT                 "On the 29th day of January, 1619, the Ship 'George' sailed from England and in the
1 CONT                 following April landed at Jamestown, Virginia. This vessel brought the new governor,
1 CONT                 Sir George Yeardley and about one hundred passengers; among whom were Dr.
1 CONT                 John Woodson, of Dorsetshire, and his wife Sara, whom he married in Devonshire.
1 CONT                 Dr. John Woodson came in the capacity of surgeon to a company of soldiers who
1 CONT                 were sent over for the better protection of the colonists; for the Indians about this
1 CONT                 time were scowling and seemed disposed to resent further encroachments of the
1 CONT                 white man. Dr. John Woodson was a man of high character and of great value to the
1 CONT                 young colony. He was born 1586, in Devonshire, England.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Like other young gentlement of his time, he, no doubt had a desire to see the new
1 CONT                 country in which the Virginia Company of London had planted their colony a dozen
1 CONT                 years previously: so at the age of 33 he, with his young wife, Sara, embarked on the
1 CONT                 ship George and landed at Jamestown, Apr. 1619.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Sometime in 1620 a black looking vessel landed at Jamestown, having on board
1 CONT                 about 20 negro captives whom the Dutch skipper had kidnapped somewhere on the
1 CONT                 coast of Africa. These were sold to the colonists as slaves and found to be quite
1 CONT                 profitable in the cultivation of tobacco which was the staple crop at that time.
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Dr. John Woodson, at this time or shortly afterwards, bought six of these Africans
1 CONT                 who were registered in 1623 as part of his household, and simply as Negars, without
1 CONT                 giving them any names."
1 CONT
1 CONT                 Dr. John Woodson located at Fleur de Hundred, or, as it was sometimes called,
1 CONT                 Piersey's Hundred, some 30 miles above Jamestown on the south side of James
1 CONT                 River in what is now Prince George County. He and his wife, and their 6 negro
1 CONT                 slaves were registered at Fleur de Hundred in Feb. 1623. It was, no doubt, at this
1 CONT                 place that their two sons, John and Robert), was born."
