0 @N6000000001612808413@ NOTE
1 CONT Joshua Wynne and Robert Malone (husband of his niece Mary WynneMalone) were paid bounty of 200 pounds to kill wolves. Joshuawas also an Indian interpreter. He inherited his father Robert'splantation. He was a Justice in Charles Ci
1 CONC ty Co., VA, and alsosheriff of Prince George Co., Virginia.
0 @N6000000003398841329@ NOTE [WOMACK.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT It is thought that Joshua Wynne was killed by an Indian.
1 CONT One story about Joshua says that in late August 1711 there was an alarm throughout the James River counties.  Rumor had it that 15 French was ships were entering the River.  The militia officers donned their uniforms, lit their bo
1 CONC nfires, leaped on their horses and dashed to Westover for news.  Major Wynne had his holster at his girdle and an armor bearer that carried his pistol, which made a good figure, but, the ships were English, and everything seemed q
1 CONC uiet again.
1 CONT Joshua was a Justice in Charles City, Feb 23, 1698, member of the House of Burgess, 1702-04, and Sheriff of Prince George County 1705 and 1711.
1 CONT Joshua's father's will provided him with a house and Oatmeal Mill in Canterbury, England and two other houses there.
0 @N6000000000401128155@ NOTE [dwarnerla.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT wed Elizabeth Anderson[1760.A2.4]. She was sister of Matthew An dersonJr. (will dated 25 Feb. 1717/8, proved 19 June 1718) wh o left "one Indian boy" to his sister "Elizabeth Liggon."
0 @N6000000000401128337@ NOTE [dwarnerla.ged]
1 CONT
1 CONT Matthew Anderson [1760.A2.1] (-1718) died in Prince George Count y (will dated 25 Feb. 1717/8- 10 June 1718) naming brothers Wi lliam and James, and sister, Elizabeth Ligon. James Thweatt wa s among the witnesses to his will and b
1 CONC rother James Anderson wa s executor.
0 @N6000000001956326374@ NOTE
1 CONT May have served in the Revolutionary War. One source has a William in the War in Charlotte County, Virginia
0 @N6000000001212180815@ NOTE Notes 2002:  Located the following on: ftp://ftp.rootsweb.com/pub/usgenweb/va/albemarle/deeds/deed0006.txt:
1 CONT
1 CONT Misc. Deeds Albemarle Co., Virginia
1 CONT
1 CONT Dbk C pg 16-17  18 Feb 1754 Benjamin Brown of Fredericksville Par., Louisa
1 CONT Co. to James Harris of Albemarle Co L12 curr.money; 80 acres... James
1 CONT Harrisses corner in Albemarle Co. line... Wm Browns line. Benjamin Brown Wit:
1 CONT Ben Brown Jr., Wm Brown, David Thomson, DAVID EPPERSON. 26 Apr 1754 proved by
1 CONT three of the witnesses.
0 @N6000000001956326060@ NOTE
1 CONT Owned a large plantation  on one of the Blue Ridge Mountains which was called Epperson Mountain.
1 CONT A devout Baptist.
1 CONT “He had pushed his way through the wilderness and chosen one of the most beautiful spots in all Virginia for his home,having traveled from the coast or the Tidewater section of Virginia up the James River territory to the Piedmont
1 CONC  country, or foot of the mountains
1 CONT “His name is on the Dissenters’ Petition of 1776, from Albemarle County, voicing his loyalty to the independent self-governing spirit of the colonist who wanted to be free from the Church of England. When the call came to resist E
1 CONC ngland’s troops, he and his sons were said to have been the first to stand at the crossroads of his parish, probably at Wood’s Gap and the Three Notch road. Later he volunteered his service in the 14th Virginia Regiment. His signa
1 CONC ture is affixed to a record which shows him to have been one of those paying for volunteers who were not financially able to enter service for their country and leave their families without support.
1 CONT At David’s death about 1400 acres, on and bordering Epperson mountain, were owned bu his combined family. (His) will gave all his estate, both real and personal, to his wife, Hannah. His land when  she had finished with it was to 
1 CONC be divided equally between his sons, Charles and John, at which atime his personal property was to be divided equally among all his children.
1 CONT Will of David Epperson 
1 CONT Albemarle County, VA Will Book 4; p. 33.
1 CONT transcribed by Michael Vaughn Sims - May 19, 2002 
1 CONT from a photocopy made by Doris Sketoe - July 1982 
1 CONT
1 CONT In the name of God, amen. 
1 CONT
1 CONT I David Epperson of Virginia and County Albemarle, being through the mercy of God of sound mind and memory, do make appoint constitute and ordain this my last will and testament and desire it to be received by all as such. Imprimi
1 CONC s my soul I humbly bequeath to God who gave it me. My body I commit to the earth from whence it was taken to be decently buried at the discretion of my Executrix and executors hereafter mentioned. My will is and I positively order
1 CONC  that all my just debts and funeral expenses be paid out of my estate, my will is and I desire that my estate be neither appraised nor sold. Item the 1st I lend unto my beloved wife all my estate real and personal during her natur
1 CONC al life. Item the 2nd I give and bequeath unto my son Charles Epperson his heirs and assigns forever one half of my land so as to include the mantion [sic] spot whereon I now live. Item I give and bequeath unto my son John Epperso
1 CONC n his heirs and assigns forever the other half of my land. Item 3d I give unto my eleven children, viz, Robert, James, Thompson, William, John, and Charles Epperson, Judith Gardner, Elizabeth Proctor, Susanna Langford, Hannah Free
1 CONC man and Lucy Burke [Burch?] to them and their heirs and assigns forever, all my stock of every kind whatsoever and household and [kitchen?] furniture together with the rest of my estate whatsoever not heretofore divided to be equa
1 CONC lly divided among them. Lastly I do nominate and appoint my wife Hannah Epperson Executrix James Epperson and Jonathan Langford Executors of this my last will and testament. In witness whereof I hereunto set my hand and seal this 
1 CONC twenty 2 day of February one thousand seven hundred and ninety nine. 
1 CONT Signed, sealed and delivered 
1 CONT In the presence of Test. 
1 CONT John Hall 
1 CONT Nathan Hall
1 CONT Candis Hall 
1 CONT David Epperson (Seal)
1 CONT his D mark 
1 CONT At Albemarle October Court 1799 
1 CONT
1 CONT This last will and testament of David Epperson dec’d. was produced unto court and proved by the oathes of John Hall Nathan Hall and Candis Hall witnesses thereto and ordered to be recorded. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Test. John Nicholas [Clerk? / Cal? / C. C. O?]
1 CONT
1 CONT Pursuant to the above order with under written commissioners did on this tenth day of Sept. 1799 proceed to divide the lands of [rest cut off by photocopy] 
1 CONT
1 CONT Received by bequeath all the property of son Francis Epperson - ca. 1784; viz - one mare, saddle and bridle, one crop of corn, two crops of tobacco 11 See Will of Francis Epperson 
1 CONT Provided lodging for the the circuit riding minister of Saint Anne’s Parish12 
1 CONT 1799 Died - October / December 1799, Saint Anne’s Parish, Albemarle County, VA; buried EPPERSON FAMILY CEMETERY ON EPPERSON MOUNTAIN, Albemarle County, VA
1 CONT At the time of his death, David Epperson and his children ownded a combined acreage of 1,400 acres on and bordering Epperson Mountain, Saint Anne’s Parish, Albemarle County, VA
